  ($q$conv-639$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The museum's new exhibit features sculptures, paintings, and ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["photographing","photographs of which","to photograph","photographs"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The series needs parallel nouns: sculptures, paintings, photographs.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-640$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The lecture was so engaging ______ nobody looked at a phone.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["than","that","what","which"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$'So...that' forms the standard result construction.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-641$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The coach asked ______ players to arrive early.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["all the of","all of","all of the","the all of"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'All of the' is the standard idiomatic phrase before a plural noun with an article.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-642$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The study, along with several earlier ones, ______ that sleep affects memory.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have suggested","suggest","are suggesting","suggests"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The singular subject 'study' takes 'suggests'; the interrupting phrase does not change that.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-643$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$By next June, the city ______ the new library for two years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has operated","had operated","will have operated","operated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The future perfect 'will have operated' fits a future time reference with a duration.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-644$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The explorers, ______ supplies had run low, turned back before reaching the summit.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whose","whom","which","who"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$'Whose' shows possession of the supplies by the explorers.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-645$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the pilot nor the flight attendants ______ the announcement before the turbulence began.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["heard","was hearing","hears","has heard"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The past tense 'heard' fits the sequence, and the verb form does not vary by number here.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-646$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The article's main argument is that cities should invest in public transit, ______ reduces both traffic and emissions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["these","what","which","who"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$'Which' refers to the investment in public transit described earlier in the clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-647$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The conductor raised her baton______ and the orchestra fell silent.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A comma precedes 'and' joining two independent clauses.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-648$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Wanting to improve his score______ Jamal began studying each evening.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ","; ",": ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A comma follows the introductory participial phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-649$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The island has one road______ it circles the coast.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", ",": "," ","; and "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon introduces the explanation of the road's route.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-650$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The senator's proposal______ which would raise the minimum wage, passed by a narrow margin.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ","; ",", "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The nonessential clause beginning with 'which' needs a comma on each side.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-651$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The physicist received two honors that year______ the Nobel Prize and the Copley Medal.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; ",": ",", "," "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon introduces the specific honors after a complete clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-652$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The path was narrow and steep______ nevertheless, the hikers pressed on.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[": "," ",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A semicolon precedes the conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-653$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The editor, who had read the manuscript twice______ asked the author for major revisions.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[" ",": ",", ","; "]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The comma closes the nonessential clause 'who had read the manuscript twice.'$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-654$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The students who studied abroad______ reported greater confidence.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; "," ",", ",": "]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The clause 'who studied abroad' identifies which students and is essential, so no comma is used.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-655$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The new vaccine must be stored at extremely low ______ as a result, clinics in remote areas need specialized freezers to use it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["temperatures,","temperatures","temperatures;","temperatures:"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"As a result" introduces a second independent clause. A semicolon must come before it; a comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-656$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$By the time we arrived, the movie ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["already starts","had already started","has already been starting","will already start"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The movie started before we arrived (also in the past), so the past perfect "had already started" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-657$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The festival's organizers made one change this ______ every performance would be free to the public.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["year;","year, being that","year,","year:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A colon after the complete clause introduces the change it describes.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-658$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Students in the program spend their mornings in the ______ in the afternoon, they work at a local farm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["classroom;","classroom, and in,","classroom,","classroom"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second part is an independent clause, so a semicolon is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-659$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$My grandmother grew up in Lagos, ______ she moved to Houston in 1979.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Nigeria;","Nigeria","Nigeria, she","Nigeria,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"My grandmother grew up in Lagos, Nigeria" and "she moved to Houston in 1979" are both independent clauses, so a semicolon is needed between them. A comma alone would create a comma splice, and no punctuation would create a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-660$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Originally built to carry freight, the narrow canal is now used mostly by kayakers and ______ its towpath has become a popular bike trail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["tour boats,","tour boats","tour boats;","tour boats, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses are joined without a conjunction, so a semicolon is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-661$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The oldest known musical ______ are flutes carved from bird bone about 40,000 years ago.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["instruments,","instruments","instruments:","instruments;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject "The oldest known musical instruments" should not be separated from its verb "are."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-662$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$When the composer finished the symphony, she sent the score to two musicians and asked ______ to play through it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["them","it","they","him or her"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun is the object of "asked" and refers to the plural "musicians," so "them" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-663$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Among the artifacts recovered from the shipwreck ______ a brass astrolabe that sailors once used to navigate by the stars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","have been","are","were"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This sentence is inverted: the subject comes after the verb. The subject is the singular "a brass astrolabe," so the singular verb "was" is needed, even though "artifacts" comes before it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-664$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Born in a small village in Kerala, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the novelist's work often described the region's rivers and monsoons.","the novelist often wrote about the region's rivers and monsoons.","the region's rivers and monsoons often appeared in the novelist's work.","rivers and monsoons were often the subject of the novelist's work."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The modifier "Born in a small village in Kerala" describes a person, so the novelist must come right after the comma. The other options make the rivers, the monsoons, or the work seem to have been born there.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-665$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The city council voted to expand the bus network, arguing that ______ would reduce traffic downtown.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they","the expansion","them","it's"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"They" would wrongly refer to the council, and "it's" means "it is." Naming "the expansion" makes clear what would reduce traffic.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-666$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The Atacama Desert in Chile is so dry that some of its weather stations have never recorded ______ astronomers value the region because its clear skies are ideal for telescopes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["rain, astronomers","rain. Astronomers","rain astronomers","rain, and, astronomers"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses need a strong boundary. A period works; a comma alone creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-667$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Each of the paintings in the gallery's new exhibit ______ a different neighborhood of Lagos.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are depicting","depict","have depicted","depicts"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject "Each" is singular, so it takes the singular verb "depicts," even though "paintings" is plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-668$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Saturn has at least 140 known ______ the largest of them, Titan, is bigger than the planet Mercury.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["moons,","moons","moons;","moons, the"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Saturn has at least 140 known moons" and "the largest of them... is bigger than the planet Mercury" are both independent clauses, so a semicolon is needed to separate them.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-669$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The lighthouse on Pemaquid Point was automated in ______ no keeper has lived there since.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1934, today","1934 today,","1934; today,","1934, today,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses are joined here. A semicolon can separate them; a comma alone creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-670$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The cat licked ______ paw after eating.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","its'","their","it's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Its" is the possessive pronoun. "It's" means "it is," and "its'" is not a word.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-671$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Every one of the students in the advanced chemistry classes ______ required to complete a safety course.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","are","is","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject "Every one" is singular, so the singular verb "is" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-672$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Researchers studying octopuses have documented a surprising ______ the animals sometimes punch nearby fish during cooperative hunts.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["behavior:","behavior","behavior,","behavior, and"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The colon follows a complete clause and introduces an explanation of the "surprising behavior."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-673$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The poet Langston ______ was a leading figure of the Harlem Renaissance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", Hughes,","Hughes,","Hughes","Hughes;"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Langston Hughes" is an essential appositive identifying which poet is meant, so it takes no commas, and the subject can't be separated from the verb "was."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-674$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$If the museum had received the grant, it ______ the new wing two years earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["would open","would have opened","will open","had opened"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A past hypothetical ("If the museum had received...") pairs with "would have" plus a past participle.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-675$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Mount Kilimanjaro's glaciers have shrunk by more than 80 percent since ______ scientists predict they could vanish entirely within a few decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1912, and,","1912; and,","1912, and","1912 and,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A comma before "and" joins the two independent clauses.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-676$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Ms. Okafor gave the award to Tomas and ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["mine","I","me","myself"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The pronoun is an object of the preposition "to," so the object pronoun "me" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-677$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The architect's design for the ______ which features a roof covered in native grasses, won a national award.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["library;","library","library,","library—"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The nonessential clause "which features a roof covered in native grasses" is set off by commas. The comma after "grasses" must be matched by a comma after "library."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-678$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The question the students kept ______ was simple: why do some bridges sway in the wind while others do not?$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["asking;","asking","asking,","asking:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The question the students kept asking" is the subject of "was," so no punctuation separates them.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-679$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$When the power went ______ the hospital's backup generators started automatically.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["out,","out","out:","out;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory dependent clause "When the power went out" must be followed by a comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-680$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The ______ decision to extend library hours was praised by students preparing for exams.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["university's","universitys'","universities'","universities"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$One university made the decision, so the singular possessive "university's" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-681$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The volunteers planted 300 trees along the river, ______ will help prevent erosion of the banks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["which","these","they","and they"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Which" creates a relative clause that refers to planting the trees. "They" or "these" would create a comma splice, and "and they" would mean the volunteers, not the trees, prevent erosion.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-682$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The box of old letters ______ found in the attic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","was","were","are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "box," which is singular, so the singular verb "was" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-683$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The pottery fragments were found near the riverbank; ______ they were sent to a laboratory for dating.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["later;","later","later:","later,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$After the semicolon, the introductory word "later" is followed by a comma before the clause it modifies.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-684$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The students who finished the lab early ______ allowed to begin the next assignment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were,","; were",", were","were"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject "The students who finished the lab early" must not be separated from its verb "were" by any punctuation.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-685$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The ancient city of ______ once a thriving trade hub on the Silk Road, now lies mostly buried under desert sand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Merv","Merv,","Merv:","Merv;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The appositive "once a thriving trade hub on the Silk Road" is nonessential, so it needs a comma on each side.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-686$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The bus was late ______ we missed the start of the film.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["again, so,","again; so,","again, so","again so,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A comma before "so" joins the two independent clauses. No comma is needed after "so."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-687$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Composer Florence Price was the first Black woman to have a symphony performed by a major American ______ the Chicago Symphony Orchestra premiered her Symphony in E Minor in 1933.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["orchestra:","orchestra","orchestra, and,","orchestra,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A colon can join two independent clauses when the second explains or illustrates the first. A comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-688$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Yesterday the orchestra ______ a piece by Duke Ellington.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["performed","performs","is performing","will perform"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Yesterday" indicates the past, so the past-tense "performed" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-689$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Pianist Hazel Scott performed at Carnegie Hall in ______ she was only 22 years old.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1942, when, she","1942;","1942,","1942"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Both parts are independent clauses, so a semicolon is needed between them. A comma alone would create a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-690$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Last summer, Marisol ______ to Peru to study Quechua.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is traveling","travels","will travel","traveled"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Last summer" signals a completed past action, so the past-tense "traveled" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-691$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$After the storm ______ the town's volunteers cleared fallen branches from the streets.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["passed:","passed,","passed—and","passed;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The introductory clause "After the storm passed" needs a comma before the main clause. A semicolon or colon can't follow a dependent clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-692$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The committee considered three possible ______ restoring the old mill, converting it into apartments, or demolishing it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["options;","options, these were","options:","options,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A colon after a complete clause introduces the list of options.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-693$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Right now, the chef ______ the soup for tonight's dinner.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["prepared","had prepared","is preparing","prepare"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Right now" signals an action in progress, so the present progressive "is preparing" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-694$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Some birds migrate thousands of ______ others stay in the same area all year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["miles; meanwhile,","miles, meanwhile,","miles, meanwhile","miles meanwhile"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses are joined by the conjunctive adverb "meanwhile." A semicolon goes before it and a comma after it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-695$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Sculptor Ruth Asawa is best known for her hanging wire ______ she also designed public fountains for San Francisco.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["sculptures, but,","sculptures but,","sculptures; but,","sculptures, but"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A comma before "but" joins the two independent clauses; no punctuation is needed after "but."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-696$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The tour guide told the visitors to keep ______ tickets ready.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["there","they're","its","their"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Their" is the possessive pronoun referring to "visitors." "There" refers to a place and "they're" means "they are."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-697$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The herd of elephants ______ toward the watering hole.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["moves","have moved","are moving","move"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Herd" is a singular collective noun, so it takes the singular verb "moves."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-698$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Novelist Chinua Achebe wrote Things Fall Apart in ______ the book has since been translated into more than 50 languages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1958","1958, it","1958;","1958,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses are joined here, so a semicolon (not a comma) is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-699$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The researchers compared the two ______ growth rates over a ten-year period.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["forests's","forests","forests'","forest's"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The growth rates belong to two forests, so the plural possessive "forests'" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-700$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The museum's newest ______ a wooden canoe carved more than 800 years ago, draws large crowds every weekend.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["exhibit","exhibit:","exhibit;","exhibit,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The appositive "a wooden canoe carved more than 800 years ago" is set off by commas. The closing comma after "ago" requires an opening comma after "exhibit."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-701$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The library is closed on ______ it reopens Monday morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Sunday, it","Sunday,","Sunday","Sunday;"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses must be separated by a semicolon (or a period). A comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-702$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$In 1955, Rosa Parks refused to give up her bus seat, an act that ______ the Montgomery bus boycott.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["helped spark","helps spark","is helping spark","will help spark"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The event took place in 1955, so the past-tense "helped spark" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-703$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Painter Frida Kahlo created about 55 ______ many of them are now held in museums in Mexico City.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["self-portraits","self-portraits, and","self-portraits and,","self-portraits, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A comma plus the coordinating conjunction "and" correctly joins the two independent clauses.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-704$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The documentary, along with several short films by the same director, ______ shown at the festival next week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will be","are going to be","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular "documentary"; the phrase "along with several short films" doesn't make it plural. "Next week" also calls for the future tense.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-705$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Engineer Mae Pierce tested the bridge cables for ______ she found that they could hold twice the expected load.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["months","months and,","months, and","months, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A comma before the coordinating conjunction "and" correctly joins the two independent clauses. Leaving out punctuation creates a run-on, and a comma after "and" is unnecessary.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-706$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The list of ingredients ______ printed on the back of the jar.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","have been","were","are"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular noun "list," so the singular verb "is" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-707$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$In 1888, inventor Granville T. Woods patented a system that let moving trains communicate with railway ______ the system reduced collisions by allowing engineers to know the locations of other trains.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["stations,","stations, and,","stations;","stations"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"In 1888... railway stations" and "the system reduced collisions..." are both independent clauses. A semicolon separates them; a comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-708$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The engineer's new design ______ the weight of the bridge by nearly a third.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reduces","to reduce","reduce","reducing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The singular subject "design" needs a singular finite verb, "reduces."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-709$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Each of the runners ______ a medal at the finish line.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["receives","receive","are receiving","have received"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Each" is singular, so it takes the singular verb "receives."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-710$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The company announced that ______ would open a new factory next year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["them","it","we","they"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The singular noun "company" requires the singular pronoun "it."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-711$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Even though the vote was ______ the council members agreed to revisit the proposal next spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["close,","close","close;","close:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory dependent clause needs a comma before the main clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-712$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Historian Kenji ______ spent a decade translating merchant diaries from the Edo period, argues that ordinary traders shaped Japanese urban culture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Mori, who","Mori; who","Mori who,","Mori who"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause "who spent a decade translating merchant diaries from the Edo period" adds nonessential information about the named historian, so it is set off by commas. The comma after "period" must be matched by a comma after "Mori."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-713$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Neither the coach nor the players ______ expecting the storm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","is","has been","was"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With "neither... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "players," which is plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-714$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The playwright's early ______ though rarely staged today, influenced a generation of writers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["work's,","works,","works'","works's,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence needs the plain plural "works" as the subject, followed by the comma that opens the interrupting phrase "though rarely staged today."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-715$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Walking along the beach at dawn, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["a pod of dolphins was spotted by Amaya just beyond the breakers.","a pod of dolphins appeared to Amaya just beyond the breakers.","Amaya spotted a pod of dolphins just beyond the breakers.","the breakers were where Amaya spotted a pod of dolphins."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory phrase "Walking along the beach at dawn" must be followed by the person doing the walking, Amaya. The other options make the dolphins or the breakers appear to be walking.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-716$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The chef insists that every ingredient ______ sourced from farms within 100 miles of the restaurant.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","be","being","was"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After a verb of demand such as "insists that," the subjunctive base form "be" is used.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-717$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Volunteers counted the birds at the reservoir every morning for a ______ by the end of the study, they had recorded 112 species.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["month, and, by","month;","month","month,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"By the end of the study, they had recorded 112 species" is an independent clause, so a semicolon is needed after "month."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-718$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Many early photographs appear to show unsmiling ______ the long exposure times required by early cameras made holding a smile for minutes nearly impossible.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["subjects the","subjects, and the,","subjects. The","subjects, the"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$These are two independent clauses, so they need a period (or semicolon). A comma creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-719$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The bakery opens at 6:00 ______ most of its bread is sold out by noon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["a.m., but","a.m.; but,","a.m., but,","a.m. but"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A comma before "but" joins the two independent clauses. The other options omit the needed comma or add unnecessary punctuation after "but."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-720$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The bridge's designers, who studied how wind interacts with long structures, ______ the deck shaped like an airplane wing to reduce swaying.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["made","making","to make","having made"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject "The bridge's designers" needs a finite main verb, "made." The other options are nonfinite and leave the sentence incomplete.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-721$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The documentary follows marine biologist Ayana ______ as she studies coral restoration in the Caribbean.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Johnson;","Johnson",", Johnson,","Johnson,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Marine biologist Ayana Johnson" is an essential identifier, so no commas separate the name from the rest of the clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-722$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Both of the ______ owners attended the meeting.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["store's","stores","stores'","storeses"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Both" signals more than one store, and the owners belong to them, so the plural possessive "stores'" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-723$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The number of people who commute by bicycle in the city ______ doubled since 2015.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","has","have","were"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject "The number" is singular, so it takes "has." (Compare "a number of people have...," which is plural.)$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-724$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The hikers reached the summit before noon and ______ lunch while looking out over the valley.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["eating","eat","having eaten","ate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The compound verb must stay parallel with "reached," so the past-tense "ate" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-725$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$When the archaeologists opened the tomb, they found that most of ______ contents had been removed centuries earlier.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they're","it's","their","its"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The pronoun refers to "the tomb," so the singular possessive "its" is needed. "It's" means "it is."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-726$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Kofi and ______ volunteered at the animal shelter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["mine","I","myself","me"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The pronoun is part of the subject, so the subject pronoun "I" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-727$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The author signed copies of ______ new book.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","they're","its","her"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The pronoun refers to one person, "the author," so "her" fits. "Its" is for things and "they're" means "they are."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-728$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$First published in 1925, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["The Great Gatsby sold poorly during its author's lifetime.","poor sales marked The Great Gatsby during its author's lifetime.","its author saw The Great Gatsby sell poorly during his lifetime.","sales of The Great Gatsby were poor during its author's lifetime."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The modifier "First published in 1925" describes the book, so "The Great Gatsby" must come right after the comma. The other options make the author or the sales the thing published in 1925.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-729$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$My brother and his friends ______ building a treehouse.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","was","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The compound subject "My brother and his friends" is plural, so it takes "are."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-730$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Though the forecast called for ______ the afternoon was sunny and warm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["rain:","rain,","rain;","rain"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The introductory dependent clause "Though the forecast called for rain" is followed by a comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-731$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The farmers' market sells honey, ______ and fresh eggs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["jam:","jam","jam,","jam;"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Items in a simple series are separated by commas: "honey, jam, and fresh eggs."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-732$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Most of the water on Earth ______ salty, making it unsuitable for drinking without treatment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","have been","were","is"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Most" takes its number from the noun after "of"; "water" is a noncount noun, so the singular "is" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-733$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The marathon route winds through all five of the city's ______ Queens, Brooklyn, Manhattan, the Bronx, and Staten Island.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["boroughs, these are","boroughs:","boroughs","boroughs;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon introduces a list that names the items referred to in the preceding complete clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-734$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The species of frog that scientists rediscovered in ______ had not been seen for more than 30 years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Ecuador;","Ecuador","Ecuador,","Ecuador—"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject "The species of frog that scientists rediscovered in Ecuador" must not be separated from its verb "had."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-735$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$To reduce food waste, the school cafeteria now ______ leftover meals to a local shelter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["donating","having donated","to donate","donates"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence needs a main verb. "Donating," "to donate," and "having donated" are nonfinite forms that leave the sentence without one.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-736$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Because the trail was ______ the rangers closed it for the rest of the week.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["flooded:","flooded,","flooded","flooded;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$An introductory dependent clause ("Because the trail was flooded") is followed by a comma before the main clause. A semicolon or colon can't follow a dependent clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-737$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Neither the original manuscript nor the early printed editions ______ the final chapter, which scholars believe was added later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has included","is including","include","includes"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$With "neither... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "editions," which is plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-738$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$By next June, the construction crew ______ the new terminal at the airport.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["finished","has finished","had finished","will have finished"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"By next June" points to an action that will be completed before a future time, so the future perfect "will have finished" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-739$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Hummingbirds, which can beat their wings more than 50 times per ______ are the only birds able to fly backward.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["second,","second","second—","second;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The nonessential clause opens with a comma after "Hummingbirds," so it must close with a matching comma before the verb "are."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-740$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The question of how the ancient builders moved such heavy stones ______ for centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; has puzzled historians",", has puzzled historians","has puzzled historians","has puzzled, historians"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The long subject "The question of how the ancient builders moved such heavy stones" must not be separated from its verb "has puzzled," and the verb must not be separated from its object.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-741$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Astronomer Henrietta Swan Leavitt discovered a relationship between a star's brightness and its pulsation ______ her finding allowed scientists to measure distances to faraway galaxies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["period, which, her","period,","period","period;"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both parts are independent clauses, so a semicolon is required to join them.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-742$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The mayor and the city's chief engineer met with residents to explain how ______ plan would protect the neighborhood from flooding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","his or her","there","their"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The plan belongs to both the mayor and the engineer, a plural antecedent, so "their" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-743$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The ______ uniforms were washed before the big game.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["team's","teams'","teams","team"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The uniforms belong to one team, so the singular possessive "team's" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-744$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The mural—painted by a group of local high school ______ covers the entire north wall of the community center.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["students—","students,","students;","students"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The interrupting phrase opens with a dash, so it must close with a matching dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-745$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The flowers in the garden ______ every spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is blooming","blooms","bloom","has bloomed"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "flowers," which is plural, so the plural verb "bloom" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-746$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The results of the survey, which was given to more than 2,000 high school students across the state, ______ that most students get less than eight hours of sleep.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is suggesting","suggest","has suggested","suggests"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is the plural "results," not "survey" or "students" in the intervening phrases, so the plural verb "suggest" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-747$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The chemist wondered whether the reaction would work at room ______ she tested it the next morning.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["temperature so,","temperature, so","temperature; so,","temperature? So"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Wondered whether..." is an indirect question, so it ends in no question mark. A comma before "so" joins the two independent clauses.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-748$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$Every morning, the twins ______ their bikes to school.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["ride","is riding","has ridden","rides"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The plural subject "twins" needs the plural verb "ride."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-749$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The measurements collected by the satellites ______ that sea levels rose faster in the past decade than in the one before.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is showing","has shown","shows","show"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is the plural "measurements," not "satellites," so the plural verb "show" is needed. The present tense also matches the ongoing relevance of the findings.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-750$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The glacier has retreated almost two ______ since 1900.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["miles:","miles;","miles,","miles"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Since 1900" belongs in the same clause as "has retreated almost two miles," so no punctuation is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-751$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The parade starts at ______ it ends at the river two hours later.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["noon and,","noon, and","noon, it","noon, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A comma before "and" joins the two independent clauses. No comma belongs after "and," and a comma alone before "it" would create a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-752$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Drought-resistant crops are becoming more popular among farmers because ______ require less irrigation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["one","it","they","this"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The pronoun refers to the plural "crops," so "they" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-753$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$The tradition of making paper lanterns for the festival ______ back more than 400 years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are dating","dates","have dated","date"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is the singular "tradition," not "lanterns," so the singular verb "dates" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-754$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Each of the twelve jurors ______ asked to keep the details of the case private.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","have been","are"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Each" is singular, so the singular verb "was" is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-755$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Many of the earliest board games, including senet from ancient ______ were connected to religious beliefs about the afterlife.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Egypt—","Egypt;","Egypt,","Egypt"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The phrase "including senet from ancient Egypt" is nonessential and opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma before the verb "were."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-756$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The team needed one more ______ a reliable goalkeeper.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["thing, and","thing, it was","thing;","thing:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A colon after a complete clause introduces the specific item it refers to. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-757$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The ______ nests were built high in the cliffs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["eagle's","eagles's","eagles'","eagles"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The nests belong to more than one eagle, so the plural possessive "eagles'" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-758$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The two scientists disagreed about the cause of the ______ one blamed warming water, and the other blamed pollution.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["die-off and","die-off:","die-off","die-off,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon after the complete clause introduces an explanation of their disagreement. A comma creates a comma splice, and leaving out punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-759$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Several of the town's oldest buildings ______ restored with funds raised by local residents.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Several" is plural, so the plural verb "were" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-760$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Biologist Rachel Carson's book Silent ______ published in 1962, helped launch the modern environmental movement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Spring:","Spring,","Spring","Spring;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase "published in 1962" is nonessential and is set off by a pair of commas. The comma after "1962" must be matched by a comma after "Spring."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-761$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The class read three plays this term: Fences, A Raisin in the ______ and Our Town.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Sun,","Sun","Sun;","Sun:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Titles in a simple list are separated by commas.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-762$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The concert was ______ however, the band played two encores anyway.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["short,","short","short:","short;"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"However" is a conjunctive adverb joining two independent clauses, so a semicolon must come before it. A comma would create a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-763$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The chef ______ trained in Kyoto, specializes in seasonal vegetable dishes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["; who","who,",", who","who"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clause "who trained in Kyoto" is nonessential here: the closing comma after "Kyoto" must be matched by an opening comma before "who." A semicolon can't introduce a relative clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-764$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$A flock of migrating cranes, along with a few geese, ______ stopped at the wetland each October.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","has","are","have"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is the singular collective noun "flock"; "along with a few geese" doesn't change its number. The singular "has" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-765$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$The recipe calls for three kinds of ______ ancho, guajillo, and pasilla.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["chiles;","chiles, and","chiles:","chiles"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A colon introduces a list after a complete clause. A semicolon would need an independent clause after it, and the other options run the list awkwardly into the sentence.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-766$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The scientists ______ their results at the conference next month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["presented","have presented","were presenting","will present"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Next month" refers to the future, so the future-tense "will present" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-767$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$When the students finished the project, ______ presented it to the class.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they","he or she","them","it"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun refers to the plural noun "students," so "they" is needed. "Them" is an object pronoun and can't be the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-768$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The pair of hiking boots ______ on sale.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","were","are","is"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is the singular noun "pair," so it takes "is."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-769$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The city's two ______ sell bread made from local grain.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bakeries'","bakery's","bakerys","bakeries"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence needs a plain plural noun, "bakeries," not a possessive.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-770$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The collection of rare stamps ______ kept in a fireproof safe.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","were","is","are"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is the singular noun "collection," not "stamps," so the singular verb "is" is needed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-771$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$After years of research, the team finally ______ the gene responsible for the plant's resistance to frost.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["identifying","to identify","identified","having identified"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence needs a finite main verb; "identified" provides one. The other options are nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-772$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 1, $q$Lena packed everything she would need for the ______ a tent, a stove, and a water filter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["hike, which","hike;","hike:","hike, these were"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A colon after the complete clause introduces the list of items she packed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-773$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The software update fixed most of the ______ several users, however, still reported that the app crashed when they uploaded photos.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bugs, and, several","bugs;","bugs","bugs,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second part is an independent clause ("several users... still reported"), so a semicolon is needed to separate it from the first.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-774$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 1, $q$The students' science fair ______ held in the gym every year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","have been","are","is"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is the singular noun "fair," so it takes "is."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-775$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The workshop covered several basic ______ measuring, cutting, and sanding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["skills;","skills:","skills, these were","skills, such as:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon after a complete clause introduces the list. A colon should not follow "such as," which is not a complete clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-776$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The tallest trees on Earth, coast ______ can grow more than 350 feet high.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["redwoods;","redwoods:","redwoods,","redwoods"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The appositive "coast redwoods" renames "The tallest trees on Earth" and is set off by commas.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-777$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$Each spring, the river's floodwaters deposit fresh ______ for centuries, farmers in the valley have relied on that soil to grow wheat.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["silt","silt, and","silt.","silt,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"For centuries, farmers... have relied on that soil" is a complete sentence, so a period is needed after "silt."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-778$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 2, $q$The restaurant, which opened in ______ still uses the owner's grandmother's recipe for flatbread.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1961;","1961,","1961—","1961"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The nonessential clause opens with a comma after "restaurant," so it must close with a comma before the verb "still uses."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-779$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 2, $q$Scientists believe that the moon's craters, unlike those on Earth, ______ largely unchanged for billions of years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is remaining","have remained","remains","has remained"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject of the clause is the plural "craters," so it needs the plural verb "have remained."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h1$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Marine ecologist Talia Ferrante had expected warming waters to drive the kelp forests off the coast of Baja California into steady decline ______ her decade of surveys revealed that some stands, shaded by persistent fog banks, were actually expanding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["decline, instead,","decline instead,","decline, instead;","decline; instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Both "Marine ecologist... into steady decline" and "her decade of surveys revealed..." are independent clauses. "Instead" is a conjunctive adverb, so a semicolon must come before it and a comma after it. The other choices create a comma splice or attach "instead" to the wrong clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h2$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The archive's most fragile holdings—letters, ledgers, and a handful of hand-drawn maps from the ______ are kept in a climate-controlled vault that only two conservators may enter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1840s,","1840s;","1840s—","1840s:"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The list of holdings is a nonessential element that interrupts the subject ("holdings") and its verb ("are kept"). Because it opens with a dash, it must close with a matching dash. A comma would mismatch the opening punctuation, and a semicolon or colon cannot separate a subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h3$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The engineers who designed the first generation of earthquake-resistant towers in ______ relied on base isolators, flexible bearings that let a building's foundation shift without transmitting the full force of the shaking upward.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Santiago,","Santiago","Santiago;","Santiago—"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The long subject "The engineers who designed the first generation of earthquake-resistant towers in Santiago" must not be separated from its verb "relied." No punctuation belongs in the blank, even though the subject is long.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h4$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In her study of medieval trade networks, historian Odile Marchetti argues that the decline of the Champagne fairs had less to do with war than with ______ the rise of permanent banking houses in Italian cities made the fairs' traveling money changers obsolete.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["finance:","finance;","finance,","finance, and"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause before the blank is complete, and what follows explains specifically how finance was responsible. A colon correctly introduces such an explanation. A semicolon would not signal that the second clause elaborates on "finance," a comma creates a comma splice, and "and" presents the explanation as a separate, unrelated point.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h5$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novelist ______ debut, a sprawling family saga set in 1920s Lagos, was rejected by eleven publishers, later called the book's rejection "the most useful education I ever received."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Chidinma Okorie whose","Chidinma Okorie; whose","Chidinma Okorie—whose","Chidinma Okorie, whose"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The clause "whose debut... was rejected by eleven publishers" is nonessential information about the novelist and is closed by a comma before "later called." It must therefore open with a comma as well. No punctuation would leave the clause unbalanced, a semicolon cannot introduce a relative clause, and a dash does not match the closing comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h6$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Some astronomers suspect that the faint, reddish object designated 2019-QX4 is not a single body at ______ two icy fragments orbiting so closely that their light blurs together in even the most powerful telescopes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["all, but","all; but","all but","all: but"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Is not a single body at all but two icy fragments" is one clause using the construction "not X but Y." Because "but" here joins two noun phrases rather than two independent clauses, no punctuation should separate them.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h7$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$To reconstruct the diets of Neolithic farmers, archaeologists analyzed three kinds of evidence: charred grains from storage ______ residues absorbed into the walls of cooking pots, some still bearing fingerprints of the potters; and isotopes preserved in human bone.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["pits, sheds, and hearths,","pits, sheds, and hearths;","pits; sheds; and hearths,","pits, sheds, and hearths:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The list after the colon has three items, and the items themselves contain commas ("pits, sheds, and hearths" and "pots, some still bearing..."). To keep the items distinct, they must be separated by semicolons, as the semicolon before "and isotopes" confirms. So the first item must end with a semicolon.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h8$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The glassmakers of Murano guarded their techniques so jealously that, for centuries, artisans were forbidden to leave the ______ who did were sometimes pursued across Europe by agents of the Venetian state.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["island; those","island, those","island those","island, and, those"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Artisans were forbidden to leave the island" and "those who did were sometimes pursued..." are independent clauses. A semicolon correctly joins them. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and, those" incorrectly puts a comma after the conjunction.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h9$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have long known that octopuses can change color in a fraction of a second. What puzzled them was how an animal with only one type of light receptor in its ______ apparently colorblind, could match the hues of its surroundings so precisely.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["eyes and thus","eyes; and thus","eyes: and thus","eyes, and thus"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"And thus apparently colorblind" is a nonessential phrase describing the animal; it is closed by the comma after "colorblind" and must be opened by a comma as well. Without that opening comma, the phrase is unbalanced; a semicolon or colon cannot introduce this modifying phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h10$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The 1911 survey of the Okefenokee Swamp recorded more than two hundred bird ______ the 1990s, fewer than half of those species could be found there during any given breeding season.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["species, by","species by","species; by","species, by:"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence contains two independent clauses: "The 1911 survey... recorded more than two hundred bird species" and "by the 1990s, fewer than half... could be found there." A semicolon joins them. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation makes "by the 1990s" appear to modify the survey.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h11$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Fermented in clay amphorae buried up to their necks in the ______ traditional Georgian wines develop a deep amber color and a tannic bite that surprises drinkers used to lighter white wines.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["earth;","earth,","earth","earth:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence begins with a long introductory participial phrase ("Fermented in clay amphorae buried... in the earth") that modifies "traditional Georgian wines." A comma should separate this introductory phrase from the main clause. A semicolon or colon requires an independent clause before it, and no punctuation produces a confusing run-together.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h12$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Of the forty-two instruments in the Smithsonian's collection made by the Cremonese luthier Nicolò ______ the 1688 viola is considered the best preserved, its varnish still bearing the original amber tint.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Amati,","Amati;","Amati","Amati:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Of the forty-two instruments... made by... Nicolò Amati" is an introductory prepositional phrase that must be set off from the main clause with a comma. A semicolon or colon would require an independent clause before it, and no punctuation would blur where the introductory phrase ends.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h13$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When the bridge's designers tested a scale model in a wind tunnel, they noticed a troubling ______ gusts above a certain speed, the deck began to twist in a slow, rhythmic motion that grew larger with each cycle.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["pattern; in","pattern, in","pattern in","pattern: in"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first clause is complete, and the second clause specifies what the troubling pattern was. A colon is used to introduce such an explanation. A semicolon doesn't signal the explanatory relationship, a comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h14$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Poet and translator Ilse ______ rendered the poems of the Swedish modernists into English for nearly four decades, insisted that a translation should "sound like it was written yesterday, in the language of the reader."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Brandt who","Brandt; who","Brandt, who","Brandt—who"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Who rendered the poems... for nearly four decades" is a nonessential clause about Brandt, and it closes with a comma before "insisted." It must open with a matching comma. A dash would not match the closing comma, a semicolon cannot introduce a relative clause, and omitting punctuation leaves the clause unbalanced.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h15$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Unlike most social insects, which build nests and defend a fixed ______ army ants are nomadic, moving their entire colony—sometimes more than a million individuals—to a new bivouac site almost every night.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["territory;","territory,","territory","territory—"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase "Unlike most social insects, which build nests and defend a fixed territory" is introductory and must be followed by a comma before the main clause ("army ants are nomadic"). A semicolon or dash is not used to end an introductory phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h16$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The study's authors were careful to note a limitation: because all of the participants were recruited from a single ______ results may not apply to people who grew up in rural areas.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["city, the","city; the","city: the","city. The"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Because all of the participants were recruited from a single city" is a dependent clause introducing the main clause "the results may not apply...." A comma correctly separates them. A semicolon, colon, or period would leave the dependent clause standing alone as if it were a complete sentence.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h17$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Hoping to understand why some languages have dozens of words for kinship relations while others have only a ______ linguists compared vocabularies from more than four hundred societies.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["few;","few","few:","few,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Hoping to understand why... while others have only a few" is a long introductory participial phrase modifying "linguists." It should end with a comma. A semicolon or colon requires a complete clause before it, and no punctuation runs the phrase into the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h18$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The violinist's 1952 recording of the partitas is prized by collectors for a strange ______ in the third movement, a passing truck can be heard rumbling beneath the music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reason, in","reason; in","reason: in","reason in"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The violinist's 1952 recording... is prized by collectors for a strange reason" is independent, and the following clause explains the reason. A colon introduces that explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, a semicolon fails to signal the explanation, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h19$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Engineers at the plant considered two options for cooling the ______ water drawn from the nearby river or a closed loop of recirculated coolant—before settling on a hybrid of both.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reactor, fresh","reactor—fresh","reactor: fresh","reactor; fresh"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase listing the two options is nonessential and closes with a dash after "coolant." It must therefore open with a matching dash. A comma or colon would not match the closing dash, and a semicolon cannot introduce a phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h20$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mathematician spent years trying to prove the ______ she discovered, to her dismay, that a colleague had published a counterexample the previous spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["conjecture; then","conjecture, then","conjecture then","conjecture, then;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses are joined here: "The mathematician spent years trying to prove the conjecture" and "then she discovered...." "Then" is an adverb, not a coordinating conjunction, so a comma alone would create a comma splice. A semicolon correctly joins the clauses.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h21$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Bioluminescent fungi, which glow a pale green in the dark forests of Brazil and ______ are thought to attract insects that carry their spores to new logs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Japan;","Japan","Japan—","Japan,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The relative clause "which glow a pale green... in the dark forests of Brazil and Japan" is nonessential and opens with a comma after "fungi." It must close with a comma before the verb "are thought." A semicolon or dash would not match, and no punctuation leaves the clause unbalanced.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h22$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Greek astronomer Aristarchus proposed that Earth revolves around the Sun nearly two millennia before Copernicus did. His idea gained few ______ the prevailing view, endorsed by Aristotle, held that a moving Earth would fling objects off its surface.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["followers; however,","followers, however,","followers, however;","followers however"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"His idea gained few followers, however" is a complete clause in which "however" contrasts with the previous sentence; it is followed by a second independent clause that explains why. The semicolon must come after "however" to join the two clauses. Placing it before "however" would wrongly attach the contrast to the explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h23$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Among the most celebrated works of the Harlem Renaissance is the 1926 collection by the poet ______ The Weary Blues, which drew on the rhythms of jazz and the blues to depict everyday Black life in the urban North.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Langston Hughes","Langston Hughes,","Langston Hughes;","Langston Hughes, and"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The Weary Blues" renames the noun phrase "the 1926 collection by the poet Langston Hughes," and it is followed by the nonessential clause "which drew on...." Because the title is an appositive that adds information about a collection already identified (the poet's 1926 collection), it is set off by a comma. A semicolon cannot introduce an appositive, no punctuation would fuse the poet's name with the book title, and "and" would wrongly turn the title into a second, separate work.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h24$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The documentary's director ______ spent three winters filming in the Arctic, says that the hardest part of the project was not the cold but the months of total darkness.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Marit Solberg, who","Marit Solberg who","Marit Solberg; who","Marit Solberg—who"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Who spent three winters filming in the Arctic" is a nonessential clause identifying more about the director, already named. It closes with a comma before "says," so it must open with a comma. A dash would not match the closing comma, and a semicolon cannot introduce a relative clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h25$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers found that bumblebees trained to associate a reward with a particular shape could later identify that shape by touch ______ suggests that bees form something like a mental image that combines information from different senses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["alone; a result that","alone. A result that","alone, a result, that","alone, a result that"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "a result that suggests..." is a noun phrase summing up the preceding clause, so it attaches with a comma. A semicolon or period would leave "a result that suggests..." as a fragment, and the comma after "result" wrongly separates the noun from its restrictive clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h26$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$To keep the reconstruction faithful to the original, the restorers used only materials available in the fourteenth ______ lime mortar, hand-split oak beams, and clay tiles fired in a wood kiln.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["century;","century, such as:","century:","century"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clause before the blank is complete, and a list of the specific materials follows. A colon introduces the list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, "such as:" incorrectly places a colon after "such as," and no punctuation runs the list into the sentence.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h27$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Even after the patent expired, few manufacturers attempted to copy the ______ precision machining it required was beyond the capabilities of most factories at the time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["device, the","device; the","device the","device, and, the"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Few manufacturers attempted to copy the device" and "the precision machining it required was beyond..." are both independent clauses. A semicolon joins them correctly. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and," with a comma after the conjunction is incorrect.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h28$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mural's central ______ woman holding a lantern above a flooded street—was inspired by a photograph the artist found in her grandmother's attic.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["figure—a","figure, a","figure: a","figure a"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The appositive "a woman holding a lantern above a flooded street" is closed with a dash before "was inspired," so it must open with a matching dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash, and no punctuation leaves the dash unpaired.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h29$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many of the town's earliest records were lost in the flood of ______ the minutes of every council meeting held between 1890 and 1902 survived because a clerk had taken them home to copy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1903, remarkably, however,","1903; remarkably however","1903, remarkably; however,","1903; remarkably, however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first independent clause ends at "1903," and a second independent clause begins. A semicolon must join them. "Remarkably" and "however" are both interrupting adverbs at the start of the second clause, so each is followed by a comma. Only the correct answer combines the semicolon with correctly punctuated adverbs.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h30$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Although the composer wrote more than three hundred works over his long career, he is remembered today almost entirely for a single ______ a brief, haunting setting of a medieval lullaby for unaccompanied choir.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["piece;","piece, that is","piece:","piece. That is"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The main clause is complete, and what follows identifies the single piece. A colon introduces this explanation. A semicolon must be followed by an independent clause, and "that is" would need to be followed by a comma (and the period choice creates a fragment).$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h31$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In the 1970s, the chemist Mario Molina and his colleague F. Sherwood Rowland warned that chlorofluorocarbons, or ______ common in aerosol sprays and refrigerants, could destroy the ozone layer that shields Earth from ultraviolet radiation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["CFCs then","CFCs, then","CFCs; then","CFCs—then"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Then common in aerosol sprays and refrigerants" is a nonessential phrase describing chlorofluorocarbons; it closes with a comma before "could destroy." The phrase must therefore open with a comma. A dash would not match, and a semicolon cannot introduce a phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h32$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novel opens with a list of ______ a red bicycle, a drawer full of keys to locks that no longer exist, and a letter that was never mailed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["objects:","objects;","objects, including:","objects that are:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The independent clause "The novel opens with a list of objects" is followed by the items of the list. A colon correctly introduces them. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it, and a colon should not follow a word like "including" or a verb like "are" that already leads grammatically into the list.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h33$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The tunnel's ______ by a team of nineteenth-century surveyors working with little more than chains, compasses, and candlelight—remained accurate to within a few inches over its entire two-mile length.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["alignment, calculated","alignment: calculated","alignment calculated","alignment—calculated"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "calculated by a team... and candlelight" is a nonessential interruption between the subject "alignment" and the verb "remained." It closes with a dash, so it must open with a dash. A comma would not match the closing dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h34$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The survey asked respondents to rank five ______ affordability, commute time, school quality, access to parks, and proximity to family.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["priorities;","priorities, which were:","priorities:","priorities which were"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The survey asked respondents to rank five priorities" is an independent clause, and the list that follows identifies the priorities. A colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, and "which were:" puts a colon after a verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h35$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Seismologists have identified a subtle signal that sometimes precedes large ______ slow, almost imperceptible slipping along a fault that can last for weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["earthquakes;","earthquakes:","earthquakes, and","earthquakes"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause before the blank is followed by a description of the signal. A colon introduces this explanation. A semicolon would require an independent clause after it, "and" would wrongly present the slipping as something the signal precedes, and no punctuation runs the explanation into "earthquakes."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h36$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The city's first public ______ in 1848 in a converted warehouse near the harbor—held fewer than two thousand volumes, most of them donated by local merchants.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["library—opened","library, opened","library; opened","library opened"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase "opened in 1848 in a converted warehouse near the harbor" interrupts the subject and verb and is closed with a dash. It must open with a dash. A comma would mismatch the closing dash, and a semicolon cannot set off a phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h37$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Ultramarine, a pigment made from ground lapis lazuli, was once more valuable than gold; Renaissance contracts sometimes specified exactly how much of it a painter was required to ______ in the robes of the Virgin Mary.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["use; usually","use: usually,","use. Usually","use, usually"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Usually in the robes of the Virgin Mary" is a modifying phrase that adds information to the clause ending "required to use." A comma attaches it. A semicolon or period would make the phrase a fragment, and the colon option adds an unnecessary comma after "usually."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h38$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The professor's lecture notes, which were later published as a ______ the basis for the first undergraduate course in ecology offered at the university.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["textbook became","textbook; became","textbook, became","textbook—became"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The relative clause "which were later published as a textbook" is nonessential and begins with a comma after "notes." It must end with a comma before the main verb "became." No punctuation, a semicolon, or a dash would fail to close the clause properly.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h39$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When its channel to the North Sea silted up in the fifteenth century, the port of Bruges was cut off from the ______ city's fortunes declined steadily as trade moved to Antwerp.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["sea, the","sea, and the","sea and, the","sea the"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses ("the port... was cut off from the sea" and "the city's fortunes declined steadily") must be joined properly. A comma followed by the coordinating conjunction "and" is correct. A comma alone is a comma splice, the comma after "and" is misplaced, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h40$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many species of ______ the tiny Kirk's dik-dik of eastern Africa, mate for life and defend small territories as a pair.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["antelope, including","antelope including","antelope; including","antelope: including"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Including the tiny Kirk's dik-dik of eastern Africa" is a nonessential phrase, closed by the comma before "mate." It must be opened by a comma. A semicolon or colon cannot introduce this phrase, and no punctuation leaves it unbalanced.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h41$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The volunteers were divided into three groups: those who received the vaccine at the start of the ______ who received it six weeks later; and those who received a placebo.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["study, those","study: those","study those","study; those"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The list after the colon contains items with internal punctuation, and the second and third items are separated with a semicolon ("later; and those"). For consistency, the first and second items must also be separated with a semicolon.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h42$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Refined during the Second World War to detect approaching ______ radar was soon adapted by meteorologists, who realized that its signals also bounced off raindrops.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["aircraft;","aircraft","aircraft,","aircraft—"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory participial phrase "Refined during the Second World War to detect approaching aircraft" must be separated from the main clause with a comma. A semicolon or dash cannot end an introductory phrase, and no punctuation would make "aircraft radar" read as one phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h43$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The architect's guiding principle was ______ "A building should look as though it grew from the ground it stands on."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["simple;","simple:","simple","simple, that"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The architect's guiding principle was simple" is an independent clause, and the quotation that follows states the principle. A colon introduces such an explanatory quotation. A semicolon would not signal the relationship, no punctuation runs the clause into the quotation, and "that" would create a grammatically awkward construction with the capitalized quotation.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h44$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Neither the original blueprints nor the contractor's ______ any mention of the hidden room that workers discovered behind the library's fireplace in 2014.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["notes contain","notes, contain","notes; contain","notes—contain"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Neither the original blueprints nor the contractor's notes" is the compound subject, and "contain" is its verb. No punctuation should separate a subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h45$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The engineers knew the dam could withstand a hundred-year ______ uncertain was whether it could survive the kind of once-in-a-millennium storm that climate models now predict will become more common.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["flood, what remained","flood what remained","flood: what, remained","flood; what remained"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first clause is independent, and "what remained uncertain was whether..." is also an independent clause (its subject is the noun clause "what remained uncertain"). A semicolon joins them. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "flood: what, remained" inserts a comma inside the noun clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h46$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$A single teaspoon of healthy topsoil can contain more microorganisms than there are people on ______ bacteria, fungi, protozoa, and nematodes, all interacting in ways scientists are only beginning to understand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Earth;","Earth, these are","Earth—","Earth"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The material after the blank is a list explaining what the microorganisms are. A dash can introduce such an explanatory list at the end of a sentence. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it, "these are" after a comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation runs the list into "Earth."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h47$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The poet Gwendolyn Brooks, whose 1949 collection Annie Allen made her the first Black author to win a Pulitzer ______ much of her life in the Bronzeville neighborhood of Chicago.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Prize spent","Prize, spent","Prize; spent","Prize—spent"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The relative clause "whose 1949 collection... to win a Pulitzer Prize" is nonessential and opens with a comma after "Brooks." It must close with a comma before the main verb "spent."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h48$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The excavation uncovered thousands of fragments of ______ which were painted with scenes from daily life, including fishing, weaving, and the brewing of beer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["pottery, many of","pottery many of","pottery; many of","pottery. Many of"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Many of which were painted..." is a relative clause providing nonessential information about the pottery fragments, so it is set off by a comma. A semicolon or period would leave "many of which..." as a fragment, and no punctuation fails to separate the nonessential clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h49$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The algorithm performed well on the test ______ when researchers applied it to images from hospitals in other countries, its accuracy fell sharply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["data, however,","data however;","data, however","data; however,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"The algorithm performed well on the test data" and "when researchers applied it... its accuracy fell sharply" are independent clauses. The conjunctive adverb "however" introduces the second, so a semicolon goes before it and a comma after. The other choices create comma splices or put the semicolon in the wrong place.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h50$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Most of the expedition's ______ the cameras, the satellite phone, and the solar chargers—failed within a week in the humidity of the cloud forest.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["electronics,","electronics:","electronics—","electronics"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The list "the cameras, the satellite phone, and the solar chargers" interrupts the subject and its verb "failed." Because the list contains commas and is closed by a dash, it must be opened by a dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h51$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The glacier has retreated nearly two miles since ______ a rate of loss that has accelerated sharply in the past two decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1900;","1900,","1900, and","1900."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"A rate of loss that has accelerated sharply" is a noun phrase that sums up the preceding clause; it is attached with a comma. A semicolon or period would turn it into a fragment, and "and" would leave the noun phrase without a verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h52$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The hummingbird's heart can beat more than 1,200 times per minute during ______ night, however, it can slow to fewer than 50 beats per minute as the bird enters a hibernation-like state called torpor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["flight; at","flight, at","flight at","flight: at"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The hummingbird's heart can beat... during flight" and "at night, however, it can slow..." are two independent clauses. They must be joined with a semicolon (the "however" is set off within the second clause). A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h53$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The town's annual festival, first held in ______ fewer than two hundred visitors; today, it draws more than forty thousand.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1958 attracted","1958; attracted","1958—attracted","1958, attracted"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"First held in 1958" is a nonessential phrase between the subject "festival" and its verb "attracted," and it opens with a comma. It must close with a comma. A dash would not match, a semicolon cannot separate a subject and verb, and no punctuation leaves the phrase unbalanced.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h54$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Only one of the three proposed sites met all of the ______ it was at least fifty miles from the nearest city, sat on stable bedrock, and had access to a reliable water supply.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["criteria,","criteria","criteria:","criteria, and"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second clause explains how the site met all of the criteria. A colon introduces such an explanation after an independent clause. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and" fails to convey that the second clause specifies the criteria.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h55$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The bridge, completed in 1937 after four years of ______ the longest suspension span in the world for nearly three decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["construction remained","construction, remained","construction; remained","construction: remained"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Completed in 1937 after four years of construction" is a nonessential phrase that opens with a comma after "bridge." It must close with a comma before the verb "remained." A semicolon or colon cannot separate the subject and verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h56$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Few people today realize that the color ______ was originally the name of a fruit and only later came to describe a hue.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["orange","orange,","orange—","\"orange,\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The color orange" uses "orange" as a restrictive appositive that identifies which color is meant; restrictive appositives are not set off with punctuation. Adding a comma or dash would separate the subject from its verb "was."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h57$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The organizers expected roughly five hundred attendees at the ______ than three thousand people arrived, forcing the event to move outdoors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["conference, instead, more","conference instead, more","conference; instead more,","conference; instead, more"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses are joined here. "Instead" is a conjunctive adverb that begins the second clause, so a semicolon precedes it and a comma follows it. The other choices create a comma splice, a run-on, or misplace the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h58$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The traditional dish requires three ______ that have soaked overnight, slow-roasted tomatoes, and a broth simmered for at least six hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["components; chickpeas","components, chickpeas","components: chickpeas","components chickpeas"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The traditional dish requires three components" is an independent clause introducing a list, so a colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, a comma would make "chickpeas" read like an appositive for only one item, and no punctuation runs the list into the clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h59$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Scientists studying the ______ a 3.4-billion-year-old rock formation in Western Australia—have found microscopic structures they believe are the fossilized remains of some of Earth's earliest life.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Strelley Pool chert,","Strelley Pool chert—","Strelley Pool chert:","Strelley Pool chert"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The appositive "a 3.4-billion-year-old rock formation in Western Australia" is closed by a dash before "have found." It must be opened by a dash as well. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash, and no punctuation leaves the dash unpaired.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h60$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Because the manuscript had been copied by hand dozens of times before the printing press was ______ its surviving versions differ from one another in hundreds of small ways.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["invented,","invented;","invented","invented:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Because the manuscript had been copied... before the printing press was invented" is a dependent clause beginning the sentence. It must be separated from the main clause with a comma. A semicolon or colon cannot follow a dependent clause, and no punctuation creates a confusing run-together.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h61$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The sculptor worked in an unusual ______ salvaged from shipwrecks along the Outer Banks of North Carolina.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["medium; timber","medium, timber, that was","medium timber","medium: timber"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The clause "The sculptor worked in an unusual medium" is complete, and what follows identifies the medium. A colon introduces this explanation. A semicolon requires an independent clause after it, "medium, timber, that was" adds unnecessary words and commas, and no punctuation runs the explanation into the noun.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h62$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The team's analysis of tree rings, ice cores, and lake ______ together provide a record of rainfall stretching back more than two thousand years—suggests that the region's current drought is the most severe in at least twelve centuries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["sediments, which","sediments; which","sediments—which","sediments which"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The relative clause "which together provide a record of rainfall..." is closed by a dash before the verb "suggests," so it must be opened by a dash. A comma would not match the closing dash, and a semicolon cannot introduce a relative clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h63$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The new law did not ban the pesticide ______ it required farmers to keep detailed records of when and where they applied it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["outright, rather,","outright; rather,","outright rather","outright; rather"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The new law did not ban the pesticide outright" and "it required farmers to keep detailed records..." are independent clauses. "Rather" is a conjunctive adverb introducing the second clause, so it is preceded by a semicolon and followed by a comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h64$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The photograph shows the inventor standing beside her ______ prototype she built in her garage in 1923 using bicycle parts and a sewing-machine motor.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["creation, a","creation; a","creation. A","creation: a,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"A prototype she built in her garage..." is an appositive noun phrase renaming "creation." A comma sets it off. A semicolon or period would leave the appositive as a fragment, and the colon choice adds a stray comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h65$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The committee's final report recommended three changes to the city's water ______ replacing lead service lines in older neighborhoods, which were built before 1950; adding corrosion inhibitors at the treatment plant; and testing every school's drinking fountains twice a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["system;","system,","system","system:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause "The committee's final report recommended three changes to the city's water system" introduces a list, so a colon is required. The list items are separated by semicolons because they contain internal commas; a semicolon before the first item would not introduce the list.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h66$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Biologist Renata Vasquez-Lowe spent six years documenting the courtship displays of the ______ small songbird whose males build elaborate bowers decorated with blue objects, from flower petals to bottle caps.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["satin bowerbird a","satin bowerbird; a","satin bowerbird, a","satin bowerbird. A"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"A small songbird whose males build elaborate bowers..." is an appositive renaming "the satin bowerbird," so it is set off with a comma. No punctuation runs the appositive into the noun, and a semicolon or period would leave the appositive as a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h67$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The software update was supposed to fix the ______ it introduced a new problem that caused the app to crash whenever users tried to upload a photo.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bug, unfortunately,","bug; unfortunately,","bug unfortunately,","bug; unfortunately"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses are joined: "The software update was supposed to fix the bug" and "it introduced a new problem...." "Unfortunately" is an introductory adverb in the second clause. A semicolon before it and a comma after it is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h68$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Historians disagree about the purpose of the stone ______ some argue that it served as an astronomical calendar, while others believe it marked a boundary between rival kingdoms.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["circle:","circle,","circle","circle, and"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The first clause is complete, and the second clause explains the disagreement. A colon is appropriate. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and" fails to show that the second clause details the disagreement.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h69$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Anyone who has tried to photograph the night sky knows that the camera often records far more than the eye can ______ stars too faint to see, the smudge of a distant galaxy, the pale green glow of the upper atmosphere.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["see;","see, such as:","see, for example;","see:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The clause before the blank is complete, and what follows is a list of examples. A colon introduces such a list. A semicolon must precede an independent clause, a colon should not follow "such as," and "for example;" misuses the semicolon.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h70$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The new telescope's mirror, which is made of 18 hexagonal segments coated in a thin layer of ______ unfold in space like the petals of a flower.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["gold was designed to","gold; was designed to","gold, was designed to","gold—was designed to"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The relative clause "which is made of 18 hexagonal segments... gold" opens with a comma after "mirror" and must close with a comma before the main verb "was designed."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h71$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When the composer's notebooks were finally made available to ______ discovered that several of her most famous melodies had first appeared decades earlier as children's songs she had written for her nieces.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["scholars they","scholars, they","scholars; they","scholars: they"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The dependent clause "When the composer's notebooks were finally made available to scholars" must be followed by a comma before the main clause "they discovered...." A semicolon or colon would leave the dependent clause as a fragment, and no punctuation blurs the boundary.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h72$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The flat, treeless ______ is home to a surprising diversity of life, from burrowing owls to the swift fox, a species once nearly eliminated from the region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["plain","plain,","plain—","plain;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The flat, treeless plain" is the subject of the sentence, and "is" is its verb. No punctuation should separate them.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h73$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In a series of ______ researchers asked participants to estimate how long a short video clip lasted, sometimes after playing them fast music and sometimes after playing slow music.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["experiments;","experiments:","experiments","experiments,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"In a series of experiments" is an introductory prepositional phrase that can be followed by a comma before the main clause. A semicolon or colon would require an independent clause before them, and with no punctuation "experiments researchers" would be confusing.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h74$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The coastal town relies on a single industry for most of its ______ it is unclear how the community will adapt as fish stocks continue to decline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["jobs,","jobs","jobs, and","jobs and,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses are joined. A comma plus the coordinating conjunction "and" is correct. A comma alone is a comma splice, no punctuation is a run-on, and "and," misplaces the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h75$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mountain pass is open for only about four months each ______ the rest of the year, snowdrifts more than thirty feet deep make it impassable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["year, for","year; for","year for","year: for,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The mountain pass is open for only about four months each year" and "for the rest of the year, snowdrifts... make it impassable" are independent clauses, so they are joined with a semicolon. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "year: for," adds a comma that separates "for" from its object.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h76$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ceramicist's glazes owe their unusual depth to an ingredient she declines to ______ she will say only that it comes from a riverbank near the village where her grandmother was born.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["name; instead,","name, instead,","name instead,","name, instead;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The first clause ends at "name," and a second independent clause begins with the conjunctive adverb "instead." A semicolon must precede "instead," and a comma follows it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h77$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The report's authors acknowledged one important ______ data on rural clinics came from surveys completed by clinic directors, who may have overstated the services their facilities provide.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["weakness, the","weakness the","weakness, and the","weakness: the"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"The report's authors acknowledged one important weakness" is independent, and the next clause identifies the weakness. A colon introduces the explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and" fails to show that the second clause names the weakness.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h78$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The violin maker inspects every piece of spruce for ______ grain lines, an absence of knots, and a bright, ringing tone when the wood is tapped.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["three qualities; tight","three qualities, tight","three qualities: tight","three qualities tight"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clause "The violin maker inspects every piece of spruce for three qualities" is complete and introduces a list. A colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, and a comma or no punctuation runs the list into the clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h79$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The theater's founder, who had trained as an architect before turning to stage ______ the building's unusual horseshoe-shaped auditorium herself.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["design planned","design, planned","design; planned","design—planned"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The nonessential clause "who had trained as an architect before turning to stage design" is opened with a comma and must close with a comma before the main verb "planned."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h80$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Because the comet's orbit carries it close to the Sun only once every 76 ______ astronomers who miss one appearance may have to wait the rest of their careers for the next.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["years,","years;","years:","years"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Because the comet's orbit carries it close to the Sun only once every 76 years" is a dependent clause at the start of the sentence, so it ends with a comma. A semicolon or colon would make it a fragment, and no punctuation runs it into the main clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h81$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The tidal ______ by the gravitational pull of both the Moon and the Sun—are strongest during new and full moons, when the two bodies align.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bulges, created","bulges: created","bulges created","bulges—created"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "created by the gravitational pull of both the Moon and the Sun" interrupts the subject and verb and is closed with a dash. It must open with a dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h82$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The study followed more than 8,000 participants for twenty ______ who walked at least thirty minutes a day had a significantly lower risk of heart disease than those who were largely sedentary.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["years, those","years those","years; those","years: those,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two independent clauses ("The study followed... for twenty years" and "those who walked... had a significantly lower risk") are joined with a semicolon. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "years: those," inserts a comma between the subject "those" and its modifier.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h83$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novelist is known for her intricate ______ chapter of her latest book, for example, is told from the point of view of a different object in the same house.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["structures, each","structures; each","structures each","structures, and, each"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The novelist is known for her intricate structures" and "each chapter... is told from the point of view of a different object" are independent clauses, joined correctly with a semicolon. (A colon would also be possible, but it is not offered.) A comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h84$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The crater, which scientists estimate is about 66 million years ______ thought to have been formed by the same impact that led to the extinction of the non-avian dinosaurs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["old, is","old is","old; is","old—is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Which scientists estimate is about 66 million years old" is a nonessential clause opened by a comma after "crater"; it must close with a comma before the verb "is thought."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h85$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The museum has acquired a rare first edition of the poet's debut ______ in 1855 and printed at the author's own expense, the book sold so poorly that most copies were eventually given away.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["collection, published","collection published","collection; published,","collection. Published"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence that begins with "Published in 1855" has its own subject ("the book") and verb ("sold"), and the introductory phrase modifies "the book." A period separating the two sentences is correct. A comma would create a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "collection; published," adds a misplaced comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h86$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Engineers designed the stadium's roof to retract in under ten ______ fans can enjoy open skies on a clear evening and be sheltered within minutes if a storm rolls in.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["minutes so,","minutes; so","minutes, so","minutes, so,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"So" is a coordinating conjunction joining two independent clauses, so it should be preceded by a comma. A comma after "so" or a semicolon before it is incorrect.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h87$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists working at the site found a cache of ______ arrowheads, a carved bone flute, and dozens of beads made from the shells of freshwater mussels.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["artifacts; obsidian","artifacts: obsidian","artifacts, obsidian","artifacts, such as: obsidian"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Archaeologists working at the site found a cache of artifacts" is complete and is followed by a list of the artifacts. A colon introduces the list. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, a comma makes "obsidian arrowheads" look like an appositive for only part of the list, and a colon should not follow "such as."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h88$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers' conclusion was ______ birds raised in noisy urban environments sang at a higher pitch than birds of the same species raised in quiet forests.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["clear:","clear;","clear,","clear"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The researchers' conclusion was clear" is an independent clause, and the following clause states the conclusion. A colon introduces it. A semicolon does not signal the explanation, a comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h89$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novel's ______ an aging lighthouse keeper on a remote Norwegian island—narrates the story in a series of unsent letters to his estranged daughter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["protagonist,","protagonist:","protagonist","protagonist—"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The appositive "an aging lighthouse keeper on a remote Norwegian island" sits between the subject and its verb "narrates" and closes with a dash. It must open with a dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h90$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Though she never studied ______ the self-taught botanist identified more than two hundred plant species in the hills around her farm.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["formally;","formally:","formally,","formally"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Though she never studied formally" is a dependent introductory clause, which should end with a comma before the main clause. A semicolon or colon would leave the dependent clause as a fragment, and no punctuation obscures the boundary.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h91$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The bakery sells three kinds of ______ a dense dark rye, a tangy sourdough, and a sweet brioche glazed with honey.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bread;","bread:","bread, including","bread, such as:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause is followed by a list of all three kinds of bread. A colon introduces the complete list. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, "including" implies the list is incomplete even though the sentence names exactly three kinds, and a colon should not follow "such as."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h92$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The geologists' survey of the ______ along with satellite measurements of how much the ground has risen—suggests that magma is accumulating beneath the volcano faster than previously thought.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["crater rim—","crater rim,","crater rim;","crater rim"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase "along with satellite measurements of how much the ground has risen" interrupts the subject "survey" and its verb "suggests." It closes with a dash and must open with a dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h93$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The documentary focuses on a single ______ an elderly fisherman in Ghana who has noticed his catch shrinking year after year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["subject;","subject. An","subject, an,","subject:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause is followed by a phrase identifying the subject. A colon introduces such a phrase. A semicolon must be followed by an independent clause, a period would make the phrase a fragment, and "subject, an," adds an incorrect comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h94$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Scientists once assumed that the deep ocean was too cold and dark to support much ______ hydrothermal vents discovered in 1977 were found to host thriving communities of tube worms, clams, and bacteria.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["life, however,","life however,","life; however,","life; however"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses are joined by the conjunctive adverb "however," so a semicolon precedes it and a comma follows it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h95$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Of all the challenges facing the ______ greatest was the lack of reliable maps of the river's upper reaches.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["expedition the","expedition, the","expedition; the","expedition: the"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Of all the challenges facing the expedition" is an introductory phrase, and "the greatest was the lack of reliable maps..." is the main clause. A comma separates them. A semicolon or colon would require an independent clause before the mark.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h96$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Some critics dismissed the film as ______ others praised its refusal to offer viewers an easy resolution.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["bleak, while","bleak; while","bleak while,","bleak. While"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"While others praised its refusal..." is a dependent clause attached to the main clause; a comma before "while" is correct here because "while" introduces a contrasting clause. A semicolon or period would leave the dependent clause as a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h97$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers measured three variables in each ______ temperature, the concentration of dissolved oxygen, and the number of insect larvae per square meter of streambed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["stream; water","stream, water","stream water","stream: water"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause introduces a list of the three variables, so a colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, and a comma or no punctuation makes "water temperature" run into the preceding noun.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h98$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many listeners assume that the song's lyrics are ______ fact, the songwriter has said they were inspired by a short story she read as a teenager.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["autobiographical, in","autobiographical in","autobiographical; in","autobiographical: in,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The two independent clauses are joined by a semicolon; "in fact" begins the second clause and is followed by a comma. A comma alone creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h99$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In a 2021 paper, Kenyan ______ Wanjiru Kamau described a method for detecting counterfeit malaria drugs using an inexpensive handheld spectrometer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["chemist,","chemist","chemist—","chemist:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Kenyan chemist" is a descriptive title placed directly before the name "Wanjiru Kamau." Such a title is restrictive and is not separated from the name by punctuation.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h100$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The surveyors reported ______ the eastern wall of the fortress had shifted nearly four inches since the previous inspection.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","that,",", that","that:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"That the eastern wall... had shifted" is a noun clause serving as the direct object of "reported." No punctuation should separate a verb from its object or separate "that" from the clause it introduces.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h101$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The vaccine proved highly effective in clinical ______ it required storage at extremely low temperatures, which made distributing it to remote areas difficult.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["trials, nevertheless,","trials nevertheless","trials; nevertheless","trials; nevertheless,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses are joined, and "nevertheless" is a conjunctive adverb at the start of the second. A semicolon must come before it and a comma after it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h102$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The bakery's sales doubled during the first year after it moved to a busier ______ the owners were able to hire four new employees and open a second location.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["street, consequently,","street consequently","street; consequently,","street, consequently;"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Consequently" is a conjunctive adverb linking two independent clauses. A semicolon precedes it and a comma follows it; the other options produce comma splices or misplace the semicolon.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h103$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The sculpture appears to be carved from a single block of ______ it is assembled from more than forty separate pieces joined so precisely that the seams are invisible.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["marble, in reality,","marble; in reality,","marble in reality,","marble; in reality"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence joins two independent clauses with the transitional phrase "in reality." A semicolon must separate the clauses, and "in reality" is followed by a comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h104$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The tortoise had been listed as extinct for more than a century when a single female was discovered on a remote volcano in ______ researchers are now searching for a mate.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["2019;","2019,","2019","2019, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence has two independent clauses ("The tortoise had been listed... in 2019" and "researchers are now searching for a mate"). They must be joined with a semicolon. A comma creates a comma splice, and "and," misplaces the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h105$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The violinist who performed the solo at last night's ______ only nineteen years old, began studying music at the age of three.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["concert","concert;","concert—","concert,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Only nineteen years old" is a nonessential phrase describing the violinist; it is closed by a comma before the verb "began." It must be opened by a comma as well. A dash would not match, and a semicolon cannot separate the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h106$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The town meeting ran past ______ residents had lined up to speak about the proposed highway, and the council refused to adjourn until every one of them had been heard.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["midnight, more than eighty","midnight more than eighty","midnight: more than eighty","midnight; and more than eighty"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first clause is complete, and the next two clauses explain why the meeting ran so late. A colon introduces that explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "; and" is an incorrect combination here.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h107$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers tested the paint samples for three heavy ______ cadmium, and mercury—all of which were once common in artists' pigments.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["metals, lead,","metals—lead,","metals: lead,","metals; lead,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The list "lead, cadmium, and mercury" is closed by a dash before "all of which," so it must be opened by a dash. A colon or comma would not pair with the closing dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h108$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ancient city of Petra, carved directly into rose-colored sandstone ______ hidden from most of the world until a Swiss explorer reached it in 1812.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["cliffs, remained","cliffs remained","cliffs; remained","cliffs—remained"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Carved directly into rose-colored sandstone cliffs" is a nonessential phrase that opens with a comma after "Petra"; it must close with a comma before the verb "remained."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h109$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The study found no link between screen time and sleep ______ it did find that teenagers who used phones in bed fell asleep, on average, twenty minutes later than those who did not.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["quality, it","quality it","quality: it,","quality; it"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses are joined. A semicolon is correct. A comma alone creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h110$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Researchers at the observatory have spent years searching for the source of a mysterious radio ______ a burst of energy that repeats roughly every sixteen days.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["signal;","signal. A","signal,","signal that is,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"A burst of energy that repeats roughly every sixteen days" is an appositive noun phrase renaming "signal," so it is set off with a comma. A semicolon or period would make the phrase a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h111$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The library's oldest ______ a fourteenth-century book of hours with gold-leaf illustrations—is displayed only one day each year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["volume,","volume—","volume:","volume"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The appositive is closed by a dash before "is displayed," so it must be opened by a dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h112$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The shipwreck lies in water so cold and so low in oxygen that the wood-boring organisms that destroy most wrecks cannot survive ______ the ship's hull, masts, and even its painted figurehead remain almost perfectly preserved.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["there; as a result,","there, as a result,","there as a result,","there; as a result"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses are joined by the transitional phrase "as a result." A semicolon goes before the phrase and a comma after it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h113$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, sailors told stories of rogue ______ sudden walls of water more than twice the height of the surrounding swells—but scientists dismissed the accounts as exaggerations until one was recorded by an oil platform's sensors in 1995.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["waves,","waves:","waves;","waves—"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "sudden walls of water... surrounding swells" is an appositive closed by a dash before "but." It must open with a matching dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h114$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The engineer explained that the turbine's blades are angled to capture wind from any ______ the hub rotates automatically to face whichever way the wind is blowing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["direction,","direction","direction:","direction, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The turbine's blades are angled to capture wind from any direction" is followed by a clause that explains how. A colon introduces the explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and," misplaces the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h115$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Although the two species look nearly ______ can be distinguished by their songs: one sings a rising trill, the other a series of short, falling notes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["identical; they","identical, they","identical. They","identical they"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Although the two species look nearly identical" is a dependent clause and must be joined to the main clause "they can be distinguished..." with a comma. A semicolon or period would leave the dependent clause as a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h116$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The new ferry can carry up to four hundred ______ than twice as many as the boat it replaced.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["passengers, more","passengers; more","passengers. More","passengers: more,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"More than twice as many as the boat it replaced" is a phrase that modifies the preceding clause, so it attaches with a comma. A semicolon or period would turn it into a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h117$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The historian's account of the ______ on letters written by soldiers on both sides—offers a strikingly personal view of the conflict.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["war, based","war; based","war based","war—based"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "based on letters written by soldiers on both sides" is closed by a dash before the verb "offers," so it must open with a dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h118$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When the volcano erupted in 1815, it ejected so much ash into the atmosphere that the following year became known in Europe and North America as the "Year Without a ______ failed as snow fell in June and frost struck in August.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Summer,\" crops","Summer\" crops","Summer\": crops","Summer,\" and, crops"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first clause is complete, and the second clause explains why 1816 earned its name. A colon (placed outside the closing quotation mark) introduces this explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and," misplaces the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h119$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The documentary's narrator never appears on ______ voice, low and unhurried, guides viewers through three centuries of the island's history.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["screen, her","screen; her","screen her","screen, and her,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The documentary's narrator never appears on screen" and "her voice... guides viewers" are both independent clauses, so a semicolon is needed. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "screen, and her," inserts a comma between the subject and its modifier.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h120$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Commissioned by a wealthy merchant in ______ the tapestry depicts a hunting scene in which every animal, from the stag to the smallest hare, is shown in astonishing detail.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1520,","1520;","1520:","1520"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Commissioned by a wealthy merchant in 1520" is an introductory participial phrase modifying "the tapestry." It ends with a comma before the main clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h121$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The pianist's hands are ______ she can span an interval of thirteen keys, nearly two full octaves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["unusually large,","unusually large","unusually, large:","unusually large:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"The pianist's hands are unusually large" is an independent clause, and the second clause illustrates just how large. A colon introduces the illustration. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the comma after "unusually" separates an adverb from the adjective it modifies.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h122$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The researchers concluded that the ______ in the Atacama Desert, which receives almost no rainfall—survive by absorbing moisture from fog that rolls in from the Pacific.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lichens, which grow","lichens which grow","lichens—which grow","lichens; which grow"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The relative clause "which grow in the Atacama Desert, which receives almost no rainfall" is closed by a dash before the verb "survive." It must be opened by a dash. A comma would not pair with the closing dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h123$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The old mill's waterwheel still ______ it no longer powers the grindstones, it now generates electricity for the nearby visitor center.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["turns, although","turns; although","turns although","turns: although,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The old mill's waterwheel still turns" is an independent clause. The rest of the sentence ("although it no longer powers the grindstones, it now generates electricity...") is a second independent clause that begins with a dependent clause. A semicolon is needed between the two independent clauses; a comma would create a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h124$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Researchers have identified the oldest known ______ a 2.4-billion-year-old filament found in volcanic rock in South Africa.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["fungus-like fossil:","fungus-like fossil;","fungus-like fossil. A","fungus-like fossil, it is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The independent clause is followed by a noun phrase identifying the fossil. A colon introduces it. A semicolon or period would leave the noun phrase as a fragment, and "it is" after a comma creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h125$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many early photographers coated their glass plates with ______ a sticky solution of gun cotton dissolved in ether—which had to be exposed and developed before it dried.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["collodion,","collodion:","collodion","collodion—"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The appositive "a sticky solution of gun cotton dissolved in ether" is closed by a dash before "which," so it must be opened by a dash. A comma or colon would not pair with the closing dash.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h126$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The committee members agreed on the goal but not on the ______ some wanted to raise the fee immediately, while others preferred to phase it in over three years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["method,","method","method:","method, so"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The first clause is complete, and the following clause explains the disagreement about method. A colon introduces such an explanation. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "so" signals a cause-and-effect relationship that doesn't exist.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h127$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Glass frogs, so named because the skin on their bellies is ______ can make themselves nearly invisible while sleeping by storing most of their red blood cells in their livers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["translucent","translucent,","translucent;","translucent—"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase "so named because the skin on their bellies is translucent" is nonessential and opens with a comma after "frogs." It must close with a comma before the verb "can make."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h128$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Unlike its neighbors, the small nation has no standing ______ it relies on a treaty with a larger ally for its defense.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["army; instead,","army, instead,","army instead,","army; instead"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses are joined with the conjunctive adverb "instead." A semicolon precedes it and a comma follows it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h129$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The authors of the report, a team of economists from three ______ that the tax credit lifted roughly 400,000 children out of poverty in its first year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["universities estimate","universities; estimate","universities—estimate","universities, estimate"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The appositive "a team of economists from three universities" begins with a comma after "report" and must close with a comma before the verb "estimate."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h130$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$To make the traditional ______ cooks wrap the seasoned meat in banana leaves and bury it in a pit lined with hot stones for most of a day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["dish;","dish:","dish,","dish"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"To make the traditional dish" is an introductory infinitive phrase. It is followed by a comma before the main clause "cooks wrap...."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h131$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mayor's plan has two main ______ to reduce traffic in the city center and to raise money for public transit.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["goals;","goals:","goals, these are","goals, they are:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause introduces the goals, which follow in a list. A colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list, and "these are" after a comma creates a comma splice; "goals, they are:" also places a colon after a verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h132$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The beetle's shell reflects only circularly polarized ______ property that has inspired engineers working on new kinds of camera filters and display screens.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["light, a","light; a","light. A","light a"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"A property that has inspired engineers..." is a noun phrase that sums up the preceding clause. It is attached with a comma. A semicolon or period would make it a fragment, and no punctuation runs it into "light."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h133$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The author's memoir describes her childhood in a farming village in northern ______ and her later career as a physician in Toronto.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Portugal; her emigration to Canada at sixteen;","Portugal: her emigration to Canada at sixteen,","Portugal, her emigration to Canada at sixteen;","Portugal, her emigration to Canada at sixteen,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence lists three things the memoir describes: her childhood, her emigration, and her career. Because none of the items contains internal commas, commas are used to separate them. Semicolons are unnecessary, and a colon would wrongly introduce the second item as an explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h134$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The fossilized footprints show that the dinosaur walked on its hind legs ______ the shallow impressions of its front feet appear only where it seems to have slipped in the mud.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["most of the time,","most of the time","most of the time;","most, of the time;"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The fossilized footprints show... most of the time" and "the shallow impressions... appear only where..." are independent clauses. A semicolon joins them. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h135$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The term "______ came into wide use in the 1970s to describe the practice of planting a mix of trees, shrubs, and crops on the same plot of land.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["agroforestry,\"","agroforestry\"","agroforestry\",","agroforestry\":"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"The term 'agroforestry'" is the subject, and "came" is its verb. No punctuation should separate them, so the closing quotation mark is followed directly by the verb. The word inside the quotation marks is a restrictive appositive for "term."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h136$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many scholars now believe that the epic was not the work of a single ______ was assembled over several centuries from the songs of many different storytellers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["poet but","poet, but,","poet; but","poet. But"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The epic was not the work of a single poet but was assembled..." is a single clause with a compound predicate built on "not... but." No punctuation is needed before "but," and a comma after "but" or a semicolon or period before it would break the construction.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h137$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ship's captain kept two ______ official one, which recorded the weather and the ship's position each day, and a private journal filled with sketches of the birds and fish he observed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["logs; an","logs, an,","logs an","logs: an"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause "The ship's captain kept two logs" introduces a description of the two logs. A colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce such a list, and the other choices misplace or omit punctuation.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h138$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Historians have long debated whether the treaty ended the war or merely paused ______ question may finally be answered by a cache of diplomatic letters discovered last year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["it, the","it the","it; the","it: the,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Historians have long debated whether... merely paused it" and "the question may finally be answered..." are independent clauses, so a semicolon joins them.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h139$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The chef sources her ingredients from within fifty miles of the restaurant, a rule she breaks for only one ______ she imports from a small family farm in Sri Lanka.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["item; cinnamon, which","item: cinnamon, which","item, cinnamon which","item: cinnamon which,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The clause "a rule she breaks for only one item" is complete, and "cinnamon" identifies the item, so a colon introduces it. The relative clause "which she imports..." is nonessential and is set off with a comma after "cinnamon."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h140$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The collection of letters, diaries, and photographs that the historian assembled over three decades of research in archives across four continents ______ now housed at the university library.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular noun "collection"; "letters, diaries, and photographs" and the long relative clause that follows are part of a modifier. A singular, present-tense verb ("is") is needed to agree with "collection" and with "now."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h141$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Among the most surprising findings of the decade-long survey of the Amazon basin ______ that several bird species thought to depend on undisturbed forest were thriving in patches of regrowth.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were the discovery","were the discoveries,","have been the discovery","was the discovery"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$This sentence is inverted: the subject, "the discovery," comes after the verb. Because "discovery" is singular, the verb must be "was." The plural verbs agree with "findings," which is the object of the preposition "among," not the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h142$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Having spent nearly a decade translating the epic from Old Norse, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the news that a rival translation would be published the same month dismayed the scholar.","a rival translation, the scholar learned, would be published the same month.","the scholar was dismayed to learn that a rival translation would be published the same month.","it was dismaying for the scholar to learn that a rival translation would be published the same month."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory phrase "Having spent nearly a decade translating the epic" must be followed immediately by the person who did the translating. Only the correct answer places "the scholar" right after the phrase. The others make it seem that the news, the rival translation, or "it" spent a decade translating.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h143$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither the lead researcher nor her two assistants ______ able to explain why the samples stored in the second freezer had degraded so much faster than the others.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$With "neither... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject. The nearer subject is "her two assistants," which is plural, so the verb must be "were." The past tense also matches "had degraded."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h144$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the time the first European ships reached the island in 1722, its inhabitants ______ hundreds of enormous stone statues, many of which stood on platforms along the coast.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had already carved","already carve","have already carved","will have already carved"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The carving happened before another past event (the ships' arrival in 1722). The past perfect, "had already carved," expresses an action completed before a past moment. The present and present perfect are inconsistent with 1722, and the future perfect is illogical.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h145$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The city's two largest employers, a hospital network and a regional airline, have both announced plans to expand, and economists expect ______ combined hiring to cut the local unemployment rate by almost a full percentage point.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","they're","there","their"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The pronoun refers to the two employers, a plural antecedent, and shows possession of "combined hiring." The plural possessive "their" is correct. "Its" is singular, "they're" means "they are," and "there" indicates a place.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h146$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The number of species known to science ______ grown dramatically over the past century, but biologists estimate that most species on Earth have still never been formally described.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","are","has","were"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"The number of species" is singular because the subject is "number" ("of species" is a prepositional phrase). The singular verb "has" is correct. Note that "a number of" would take a plural verb, but "the number of" takes a singular one.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h147$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Originally designed to measure the brightness of distant stars, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["climate scientists later used the instrument to track changes in the reflectivity of Arctic ice.","the instrument was later used by climate scientists to track changes in the reflectivity of Arctic ice.","changes in the reflectivity of Arctic ice were later tracked with the instrument by climate scientists.","the reflectivity of Arctic ice was later tracked by climate scientists using the instrument."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The modifier "Originally designed to measure the brightness of distant stars" describes the instrument, so "the instrument" must come immediately after the comma. The other choices make it sound as though climate scientists, changes, or reflectivity were designed to measure stars.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h148$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Each spring, a team of volunteers from the nearby university ______ the river's banks for invasive plants and records the location of every patch they remove.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["surveys","survey","have surveyed","are surveying"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular noun "team" (the phrase "of volunteers from the nearby university" is a modifier). The verb must be singular and present tense to match the other verb in the sentence, "records." "Surveys" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h149$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The painter's early work drew heavily on the folk traditions of her native Oaxaca, but by the 1960s she ______ to experiment with abstraction, layering bands of color that only faintly suggested landscapes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has begun","begins","will begin","had begun"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence describes the past ("by the 1960s"), and "by" signals an action completed before a past reference point. "Had begun" is correct; "has begun" and "begins" are inconsistent with the past time frame, and "will begin" is illogical.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h150$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Several of the documents that the archivist recovered from the flooded basement ______ written in a shorthand that no one at the library could read.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","is","were","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "Several," which is plural; the phrase "of the documents that the archivist recovered..." is a modifier. The plural, past-tense "were" agrees with the subject and matches the past-tense "recovered."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h151$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$One of the oldest surviving maps of the Pacific, which show islands that European navigators had not yet reached, ______ to have been drawn with the help of a Tahitian navigator named Tupaia.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are believed","is believed","were believed","believe"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "One," which is singular; "of the oldest surviving maps of the Pacific" is a modifier, and the relative clause is set off by commas. The verb must be singular, so "is believed" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h152$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$In 1919, the astronomer's calculations, which built on observations collected by dozens of earlier ______ that the Milky Way was far larger than most of her contemporaries believed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["astronomers, suggested","astronomers, suggests","astronomers' suggested","astronomer's suggested"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject "calculations" needs a past-tense verb (the sentence is set in 1919), and the nonessential relative clause must be closed with a comma before that verb. "Astronomers, suggested" is correct. "Suggests" is in the wrong tense, and the possessive forms are incorrect because nothing is being possessed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h153$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The tradition of carving masks for the winter festival, passed down through generations of families in the mountain villages, ______ nearly vanished in the twentieth century before a revival in the 1980s.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","were","are","had"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "tradition," which is singular. "Had nearly vanished" agrees with it and correctly places the near-disappearance before the 1980s revival. The plural verbs agree with "generations" or "families," which are inside modifying phrases.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h154$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The researchers asked participants to rate how confident they were in each answer. Surprisingly, those who scored lowest on the test tended to rate ______ confidence the highest.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they're","there","their","its"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The pronoun refers to "those who scored lowest," a plural group, and it indicates possession of "confidence." "Their" is the plural possessive pronoun.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h155$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Walking through the reconstructed village, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the methods residents used to spin wool, cure fish, and repair boats can be seen.","visitors can see how residents spun wool, cured fish, and repaired boats more than a thousand years ago.","a thousand-year-old way of life, including spinning wool, curing fish, and repairing boats, is visible.","it is possible to see how residents spun wool, cured fish, and repaired boats."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The opening phrase "Walking through the reconstructed village" must describe the subject that immediately follows it. Only "visitors" can walk through the village.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h156$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$If the city's proposal is approved, every new building over ten stories tall ______ required to include a rooftop garden or solar panels covering at least half of its roof.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will be","would have been","was","had been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The conditional "If the city's proposal is approved" refers to a possible future event. The main clause should use the future tense, "will be." The other choices refer to the past or to an unreal past condition.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h157$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The museum's new wing, along with the renovated sculpture garden and two restored galleries, ______ to the public next spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are scheduled to open","were scheduled to open","have been scheduled to open","is scheduled to open"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is the singular "wing"; "along with the renovated sculpture garden and two restored galleries" is a parenthetical phrase that does not make the subject plural. The singular "is" is correct, and the present tense fits "next spring."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h158$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The coral polyps that build the reef depend on microscopic algae living inside ______ tissues; when water temperatures rise too high, the algae are expelled and the coral turns white.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","it's","their","they're"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The pronoun refers to "The coral polyps," which is plural, so the plural possessive "their" is correct. "Its" is singular, and "it's" and "they're" are contractions.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h159$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Unlike the earlier model, which required a technician to recalibrate it every few ______ automatically adjusts for changes in temperature and humidity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["weeks, the new sensor's design","weeks, the new sensor","weeks, it is the new sensor that","weeks, there is a new sensor that"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase "Unlike the earlier model" sets up a comparison between two devices. The subject that follows must therefore be the new sensor, and "the new sensor automatically adjusts" is a complete clause. "The new sensor's design" compares a model to a design, and "it is the new sensor that" and "there is a new sensor that" add unnecessary or illogical structures.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h160$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Data from the satellite, which ______ orbiting Earth since 2009, show that the ice sheet lost more mass in the past five years than in the previous fifteen combined.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been","have been","were","had been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The relative clause describes "the satellite," a singular noun, and the action continues up to the present ("since 2009"). The present perfect singular "has been" is correct. "Have been" would wrongly agree with "Data."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h161$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The novel follows a family of glassblowers over four generations; by the final chapter, the youngest daughter, ______ the family workshop, must decide whether to sell it to a developer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom has inherited","which has inherited","who have inherited","who has inherited"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The relative pronoun refers to "the youngest daughter," a person, and is the subject of the clause, so "who" is correct (not "whom" or "which"). The verb must be singular, "has inherited," to agree with "daughter."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h162$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$There ______ a growing body of evidence suggesting that sleep plays a crucial role in consolidating memories formed during the day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","were","is","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In this inverted sentence, the subject follows the verb: "a growing body of evidence." The subject is the singular noun "body," so the verb must be "is." The plural verbs would agree only with "evidence" incorrectly treated as plural or with nothing.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h163$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$To reduce the risk of wildfires, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["dead brush is cleared from the trails each summer and certain areas are closed during periods of high wind.","the park service clears dead brush from the trails each summer and closes certain areas during periods of high wind.","clearing dead brush and closing certain areas during high winds are done each summer.","each summer the trails are cleared of dead brush, with certain areas being closed in high winds."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The introductory infinitive phrase "To reduce the risk of wildfires" must be followed by the agent performing the action. Only the correct answer names an agent ("the park service") as the subject. The other choices use passive or abstract subjects that cannot reduce risk.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h164$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The orchestra's conductor, as well as several of its principal players, ______ that the hall's new acoustic panels made the strings sound noticeably brighter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["agrees","agree","are agreeing","have agreed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the singular "conductor"; "as well as several of its principal players" is a parenthetical addition and does not make the subject plural. The singular "agrees" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h165$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Many of the island's endemic plants, such as a species of tree daisy that grows nowhere else on Earth, ______ threatened by goats introduced by sailors in the eighteenth century.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","was","has been","are"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "Many," which is plural; the phrase beginning "such as" is an example set off by commas. The plural, present-tense "are" agrees with the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h166$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The engineer's design for the footbridge, unlike most of the other proposals submitted to the city, ______ no supporting cables at all.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["use","have used","uses","are using"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is the singular "design"; "unlike most of the other proposals..." is a parenthetical phrase. The singular, present-tense "uses" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h167$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$In the original manuscript, the author had included a long chapter about the protagonist's childhood, but her editor persuaded her that the chapter slowed the story, so she ______ it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["cuts","cut","has cut","will cut"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The narration is in the past tense ("had included," "persuaded," "slowed"). The simple past "cut" maintains consistency. The present and future forms are inconsistent with the time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h168$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The reason that monarch butterflies can travel thousands of miles without getting lost ______ a subject of debate among biologists for decades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been","have been","are","were"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is "The reason," which is singular; "that monarch butterflies can travel... lost" is a clause describing the reason. "Has been" agrees with the singular subject and fits "for decades," which describes a situation continuing to the present.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h169$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Each of the cave paintings, some of which depict animals that have been extinct for more than ten thousand years, ______ been carefully photographed and catalogued.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","are","were","has"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "Each," which is always singular; the prepositional phrase and nonessential clause do not change that. "Has" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h170$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$After years of studying the ______ the linguist concluded that its grammar had been shaped by contact with at least three neighboring languages.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["dialect, it was","dialect, the conclusion of","dialect,","dialect, it seemed to"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory phrase "After years of studying the dialect" must be followed by the person who studied it, "the linguist." Only the correct answer keeps "the linguist" as the subject immediately after the phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h171$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The first known recipe for ice cream in English appears in a cookbook from 1718, though historians believe the dessert ______ in Europe for decades before that.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been enjoyed","had been enjoyed","is enjoyed","will have been enjoyed"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The enjoyment of ice cream occurred before the 1718 cookbook, another past event. The past perfect "had been enjoyed" places the action before that reference point.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h172$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The jury, after deliberating for nearly three days, finally ______ its verdict late on Friday afternoon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["delivered","delivers","deliver","have delivered"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The jury" is acting as a single unit here, as the singular possessive "its" confirms, so it takes a singular verb. The past tense "delivered" fits the past time frame of the sentence.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h173$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Long overlooked by art historians, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["major museums now exhibit the quilts made by the women of Gee's Bend, Alabama, as works of modernist abstraction.","art historians now study the quilts made by the women of Gee's Bend, Alabama, as works of modernist abstraction.","there is now widespread recognition of the quilts made by the women of Gee's Bend, Alabama.","the quilts made by the women of Gee's Bend, Alabama, are now exhibited in major museums and studied as works of modernist abstraction."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The introductory phrase "Long overlooked by art historians" describes the quilts, so "the quilts" must immediately follow the comma. The other choices place museums, art historians, or "there" in that position, creating a dangling modifier.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h174$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The bones of the ancient whale, discovered in a desert more than a hundred miles from the nearest ocean, ______ that the region was once submerged beneath a shallow sea.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["indicates","is indicating","indicate","has indicated"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is the plural "bones"; "of the ancient whale" and the participial phrase are modifiers. The plural verb "indicate" agrees with "bones."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h175$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The council voted to preserve the old train station, a decision that ______ the building from demolition and turned it into the town's most popular gathering place.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["saves","saved","will save","has saved"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence describes a completed past event, and the other verb in the same relative clause, "turned," is in the simple past. "Saved" keeps the two verbs parallel and consistent.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h176$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The effects of the medication on patients whose symptoms had been mild before treatment ______ far less pronounced than the researchers had predicted.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the plural "effects"; everything from "of the medication" to "before treatment" modifies it. The plural, past-tense "were" agrees with the subject and matches "had predicted."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h177$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Hidden beneath the floorboards of the old schoolhouse ______ dozens of letters written by students during the influenza epidemic of 1918.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","has been","is","were"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$This sentence is inverted: the subject, "dozens of letters," follows the verb. Because the subject is plural, the verb must be "were." "Schoolhouse" and "floorboards" are objects of prepositions, not the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h178$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$What the engineers found most troubling about the collapsed bridge ______ not the corrosion in its cables but the absence of any record that the cables had ever been inspected.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","are","was","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is the noun clause "What the engineers found most troubling about the collapsed bridge," which is singular. The singular, past-tense "was" is correct. "Cables" is part of the predicate, not the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h179$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$When conservators removed the yellowed varnish from the portrait in 2015, they discovered that the artist ______ it over an earlier, unfinished painting of a landscape.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has painted","had painted","paints","will paint"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The painting of the portrait occurred before the conservators' discovery in 2015. The past perfect "had painted" marks an action completed before another past event; the other tenses are inconsistent with the past time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h180$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$A pioneer of electronic music who built many of her own instruments from discarded radio parts, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Wanda Okonjo composed the score for the documentary using only sounds she recorded in the forest.","the documentary's score was composed by Wanda Okonjo using only sounds she recorded in the forest.","the forest provided all of the sounds that Wanda Okonjo used in composing the documentary's score.","only sounds recorded in the forest were used by Wanda Okonjo to compose the documentary's score."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The opening phrase describes a person ("A pioneer of electronic music who built... instruments"), so that person, Wanda Okonjo, must be the subject immediately following the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h181$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The botanist ______ the society honored at its annual banquet had spent forty years cataloging the mosses of the Appalachian Mountains.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who","which","whose","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The pronoun is the object of the verb "honored" in the clause "the society honored ___." The object form "whom" is correct. "Who" is a subject form, "which" refers to things, and "whose" shows possession.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h182$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the end of the race, the athlete had ______ more than six miles through water that was barely above freezing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["swam","swimmed","swum","swimming"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The past participle of "swim" is "swum," which is the form used with the helping verb "had." "Swam" is the simple past, "swimmed" is not a standard form, and "swimming" does not work with "had."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h183$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Because of the melting snowpack, the level of the reservoir has ______ nearly fifteen feet since the beginning of April.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["rose","risen","raised","rised"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Has" requires the past participle. The intransitive verb "rise" (to go up on its own) has the past participle "risen." "Rose" is the simple past, "raised" is a form of the transitive verb "raise," which needs an object, and "rised" is not a word.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h184$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The program aims to lower the cost of prescription drugs, to shorten the time patients wait for appointments, and ______ the number of clinics in rural counties.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["to increase","increasing","the increase of","increased"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Items in a series must be parallel. The first two items are infinitive phrases ("to lower," "to shorten"), so the third must also be an infinitive: "to increase."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h185$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Built for the 1889 World's Fair in Paris, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["many of the city's leading artists and writers initially derided the Eiffel Tower.","the city's leading artists and writers initially derided the Eiffel Tower.","derision from many of the city's leading artists and writers initially greeted the Eiffel Tower.","the Eiffel Tower was initially derided by many of the city's leading artists and writers."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The opening phrase "Built for the 1889 World's Fair in Paris" describes the tower, so "the Eiffel Tower" must be the subject immediately after the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h186$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Either the project manager or the two engineers who reviewed the plans ______ responsible for approving the change to the foundation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","is","were","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$With "either... or," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "the two engineers," which is plural. "Were" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h187$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Economics, a discipline that relies heavily on mathematical models and statistical evidence, ______ often called "the dismal science," a nickname coined in the nineteenth century.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","were","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Economics" is the name of a single field of study and takes a singular verb, despite ending in -s. The appositive phrase that follows does not change the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h188$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The tilt of Earth's axis and the shape of its orbit around the Sun ______ how much sunlight each hemisphere receives over the course of a year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["determine","determines","has determined","is determining"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The compound subject "The tilt of Earth's axis and the shape of its orbit" is joined by "and," so it is plural and takes the plural verb "determine."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h189$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$A series of powerful storms ______ caused the beach to lose nearly a third of its sand over the past two winters.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","are","were","has"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "A series," which is singular; "of powerful storms" is a prepositional phrase. "Has caused" agrees with "series" and fits "over the past two winters," a period extending to the present.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h190$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Since 2010, the population of the endangered crane ______ nearly doubled, thanks largely to a captive-breeding program that releases young birds into protected wetlands.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","had","has","will have"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Since 2010" signals an action that began in the past and continues to the present, which calls for the present perfect. The subject "population" is singular, so "has" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h191$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$As a child growing up in a village high in the Andes, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Ana Quispe's fascination began with the way her grandmother read the clouds to predict the weather.","Ana Quispe became fascinated by the way her grandmother read the clouds to predict the weather.","the way her grandmother read the clouds to predict the weather fascinated Ana Quispe.","her grandmother's way of reading the clouds to predict the weather fascinated Ana Quispe."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The phrase "As a child growing up in a village..." describes Ana Quispe, so her name—not her fascination, the way, or her grandmother's way—must follow the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h192$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The final decision about which paintings to include in the exhibition was left to the museum's director and ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["her.","she.","herself.","hers."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun is part of the compound object of the preposition "to" ("to the museum's director and ___"), so it must be in the object case: "her." "She" is a subject form, "herself" is reflexive and has no antecedent in the clause that it could refer back to, and "hers" is possessive.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h193$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Each of the city's four bridges has ______ own maintenance schedule, set according to the age of the structure and the volume of traffic it carries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","it's","they're","its"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The antecedent is "Each," which is singular, so the singular possessive "its" is required. "Their" is plural, and "it's" and "they're" are contractions.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h194$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The pair of rare manuscripts, which had been missing from the monastery library since the 1600s, ______ at auction last year for more than two million dollars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were sold","have been sold","was sold","are sold"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "pair," a singular noun; "of rare manuscripts" is a prepositional phrase. "Was sold" agrees with the singular subject and fits "last year."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h195$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The scientists ______ been working on the vaccine for nearly a decade when a sudden outbreak gave them the chance to test it on a large scale.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","had","has","will have"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The working continued up to a moment in the past (when the outbreak occurred), so the past perfect progressive "had been working" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h196$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Once dismissed as a weed that choked farm ponds, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the water fern is now cultivated in rice paddies across Asia because it adds nitrogen to the soil.","farmers across Asia now cultivate the water fern in rice paddies because it adds nitrogen to the soil.","rice paddies across Asia now contain the water fern because it adds nitrogen to the soil.","it is now common for farmers across Asia to cultivate the water fern in rice paddies."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The modifier "Once dismissed as a weed..." describes the water fern, so the water fern must be the subject immediately following it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h197$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Each of the employees and volunteers at the wildlife center ______ a two-day training course before handling any of the animals.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["complete","are completing","have completed","completes"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "Each," which is singular; "of the employees and volunteers" is a prepositional phrase. The singular, present-tense "completes" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h198$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The population of Lagos, which has grown rapidly since the 1970s, is now significantly larger than ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Cairo.","Cairo's population is.","that of Cairo.","those of Cairo."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The comparison must be between two populations, not between a population and a city. "That of Cairo" (meaning "the population of Cairo") is logical and singular. "Cairo" compares a population to a city, "Cairo's population is" adds an unneeded verb that makes the comparison awkward, and "those" is plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h199$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The study's main findings, along with the raw data collected at each of the twelve field sites, ______ made available to the public last month.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","were","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is the plural "findings"; "along with the raw data..." is a parenthetical phrase that does not change the number of the subject. "Were" agrees with "findings" and fits "last month."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h200$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Behind the lighthouse, on a narrow ledge just above the high-tide line, ______ two small cottages once occupied by the keeper and his family.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["stand","stands","is standing","has stood"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence is inverted; the subject, "two small cottages," comes after the verb. Because the subject is plural, the verb must be "stand."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h201$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By 2030, the utility company ______ all of the coal-fired generators at its three largest plants with wind and solar facilities, according to its published plan.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had replaced","has replaced","replaced","will have replaced"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"By 2030" refers to a point in the future by which an action will be completed. The future perfect "will have replaced" expresses this. The other tenses refer to the past.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h202$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Using a technique called dendrochronology, which matches patterns in tree rings to a master record, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the beams in the farmhouse were determined to come from trees cut down in the winter of 1741.","the trees used for the farmhouse's beams were found to have been cut down in the winter of 1741.","researchers determined that the beams in the farmhouse came from trees cut down in the winter of 1741.","it was determined that the beams in the farmhouse came from trees cut down in the winter of 1741."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The modifier "Using a technique called dendrochronology" must describe the people who used it. Only the correct answer places "researchers" immediately after the modifier.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h203$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The poet ______ collection of sonnets won the national prize this year began writing in her sixties, after retiring from a career as a surgeon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["who's","whose","who","whom"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The blank introduces a clause in which the poet possesses the "collection of sonnets." The possessive relative pronoun "whose" is correct. "Who's" means "who is," and "who" and "whom" do not show possession.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h204$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The lighthouse, which ______ on the cliff above the harbor since 1872, still guides fishing boats home through the fog.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has stood","stood","had stood","will stand"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Since 1872" combined with "still guides" indicates an action continuing from the past into the present, which requires the present perfect "has stood."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h205$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Unlike the novels of her contemporaries, which were often set in the drawing rooms of the wealthy, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Hester Lund portrays the lives of factory workers and domestic servants.","the lives of factory workers and domestic servants are portrayed by Hester Lund.","Hester Lund wrote about the lives of factory workers and domestic servants in her novels.","Hester Lund's novels portray the lives of factory workers and domestic servants."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Unlike the novels of her contemporaries" sets up a comparison with other novels, so the subject must be Hester Lund's novels. The other choices illogically compare novels with a person or with the lives of workers.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h206$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The committee recommended that every applicant ______ a portfolio of at least ten original works by the end of March.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["submits","submitted","submit","will submit"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After verbs of recommending or requiring followed by "that," Standard English uses the subjunctive mood, which is the base form of the verb: "submit."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h207$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The rover's instruments, which were designed to detect even trace amounts of organic molecules, ______ several compounds that scientists had not expected to find on Mars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["identifies","identified","has identified","is identifying"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject "instruments" is plural, and the sentence describes a completed past action ("had not expected"). The simple past "identified" works with a plural subject and fits the time frame. "Identifies" and "has identified" are singular forms, and "is identifying" is singular and present.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h208$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The twins, who were separated at birth and raised in different countries, discovered at their first meeting that they shared an unusual hobby, a fact that ______ researchers studying the influence of genes on personality.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["intrigued","intrigue","have intrigued","are intriguing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject of the relative clause is "that," referring to "a fact," which is singular, and the context is past. "Intrigued" is correct. "Intrigue," "have intrigued," and "are intriguing" are plural forms.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h209$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Not only did the restored wetland filter pollutants from the river, ______ it also provided habitat for dozens of migratory bird species.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["and","so","yet","but"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The correlative conjunction "Not only" must be paired with "but (also)." Pairing it with "and," "so," or "yet" breaks the construction.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h210$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Sharp-eyed and endlessly patient, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["an hour can pass before the heron, standing motionless in shallow water, strikes at a passing fish.","standing motionless in shallow water for an hour is how the heron catches a passing fish.","the heron can stand motionless in shallow water for an hour before striking at a passing fish.","a passing fish may wait an hour before the motionless heron strikes."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The adjectives "Sharp-eyed and endlessly patient" describe the heron, so "the heron" must be the subject immediately after the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h211$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The theater company, founded by a group of recent graduates in a converted garage, ______ its tenth season next fall with a production of a play written by one of its founders.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["opened","will open","has opened","had opened"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Next fall" places the action in the future, so the future tense "will open" is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h212$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Few people outside the region know that the river ______ its name from a word in the Lenape language meaning "swift water."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["takes","take","taking","have taken"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject of the clause is "the river," which is singular, and the present tense matches "know." "Takes" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h213$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Unlike ferns and flowering plants, mosses lack vascular tissue for moving water from one part of the plant to another; instead, they absorb moisture directly through ______ leaves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","it's","they're","their"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The pronoun refers to "mosses," which is plural, and shows possession of "leaves." The plural possessive "their" is correct. "Its" is singular, and "it's" and "they're" are contractions.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h214$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The list of ingredients printed on the back of the jar, which runs to more than thirty items, ______ several additives that are banned in other countries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["include","are including","includes","have included"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "list," which is singular; "of ingredients printed on the back of the jar" and the nonessential clause are modifiers. The singular "includes" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h215$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$A flock of nearly ten thousand starlings ______ above the marsh each evening, forming shifting, swirling shapes known as murmurations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["gather","gathers","are gathering","have gathered"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "flock," a collective noun acting as a single unit, so it takes the singular verb "gathers." "Of nearly ten thousand starlings" is a prepositional phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h216$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The results of the experiment, which was repeated in four different laboratories over a period of two years, ______ that the effect is real but much smaller than originally reported.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["suggest","suggests","has suggested","is suggesting"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the plural "results"; the clause "which was repeated..." describes "experiment." The plural verb "suggest" agrees with "results."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h217$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither the mayor nor the members of the city council ______ commented publicly on the report's findings.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has","was","is","have"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$With "neither... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "the members," which is plural. "Have" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h218$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Each of the paintings in the exhibition, most of which were borrowed from private collections, ______ a scene from the artist's childhood in rural Georgia.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["depict","are depicting","depicts","have depicted"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Each" is the subject and is always singular, so the verb must be "depicts." The prepositional phrase and nonessential clause do not affect agreement.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h219$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$One of the main reasons that the restoration took so long ______ the difficulty of finding craftspeople trained in traditional plastering techniques.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","have been","are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "One," which is singular; "of the main reasons that the restoration took so long" is a modifier. "Was" agrees with the subject and with the past tense "took."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h220$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Along the banks of the slow-moving river ______ dense thickets of willow and alder that provide shelter for nesting songbirds.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["grow","grows","is growing","has grown"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This sentence is inverted; the subject, "dense thickets," follows the verb. Because "thickets" is plural, the verb must be "grow."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h221$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The price of tickets for the city's most popular summer concerts ______ risen so sharply that many longtime residents can no longer afford to attend.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","are","were","has"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "price," which is singular; "of tickets for the city's most popular summer concerts" is a modifier. "Has risen" agrees with "price."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h222$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Every student and teacher at the school ______ required to complete the emergency training before the start of the fall term.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","were","is","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When a compound subject joined by "and" is preceded by "every," it is treated as singular. "Every student and teacher... is" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h223$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The collection of essays, written over a span of forty years, ______ how the author's views on art and politics changed as she grew older.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["trace","traces","are tracing","have traced"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "collection," which is singular. The singular verb "traces" agrees with it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h224$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Located at the mouth of the river, where fresh water meets the sea, ______ three small islands that serve as breeding grounds for pelicans.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","was","has been"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This sentence is inverted; the subject, "three small islands," follows the verb. A plural verb, "are," is required.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h225$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$These atmospheric phenomena, which include the green flash sometimes seen at sunset, ______ only under very specific conditions of temperature and humidity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["occurs","has occurred","is occurring","occur"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Phenomena" is the plural of "phenomenon," so it takes the plural verb "occur." The demonstrative "These" also signals the plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h226$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The criteria that the judges used to select the winning design ______ never made public, which led several architects to question whether the competition had been fair.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","has been","were","is"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Criteria" is the plural of "criterion," so it requires a plural verb. The past tense "were" also fits the past context ("led," "had been").$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h227$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Anyone who wishes to visit the archive's rare-book room ______ to make an appointment at least two weeks in advance.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["need","needs","are needing","have needed"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Anyone" is an indefinite pronoun that is always singular. The singular "needs" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h228$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The bacteria found in the hot springs, which can survive temperatures above 80°C, ______ enzymes that are now used in a common laboratory technique for copying DNA.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["produce","produces","is producing","has produced"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Bacteria" is the plural of "bacterium," so it takes the plural verb "produce."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h229$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$After the merger, the newly formed company announced that ______ headquarters would remain in Chicago but that several departments would move to Atlanta.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","it's","they're","its"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"The newly formed company" is a single entity and a singular noun, so the singular possessive "its" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h230$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The award for outstanding research was shared by Professor Adeyemi and ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["I.","myself.","me.","mine."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The pronoun is part of the compound object of the preposition "by," so it must be in the object case: "me." (Test it alone: "shared by me," not "shared by I.") "Myself" is reflexive and has no antecedent here, and "mine" is possessive.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h231$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The physicist ______ many historians believe deserved the Nobel Prize for the discovery was never nominated during her lifetime.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","which","whose"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The pronoun is the subject of the verb "deserved" ("many historians believe" is an interrupting clause). The subject form "who" is correct. Test it: "she deserved the prize," not "her deserved."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h232$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The diplomat to ______ the letter was addressed claimed that she had never received it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","which","whose"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun is the object of the preposition "to," so the object form "whom" is correct. "Which" refers to things, and "whose" is possessive.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h233$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$______ been nearly a century since anyone has seen the bird in the wild, but ornithologists have not given up hope that a small population survives in the remote valley.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Its","Its'","It is","It's"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The sentence requires a contraction of "it has" ("It has been nearly a century"). "It's" can stand for either "it is" or "it has." "It is been" is ungrammatical, "Its" is possessive, and "Its'" is not a word.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h234$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The researchers hope that once the sensors are installed, ______ able to detect leaks in the pipeline within minutes rather than days.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","there","they'll be","they're being"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clause needs a subject and verb: "they will be able to detect." "They'll be" is the contraction for "they will be." "Their" is possessive, "there" refers to a place, and "they're being able" is ungrammatical.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h235$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the time the rescue team reached the summit, the storm ______ and the climbers had begun their descent on their own.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["passes","had passed","has passed","will pass"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Both actions (the storm passing and the climbers beginning to descend) happened before another past event (the team reaching the summit). The past perfect "had passed" is parallel with "had begun."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h236$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$If the levee had been reinforced as engineers recommended, the low-lying neighborhoods ______ from the flooding.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["would have been spared","will be spared","were spared","are spared"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$This is a past unreal (counterfactual) conditional: "If the levee had been reinforced." The result clause requires "would have" plus a past participle: "would have been spared."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h237$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Before pouring the concrete, the workers carefully ______ steel reinforcing bars in a grid across the entire foundation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["lay","lain","layed","laid"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The verb needs to be the past tense of the transitive verb "lay" (to place something), which takes the object "steel reinforcing bars." The past tense is "laid." "Lay" is the present tense of "lay" (or the past of "lie"), "lain" is the past participle of "lie," and "layed" is not standard.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h238$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$If the Moon ______ even slightly closer to Earth, the tides would be noticeably higher along every coastline.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","is","were","will be"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence describes a hypothetical situation contrary to fact, which calls for the subjunctive "were" ("If the Moon were closer..."). The result clause "would be" confirms the hypothetical.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h239$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The expedition's leader insisted that she had ______ the route because it avoided the glacier's most dangerous crevasses.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["chose","chosen","choosed","choosing"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After the helping verb "had," the past participle is required. The past participle of "choose" is "chosen."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h240$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Within a week of being transplanted, the seedlings had ______ to produce new leaves, a sign that their roots were taking hold.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["begun","began","begin","beginned"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$After "had," the past participle is required: "begun." "Began" is the simple past, and "beginned" is not a word.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h241$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Over the course of the drought, the lake ______ to less than a third of its former size, exposing boat docks that now sat in dry mud.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["shrunk","shrinked","shrinks","shrank"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The simple past tense of "shrink" is "shrank." "Shrunk" is the past participle, which requires a helping verb such as "had." "Shrinked" is not standard, and "shrinks" is in the present tense.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h242$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The steamship ______ within minutes after striking a submerged rock, and all but three of its passengers reached the shore in lifeboats.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["sunk","sinked","sank","has sunk"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The simple past tense of "sink" is "sank," which matches "reached." "Sunk" is a past participle requiring a helping verb, and "has sunk" is inconsistent with the past time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h243$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$In the novel's final chapter, the narrator returns to the house where she grew up and ______ that the orchard her father planted has been cut down.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["discovered","discovers","had discovered","will discover"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Literary summaries conventionally use the present tense, and the sentence already uses "returns." "Discovers" maintains consistency.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h244$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The scientists' conclusions, which were based on more than ten thousand measurements, ______ by an independent team the following year.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were confirmed","was confirmed","confirmed","has been confirmed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is the plural "conclusions," and the conclusions received the action, so a plural passive verb is needed: "were confirmed." "Confirmed" alone would mean the conclusions did the confirming.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h245$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The gardeners noticed that the ______ habitat had shrunk to a narrow strip of meadow along the edge of the highway.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["specie's","species's","specie","species'"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Species" is both singular and plural and ends in -s, so its possessive form is "species'." "Specie" is a different word (meaning coined money).$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h246$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The museum recently opened a new wing devoted to ______ clothing from the nineteenth century, including dresses, hats, and hand-embroidered shoes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["womens'","womens","women's","woman's"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Women" is already plural, so its possessive is formed by adding -'s: "women's." "Womens'" and "womens" are not standard, and "woman's" refers to a single woman.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h247$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Several of the ______ employees testified that safety inspections had been skipped for months before the accident.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["companys'","company's","companies","company"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The employees belong to a single company, so the singular possessive "company's" is correct. "Companies" is a plural noun with no possessive, and "companys'" is not a correct form.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h248$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The two research teams agreed to review ______ data before publishing, a practice that helped each group catch errors the other had missed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["each other's","each others'","each others","each other"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive form of "each other" is "each other's." "Each others'" and "each others" are nonstandard, and "each other" without the possessive cannot modify "data."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h249$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The landlord agreed to give the tenants a full ______ notice before beginning repairs on the building's roof.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["weeks'","weeks","week","week's"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The notice is the length of one week, so the singular possessive "week's" is correct (as in "a week's notice"). "Weeks'" would refer to more than one week.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h250$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Measuring nearly forty feet from nose to tail, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["paleontologists have called the fossilized skeleton the largest of its kind ever found in North America.","North America has never yielded a larger fossilized skeleton of its kind.","the fossilized skeleton is the largest of its kind ever found in North America.","the largest of its kind ever found in North America, according to paleontologists, is the fossilized skeleton."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The phrase "Measuring nearly forty feet from nose to tail" must modify the skeleton, which should immediately follow the comma. The other choices place paleontologists, North America, or "the largest" in that position.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h251$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Raised by her grandparents on a sheep farm in New Zealand, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the rhythms of shearing songs often appear in the composer's orchestral works.","the composer often incorporates the rhythms of shearing songs into her orchestral works.","her orchestral works often incorporate the rhythms of shearing songs.","shearing songs have often influenced the composer's orchestral works."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The opening phrase describes a person raised by her grandparents, so "the composer" must follow the comma. The other choices make the rhythms, the works, or the songs the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h252$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Exhausted after the twelve-hour climb, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the team set up camp just below the ridge and waited for the wind to die down.","camp was set up just below the ridge while the team waited for the wind to die down.","the ridge offered the team some shelter while they waited for the wind to die down.","the wind kept the team from going farther than the ridge."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Exhausted after the twelve-hour climb" describes the team, so "the team" must be the subject immediately after the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h253$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Written in a code that combined Latin, Greek, and invented symbols, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["a historian finally cracked the code in the scientist's notebooks in 2009.","no one could read the scientist's notebooks until a historian cracked the code in 2009.","it was not until 2009 that a historian cracked the code in the scientist's notebooks.","the scientist's notebooks remained unreadable until a historian cracked the code in 2009."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The modifier "Written in a code..." describes the notebooks, so "the scientist's notebooks" must follow it as the subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h254$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$To qualify for the scholarship, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["two letters of recommendation and a writing sample must be submitted by March 1.","the deadline for submitting two letters of recommendation and a writing sample is March 1.","applicants must submit two letters of recommendation and a writing sample by March 1.","a writing sample and two letters of recommendation are required by March 1."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory phrase "To qualify for the scholarship" must be followed by the people who qualify. Only "applicants" can qualify for a scholarship.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h255$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$After analyzing the chemical composition of the pigments, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the painting was found to have been made at least a century later than its label claimed.","the conservators concluded that the painting had been made at least a century later than its label claimed.","the label's claim about the painting's date turned out to be wrong by at least a century.","it became clear that the painting had been made at least a century later than its label claimed."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The introductory phrase "After analyzing the chemical composition of the pigments" must describe whoever did the analyzing. Only "the conservators" could have performed the analysis.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h256$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Widely regarded as the founder of modern statistics, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Ronald Fisher developed many of the methods that scientists still use to design experiments.","many of the methods that scientists still use to design experiments were developed by Ronald Fisher.","the methods that scientists still use to design experiments owe much to Ronald Fisher.","scientists still use many of the experimental methods developed by Ronald Fisher."]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase "Widely regarded as the founder of modern statistics" describes Ronald Fisher, so his name must come directly after the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h257$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Stretching for more than 2,300 kilometers along the coast of Queensland, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["living organisms have built the Great Barrier Reef, the largest structure of its kind on Earth.","scientists consider the Great Barrier Reef the largest structure on Earth built by living organisms.","no structure on Earth built by living organisms is larger than the Great Barrier Reef.","the Great Barrier Reef is the largest structure on Earth built by living organisms."]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The phrase "Stretching for more than 2,300 kilometers along the coast of Queensland" describes the reef, so "the Great Barrier Reef" must follow it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h258$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Though barely visible to the naked eye, ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["extreme cold, intense radiation, and even the vacuum of space can be survived by tardigrades.","scientists have shown that tardigrades can survive extreme cold, intense radiation, and even the vacuum of space.","tardigrades can survive extreme cold, intense radiation, and even the vacuum of space.","the vacuum of space, along with extreme cold and intense radiation, cannot kill tardigrades."]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The phrase "Though barely visible to the naked eye" describes tardigrades, so "tardigrades" must be the subject immediately following it.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h259$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The museum's curators, along with a team of volunteers, ______ spent the past year cataloging more than 40,000 insect specimens.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has","have","is","was"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "curators," which is plural; "along with a team of volunteers" is a parenthetical phrase that does not change the subject. The plural "have" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h260$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Mathematics and music, which share a reliance on pattern and proportion, ______ often studied together in the ancient world.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","was","has been","is"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The compound subject "Mathematics and music" is joined by "and," so it is plural. The past-tense plural "were" fits "in the ancient world."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h261$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$There ______ several reasons why the city chose to bury the power lines rather than repair the damaged poles.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","is","has been","were"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In this inverted sentence, the subject "several reasons" follows the verb. It is plural, so the verb must be "were." The past tense fits "chose."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h262$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The council's recommendations, which were based on two years of public hearings, ______ largely ignored by the state legislature.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","has been","were","is"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is the plural "recommendations," so the plural "were" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h263$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The swarm of locusts that descended on the region last spring ______ consumed an estimated 200 tons of crops in a single day.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","had","were","are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "swarm," a singular collective noun; "of locusts" is a prepositional phrase. "Had consumed" agrees with the singular subject and fits the past context.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h264$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The archaeologists believe that the settlement ______ abandoned suddenly, perhaps after a fire, because meals were found still sitting in cooking pots.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","is","has been","will be"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The abandonment happened in the distant past, so the simple past passive "was abandoned" is correct. The other tenses are inconsistent with that time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h265$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Her grandfather, who emigrated from Lebanon in 1912, ______ a small grocery store in Detroit that the family still operates today.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["opens","has opened","will open","opened"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The opening of the store occurred in the past and is a completed action, so the simple past "opened" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h266$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The orchestra performed the symphony exactly as the composer ______ it, using instruments built to nineteenth-century specifications.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["intends","has intended","had intended","will intend"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The composer's intention existed before the orchestra's performance, which was itself in the past. The past perfect "had intended" correctly places the intention earlier.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h267$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The children ______ found the injured owl brought it to the wildlife center, where veterinarians repaired its broken wing.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whom","who","which","whose"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The pronoun is the subject of the verb "found," and it refers to people, so "who" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h268$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The vast majority of the meteorites ______ have been collected in Antarctica came from the asteroid belt between Mars and Jupiter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["that","who","whom","whose"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The relative pronoun refers to "meteorites," which are things, so "that" is correct. "Who" and "whom" refer to people, and "whose" is possessive.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h269$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The cooperative, founded by six coffee growers in the 1990s, now ______ more than four hundred members across three provinces.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","are having","were having","has"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "cooperative," which is singular, and "now" calls for the present tense. "Has" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h270$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Not until the 1960s ______ that the continents move slowly across Earth's surface on massive plates.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["most geologists accepted","had most geologists accepted","did most geologists accept","most geologists did accept"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$When a sentence begins with a negative expression such as "Not until," the subject and auxiliary verb are inverted: "did most geologists accept." The other choices fail to invert or use the wrong tense.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h271$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The study compared the reading habits of adults who grew up with libraries in their neighborhoods with ______$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["adults who did not.","those of adults who did not.","that of adults who did not.","adults' who did not."]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The sentence compares reading habits, so the second part of the comparison must also be reading habits: "those of adults who did not." "Those" is plural to match "habits." Comparing habits with "adults" is illogical.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h272$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The bridge's original designers could not have anticipated the volume of traffic ______ carries today.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["it","they","its","it's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The pronoun refers to "the bridge," which is singular, and is the subject of "carries." "It" is correct; "its" is possessive and "it's" means "it is."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h273$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither of the two proposals submitted to the board ______ adequately how the new facility would be funded.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["explain","are explaining","have explained","explains"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Neither" used as a pronoun is singular, so it takes the singular verb "explains."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h274$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The herd of elephants ______ across the dry riverbed in single file, the youngest calves sheltered between the adults.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were moving","move","moved","have moved"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Herd" is a singular collective noun acting as a unit. "Moved" is the correct past-tense form; "were moving," "move," and "have moved" are plural forms.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h275$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither rain nor the threat of lightning ______ the crowd from waiting outside the stadium for the band's arrival.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were deterring","deterred","have deterred","deter"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$With "neither... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "the threat," which is singular. "Deterred" (singular past) is correct; "were deterring," "have deterred," and "deter" are plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h276$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Marine geologist Ines Varga spent three summers mapping the seafloor off Iceland's southern coast. Her sonar surveys revealed something no earlier expedition had ______ chain of hydrothermal vents running parallel to the coastline for nearly forty kilometers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["detected. A","detected a","detected; a","detected: a"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause ending in "detected" is followed by a noun phrase that specifies the "something," so a colon is correct. A semicolon or period would require an independent clause after it, but "a chain of hydrothermal vents running..." has no main verb. With no punctuation, the phrase would wrongly read as the object of "detected."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h277$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The 1913 Armory Show introduced many Americans to European modernism. Critics mocked Marcel Duchamp's <i>Nude Descending a Staircase</i>, one reviewer likening it to ______ crowds lined up for hours to see it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["\"an explosion in a shingle factory,\" yet","\"an explosion in a shingle factory\"; yet,","\"an explosion in a shingle factory,\" yet,","\"an explosion in a shingle factory\" yet"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses joined by the coordinating conjunction "yet" need a comma before the conjunction and none after it. Omitting the comma creates a run-on. A comma after "yet" wrongly separates the conjunction from its clause. A semicolon followed by "yet," treats the conjunction as a transitional adverb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h278$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Unlike most spiders, which rely on webs to trap prey, the jumping spider <i>Portia fimbriata</i> hunts by stealth. It will take a long, winding detour to approach another spider from ______ path that sometimes keeps its target out of sight for an hour or more.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["behind, a","behind; a","behind. A","behind a"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"A path that sometimes keeps its target out of sight..." is a noun phrase that renames the "long, winding detour." It can't stand alone, so a semicolon or period creates a fragment. With no punctuation, "behind a path" misreads as a prepositional phrase. A comma correctly sets off the appositive at the end of the sentence.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h279$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The Voynich manuscript, a fifteenth-century book written in an unknown script and illustrated with unidentifiable ______ resisted every attempt at decipherment, including efforts by some of the most skilled code breakers of World War II.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["plants—has","plants has","plants, has","plants; has"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The appositive beginning "a fifteenth-century book" opens with a comma, so it must close with a comma before the main verb "has." A dash would mismatch the opening comma, a semicolon would separate subject from verb, and no punctuation leaves the appositive unclosed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h280$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Economist Ama Owusu studies informal lending networks in West African markets, where traders often borrow from one another rather than from ______ these networks, she has found, can extend credit faster than formal institutions and with lower default rates.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["banks","banks, and that","banks,","banks;"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses meet at the blank: "traders often borrow ... from banks" and "these networks ... can extend credit faster." A semicolon correctly joins them. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and that" produces an ungrammatical structure.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h281$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Historian Mara Quist has argued that the problem with the official account of the 1871 flood is not that it is ______ that it is incomplete.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["untrue; but","untrue, but,","untrue but","untrue: but"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The structure "not that X but that Y" is a single correlative construction, so no punctuation is needed between its halves. Commas, a semicolon, or a colon would split the paired elements of one clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h282$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In 1959, physicist Richard Feynman delivered a lecture titled "There's Plenty of Room at the ______ in which he imagined machines that could manipulate individual atoms, an idea later credited with inspiring the field of nanotechnology.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Bottom;\"","Bottom\",","Bottom,\"","Bottom\""]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A comma is needed before the nonrestrictive clause "in which he imagined machines..." In American Standard English, commas go inside closing quotation marks. The choice with the comma outside the quotation mark is incorrect, no punctuation runs the clause into the title, and a semicolon would leave a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h283$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Researchers tracking Arctic terns with lightweight geolocators made a surprising discovery: rather than flying directly between breeding and wintering grounds, the birds follow an S-shaped route over the Atlantic. This ______ allows the terns to take advantage of prevailing winds, adds thousands of kilometers to the trip.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["path, which","path, it","path which","path; which"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The relative clause "which allows the terns to take advantage of prevailing winds" is nonrestrictive and must be set off by a comma before "which" and a comma after "winds," as the text already provides. Omitting the first comma leaves the pair unbalanced, a semicolon creates a fragment, and "it" creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h284$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Sociologist Pedro Alcántara's study of three Chicago neighborhoods identified the factors most associated with residents' sense of ______ access to green space, the presence of locally owned businesses, and the number of years residents had lived on the same block.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["safety;","safety:","safety, these were:","safety, including:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause ends with "safety," and a list follows that explains which factors, so a colon is correct. A semicolon cannot introduce a list. "These were:" creates a comma splice, and "including:" places a colon after a word that already introduces the list.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h285$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Most comets visible from Earth originate in the Oort cloud, a vast sphere of icy bodies far beyond Neptune. ______ astronomers have never directly observed a single object within it; its existence is inferred from the orbits of the comets themselves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Remarkably","Remarkably;","Remarkably,","Remarkably:"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Remarkably" is an introductory adverb modifying the whole clause, so it is followed by a comma. A semicolon or colon would require an independent clause before it, and omitting punctuation after a sentence-level adverb is nonstandard here.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h286$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The architect Lina Bo Bardi designed São Paulo's SESC Pompéia by converting an old steel-drum factory rather than demolishing it. She kept the brick sheds intact and added two concrete towers connected by ______ irregular "windows" were cut into the towers' walls without glass, so air and light pass freely through them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["walkways, and their,","walkways; their","walkways, their","walkways their"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses meet here. A semicolon correctly joins them. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the comma after "their" wrongly separates a possessive from the noun it modifies.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h287$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The Great Pacific Garbage Patch is often imagined as a floating island of trash. In reality, most of its debris consists of microplastics suspended in the water ______ dispersed that a ship could pass through the area without its crew noticing anything unusual.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["column, so,","column so","column, so","column; so"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The phrase "so dispersed that a ship could pass..." modifies the microplastics and isn't an independent clause. A comma before "so" sets off this descriptive phrase. A semicolon would create a fragment, no comma blurs the boundary, and a comma after "so" splits the "so ... that" construction.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h288$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When the Rosetta Stone was discovered in 1799, scholars recognized its potential immediately, since the stone bears the same decree in three ______ Egyptian hieroglyphs, Demotic script, and ancient Greek.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["scripts:","scripts, they are","scripts;","scripts, being"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The clause ending in "three scripts" is independent, and the list that follows specifies which scripts, so a colon is correct. A semicolon can't introduce a list, "being" creates an awkward, ungrammatical phrase, and "they are" creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h289$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The collection of letters, journals, and unpublished poems that the archivist discovered in a sealed trunk beneath the library's main staircase ______ new light on the poet's final years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are shedding","have shed","shed","sheds"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is the singular "collection," not "letters, journals, and poems" or "trunk." The singular present-tense verb "sheds" agrees with it. "Shed" (as a present-tense form), "have shed," and "are shedding" are all plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h290$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither the lead investigator nor the members of her research team ______ able to explain why the bacterial colonies grew faster in the dimly lit incubator.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","has been","was","were"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$With "neither ... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "members," which is plural. "Were" is correct. "Was," "has been," and "is" are singular forms that would agree only with "investigator."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h291$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Each spring, thousands of horseshoe crabs crawl onto the beaches of Delaware Bay to spawn, and migrating red knots, a shorebird, time ______ arrival to feast on the crabs' eggs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["their","its","it's","they're"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive pronoun refers to "red knots," a plural noun, so "their" is correct. The appositive "a shorebird" names the species but does not change the plural subject. "Its" is singular, and "it's" and "they're" are contractions.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h292$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Having spent decades studying how glaciers carve valleys, ______ that the rate of erosion depends more on the speed of the ice than on its thickness.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["geologist Hanne Lund has concluded","the conclusion of geologist Hanne Lund is","geologist Hanne Lund's conclusion is","it has been concluded by geologist Hanne Lund"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase "Having spent decades studying..." must modify the person who studied glaciers, so Hanne Lund must immediately follow the comma. The other choices place "the conclusion," "Lund's conclusion," or "it" in that position, creating dangling modifiers.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h293$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the time the Erie Canal opened in 1825, workers ______ more than 360 miles of channel through forests, swamps, and limestone ridges, mostly with shovels, picks, and animal power.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have dug","dig","will have dug","had dug"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"By the time" with a past event (the canal opened in 1825) calls for the past perfect, "had dug," to show that the digging was completed before that point. The present perfect, future perfect, and present tense don't fit the past time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h294$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The museum's newly restored mural, along with the dozens of preparatory sketches that the artist made in the months before painting ______ on display in the east gallery through March.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["it are","it; is","it, is","it, are"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "mural"; the phrase "along with the dozens of preparatory sketches..." is parenthetical and does not make the subject plural. The singular "is" is needed, and the opening comma before "along" must be matched by a comma before the verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h295$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$According to a 2021 survey, the share of American adults who read at least one print book in the previous year was larger than ______ who listened to an audiobook.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["adults","the share of those","those","the adults"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The comparison must be logical: a share is compared with another share. "The share of those" keeps the comparison parallel. The other choices compare a share with people ("those," "adults," or "the adults"), which is an illogical comparison.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h296$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The team of engineers that designed the bridge's suspension cables tested several alloys before choosing one whose resistance to corrosion ______ the requirements set by the city.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["exceed","were exceeding","exceeded","have exceeded"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The verb's subject is "resistance," which is singular, and the narrative is in the past tense ("tested," "choosing"). "Exceeded" fits both. "Exceed" and "have exceeded" don't agree with a singular subject, and "were exceeding" is plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h297$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$In her study of medieval manuscripts, historian Rosalind Achebe found that scribes often corrected their own mistakes in the margins, a practice that suggests the copyists cared more about accuracy than ______ often assume.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["modern readers","modern readers do","modern reader's","modern readers'"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Than modern readers often assume" is a complete comparative clause: "modern readers" is its subject and "assume" its verb. Adding "do" duplicates the verb, and the possessive forms "reader's" and "readers'" have nothing to possess.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h298$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Centuries before the telescope was invented, the Maya astronomers whose calculations survive in the Dresden Codex ______ the cycle of Venus with remarkable precision, predicting its appearances to within a few hours.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had tracked","tracks","will track","have tracked"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence describes something completed before another past event, the invention of the telescope, so the past perfect "had tracked" is correct. The present, present perfect, and future tenses don't fit this past time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h299$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Studying the thousands of clay tablets excavated at Ebla, ______ evidence of a sophisticated trade network that stretched from Anatolia to Mesopotamia in the third millennium BCE.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["archaeologists have found","evidence has been found by archaeologists, which offers","there has been found","the archaeologists' findings offer"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase "Studying the thousands of clay tablets" must be followed by the people who studied them. Only "archaeologists have found" supplies that subject. The other choices make "evidence," "findings," or an empty "there" do the studying.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h300$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Among the many species of fungi that form partnerships with plant roots ______ a group known as ectomycorrhizal fungi, which wrap around root tips and help trees absorb nitrogen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","is","are","were"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$This sentence is inverted: the subject comes after the verb. The subject is the singular "a group," so "is" is correct. "Species" and "fungi" belong to the prepositional phrase and do not control the verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h301$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The festival's lineup featured choreographers from three continents: Akram Khan, who blends kathak with contemporary dance; Germaine Acogny, often called the mother of contemporary African ______ and Pina Bausch's former collaborator Dominique Mercy, who staged a revival of one of her early works.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["dance:","dance;","dance,","dance"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The list items contain internal commas, so semicolons must separate them, as after "contemporary dance." A semicolon is needed before "and Pina Bausch's former collaborator." A comma would blur the boundary between items, and a colon cannot separate list items.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h302$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In 1950, the poet ______ became the first Black author to win a Pulitzer Prize, for <i>Annie Allen</i>, a collection that follows a girl's coming-of-age in Chicago's Bronzeville neighborhood.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$[", Gwendolyn Brooks,","Gwendolyn Brooks,",", Gwendolyn Brooks","Gwendolyn Brooks"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"The poet Gwendolyn Brooks" is a restrictive appositive: the name identifies which poet is meant and is essential, so no commas should set it off. Adding commas treats the name as extra information and also separates the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h303$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Bioluminescent dinoflagellates glow when the water around them is disturbed. Researchers long assumed the light served no purpose. Recent experiments ______ suggest that the flashes startle the small crustaceans that feed on the plankton, causing them to release their prey.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["by Maya Iyer's team, however","by Maya Iyer's team, however,","by Maya Iyer's team however,","by Maya Iyer's team; however,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"However" is an interrupting transition placed between the subject ("Recent experiments by Maya Iyer's team") and the verb ("suggest"), so it must be set off by a pair of commas. Omitting either comma leaves it half-enclosed, and a semicolon would split the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h304$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Cartographer Kenji Moriyama's 1998 atlas of Tokyo's ______ that the city's postwar street grid followed the paths of rivers that had long since been paved over, an argument now widely accepted by urban historians.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["waterways argued","waterways, argued","waterways—argued","waterways; argued"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Cartographer Kenji Moriyama's 1998 atlas of Tokyo's waterways" is the subject and "argued" is its verb. No punctuation should separate a subject from its verb, so commas, semicolons, and dashes are all incorrect here.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h305$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$For centuries, the bristlecone pines of California's White Mountains have survived conditions that would kill most trees ______ thin soil, high winds, and a growing season that can last as little as six weeks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["outright:","outright, such as:","outright; including","outright, these include"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The independent clause ends with "outright," and a list explains the conditions, so a colon is correct. A colon should not follow "such as," a semicolon cannot introduce a list, and "these include" creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h306$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The documentary ______ a two-year effort to reintroduce beavers to a degraded English river valley—won the festival's top prize, surprising critics who had expected a political drama to take the award.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["<i>Return of the Engineers</i>, which chronicles","<i>Return of the Engineers</i>—which chronicles","<i>Return of the Engineers</i>: which chronicles","<i>Return of the Engineers</i> which chronicles"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The nonrestrictive clause "which chronicles a two-year effort..." closes with a dash before "won," so it must open with a dash too. Mixing a comma and a dash, or a colon and a dash, makes an unmatched pair, and no punctuation leaves the clause unbalanced.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h307$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Most hummingbirds can enter a state called torpor on cold nights, lowering their body temperature and heart rate to conserve ______ some Andean species allow their body temperature to drop to just 3.3°C, the lowest recorded for any bird.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["energy. In extreme cases,","energy, in extreme cases;","energy, in extreme cases,","energy in extreme cases,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses meet here. A period correctly separates them, with "In extreme cases" introducing the second. A comma creates a comma splice, omitting punctuation creates a run-on, and the semicolon after "cases" splits the second clause in the wrong place.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h308$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In her memoir, the astronaut describes the first time she saw Earth from orbit. She had expected to feel ______ she felt an overwhelming protectiveness toward the thin blue line of atmosphere that separated the planet from the void.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["awe instead","awe, instead;","awe, instead,","awe; instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses are linked by the conjunctive adverb "instead." A semicolon goes before "instead" and a comma after it. A comma before "instead" creates a comma splice. The other two choices place the boundary after "instead" or omit punctuation altogether.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h309$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Why do some languages have dozens of words for kinship relations while others make do with only a handful? Anthropologist Teresa Ochoa thinks the answer lies in inheritance ______ the more complex a society's rules for passing down land, she argues, the more precisely its language distinguishes among relatives.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["customs","customs?","customs:","customs, which"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second part explains the claim that the answer lies in inheritance customs, so a colon fits. A question mark is wrong because this sentence is a statement, not a question. No punctuation creates a run-on, and "which" produces a relative clause that does not fit grammatically with the rest of the sentence.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h310$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The tallest known living tree, a coast redwood named ______ stands more than 115 meters high, yet its exact location in California's Redwood National Park is kept secret to protect it from visitors.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Hyperion;","Hyperion","Hyperion,","Hyperion—"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The appositive "a coast redwood named Hyperion" opens with a comma after "tree," so it must close with a comma before the verb "stands." Without punctuation, the appositive is left unclosed. A semicolon separates subject and verb, and a dash doesn't match the opening comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h311$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Ethnomusicologist Daniel Asante recorded more than four hundred work songs sung by fishermen along Ghana's ______ meant to coordinate the pull of the nets, the songs also preserve local history, naming storms, shipwrecks, and record catches from generations past.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["coast though","coast. Though","coast, though","coast; though,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The first sentence ends after "coast." The next begins with the dependent clause "Though meant to coordinate the pull of the nets," followed by the main clause "the songs also preserve local history." A comma or no punctuation would attach the dependent clause to the wrong sentence and create a run-on, and a semicolon with a comma after "though" breaks up the dependent clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h312$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The Svalbard Global Seed Vault, built into a mountainside on a remote Norwegian ______ seeds from nearly every country on Earth as a safeguard against the loss of crop diversity.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["island, stores","island: stores","island; stores","island stores"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The appositive phrase "built into a mountainside on a remote Norwegian island" opens with a comma after "Vault" and must close with a comma before the verb "stores." The other choices either leave the phrase unclosed or separate the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h313$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Historian Claire Ashworth's account of the 1834 fire that destroyed the old Palace of Westminster draws on an unusual ______ eyewitness sketches made by artists, including J. M. W. Turner, who rushed to the riverbank to record the blaze.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["source, it is","source:","source, being","source;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The clause ending with "unusual source" is independent, and the phrase that follows specifies the source, so a colon is correct. A semicolon would need an independent clause after it, "it is" creates a comma splice, and "being" creates an awkward, ungrammatical construction.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h314$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Inside the Hagia Sophia, the play of light from the forty windows that ring the base of the dome ______ the illusion that the dome floats, unsupported, above the nave.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are creating","create","have created","creates"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "the play," which is singular. "From the forty windows that ring the base of the dome" is a long intervening phrase. The singular verb "creates" agrees with "play." The other choices are plural forms that agree with "windows" instead.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h315$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The committee reviewing proposals for the new public library announced that ______ would delay its final decision until residents had a chance to comment on the three finalist designs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they","one","we","it"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The pronoun refers to "the committee," a singular collective noun. The text confirms this with "its final decision," so "it" is correct. "They" mismatches "its," and "we" and "one" don't refer to the committee.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h316$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$First discovered in 1984, the Burgess Shale-type fossil site in China's Yunnan Province, known as Chengjiang, ______ preserved soft-bodied animals in such detail that researchers can trace their nervous systems.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["to have","having","has","have"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "the ... fossil site," which is singular, so the singular finite verb "has" is needed. "Have" is plural, and the nonfinite forms "having" and "to have" leave the sentence without a main verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h317$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The researchers' analysis of tree-ring data from more than four hundred sites ______ that the megadrought afflicting the American Southwest since 2000 is the region's worst in at least 1,200 years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["indicates","indicating","which indicates","to indicate"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The sentence needs a main verb for the subject "analysis." "Indicates" is a singular finite verb. "Indicating" and "to indicate" are nonfinite, and "which indicates" turns the rest into a relative clause, leaving no main verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h318$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$A technique ______ by Polynesian navigators for centuries, wave piloting involves reading the swells that rebound off distant islands to determine where land lies beyond the horizon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["uses","that used","used","using"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The opening phrase describes the technique, which was used by navigators, so a passive past participle is needed: "A technique used by Polynesian navigators." "Using" and "that used" make the technique do the using, and "uses" creates a second main verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h319$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Whereas earlier biographers portrayed the inventor as a solitary genius, Okonjo's new biography emphasizes the workshop assistants, investors, and rival engineers whose contributions ______ his famous designs possible.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["making","made","to make","having made"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The relative clause "whose contributions ______ his famous designs possible" needs a finite verb with "contributions" as its subject. "Made" provides one. "Making," "to make," and "having made" are nonfinite and leave the clause incomplete.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h320$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Although mosses lack the vascular tissue that carries water through most land plants, ______ able to colonize bare rock, where they slowly build up the soil that later allows other plants to take root.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they being","they are","which are","it is"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The main clause needs a subject and finite verb referring to "mosses," a plural noun. "They are" provides both. "It is" is singular, "they being" is nonfinite, and "which are" makes the main clause a relative clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h321$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The orchestra's principal cellist, along with two violinists who joined the ensemble last season, ______ to perform the quartet at the composer's memorial concert.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are invited","were invited","was invited","have been invited"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is the singular "cellist." The phrase "along with two violinists..." doesn't make the subject plural, so the singular "was invited" is correct. The other choices are plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h322$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Archaeologists working at Göbekli Tepe believe that the site's massive carved pillars ______ erected by hunter-gatherers roughly 11,000 years ago, before the advent of farming in the region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","has been","were","was"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "pillars," which is plural, and the action happened in the distant past, so "were" is correct. "Was," "is," and "has been" are singular forms that agree with "site" instead.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h323$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Of all the explanations that have been proposed for the extinction of the woolly mammoth, the one that most researchers now favor ______ climate change with hunting pressure from humans.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are combining","combines","combine","have combined"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "the one," which is singular. "Researchers" is the subject of the relative clause "that most researchers now favor." The singular "combines" agrees with "one."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h324$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Novelist Yaa Gyasi structures <i>Homegoing</i> as a series of linked stories, each following a descendant of one of two half-sisters, so that readers ______ the consequences of a single eighteenth-century choice across three hundred years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["tracing","trace","has traced","traces"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the "so that" clause, the subject is "readers," a plural noun, and the context is a general present. "Trace" agrees in number and tense. "Traces" and "has traced" are singular, and "tracing" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h325$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The glassblowers of Murano, an island in the Venetian lagoon, were once forbidden to leave the Republic of ______ their techniques were considered state secrets, and artisans who fled risked severe punishment.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Venice","Venice, and,","Venice,","Venice;"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses meet at the blank, and they are closely related: the second explains the prohibition. A semicolon joins them correctly. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and a comma after "and" wrongly separates the conjunction from its clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h326$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Physicist Lise Meitner, working with her nephew Otto Frisch during the winter of ______ the first theoretical explanation of nuclear fission, a discovery for which her longtime collaborator Otto Hahn alone received the Nobel Prize.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1938–39—provided","1938–39; provided","1938–39 provided","1938–39, provided"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The participial phrase "working with her nephew Otto Frisch during the winter of 1938–39" begins with a comma after "Meitner" and must end with one before the verb "provided." Omitting the comma, or using a semicolon or dash, breaks the matched pair or separates subject from verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h327$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The software flagged hundreds of possible ______ a team of trained volunteers then reviewed each one by hand, discarding the false alarms caused by passing satellites and atmospheric glare.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["meteors, and,","meteors and,","meteors and","meteors, and"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses joined by "and" need a comma before the conjunction. No comma after "and" is needed, because "then" is integrated into the clause. The choice without a comma creates a run-on, and the ones with a comma after "and" are incorrect.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h328$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Grandmaster Judit Polgár, who in 2005 became the only woman ever to compete for the overall world chess championship, rarely entered women's-only ______ preferring to test herself against the strongest players regardless of gender.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["events,","events;","events","events:"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The participial phrase "preferring to test herself..." modifies Polgár and needs a comma to separate it from the main clause. A semicolon or colon would make the phrase stand alone as a fragment, and no punctuation runs the modifier into "events."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h329$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Some of the earliest known musical instruments are flutes carved from the wing bones of vultures and the tusks of ______ found in caves in southern Germany, they are roughly 40,000 years old.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["mammoths discovered","mammoths. Discovered","mammoths, discovered","mammoths; discovered,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The first sentence ends after "mammoths." The second begins with the participial phrase "Discovered in caves in southern Germany," which modifies "they." A comma or no punctuation would attach the phrase to the wrong sentence and create a comma splice before "they." A semicolon would leave the phrase stranded as a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h330$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The phrase "the medium is the message" is often quoted but less often ______ McLuhan meant that the form of a communication technology shapes society more profoundly than any particular content it carries.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["understood Marshall","understood: Marshall","understood, Marshall","understood: and Marshall"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second independent clause explains what the phrase actually means, so a colon is appropriate. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and" after a colon is ungrammatical.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h331$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Many of the lizard species living in Chile's Atacama Desert are found nowhere else on Earth, a pattern that biologist Rafael ______ is the product of millions of years of isolation between the desert's scattered oases.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Ibarra; believes","Ibarra believes","Ibarra believes,","Ibarra, believes"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the relative clause "that biologist Rafael Ibarra believes is the product...," "biologist Rafael Ibarra" is the subject and "believes" is the verb. No punctuation belongs between them or after "believes," which would cut the verb off from what follows.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h332$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Historian Nell Painter's biography of Sojourner Truth separates the documented facts of Truth's life from the legends that grew up around ______ famous "Ar'n't I a Woman?" speech, for example, was reconstructed by a white writer more than a decade after it was delivered.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["her; the","her, the","her the","her, for the"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, and a semicolon correctly joins them. A comma alone creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "for" combined with the later "for example" produces a confusing, redundant structure.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h333$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Each object in the exhibit was chosen by a different resident of the ______ a grandmother's sewing machine, a high school trophy, a hand-painted sign from a shuttered diner.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["neighborhood;","neighborhood:","neighborhood, such as:","neighborhood, and"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$A colon correctly introduces examples after an independent clause. A semicolon can't introduce a list, a colon should not follow "such as," and "and" would create a confusing structure in which the list seems to be a second subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h334$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$When biologist Rachel Carson published <i>Silent Spring</i> in 1962, the chemical industry attacked both the book and its ______ one company threatened to sue her publisher, and industry spokespeople questioned her credentials.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["author, which","author","author, and","author:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The second part gives specific examples of how the industry attacked Carson, so a colon introducing the elaboration is correct. No punctuation creates a run-on, "and" with a comma creates a list that confusingly runs into another "and," and "which" creates a relative clause that does not fit grammatically.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h335$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The Andean condor can soar for more than five hours without flapping its ______ it rides thermals and wind deflected upward by mountain slopes, flapping for only about one percent of its flight time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["wings, instead,","wings instead,","wings; instead","wings; instead,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Instead" is a conjunctive adverb linking two independent clauses. A semicolon goes before it and a comma after it. A comma before "instead" creates a comma splice, and omitting the comma after "instead" is nonstandard when it introduces a clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h336$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, linguists estimate that nearly half are endangered. The causes vary from region to ______ in nearly every case, the decline begins when parents stop speaking the language to their children.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["region but","region; but,","region, but,","region, but"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses joined by "but" need a comma before the conjunction. No comma follows "but" because "in nearly every case" already has its own comma after it. The other choices omit the needed comma or add one after "but."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h337$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The data collected by the two rovers, which landed on opposite sides of the planet in 2004, ______ that liquid water once flowed across large areas of the Martian surface.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["shows","show","showing","has shown"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Data" is plural in formal usage, and here it is modified by "collected by the two rovers," so the plural "show" is correct. "Shows" and "has shown" are singular, and "showing" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h338$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The tale of the Trojan Horse, which appears only briefly in the <i>Odyssey</i>, ______ by later poets into one of the most famous episodes in Greek myth.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were expanded","was expanded","expanding","have been expanded"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "the tale," which is singular, so "was expanded" agrees. "Were expanded" and "have been expanded" are plural, and "expanding" leaves the sentence without a finite verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h339$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$When the city first installed the sensors, engineers worried that the devices would not survive the harsh winters. Five years later, every one of the original sensors ______ still transmitting data.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","is","were","are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject "every one" is singular, and "five years later" with "still" points to the present. "Is" is correct. "Are," "were," and "have been" are plural forms that agree with "sensors."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h340$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Rather than ______ the experiment's surprising results to contamination, the chemists repeated the procedure in a different laboratory with newly purchased reagents.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had attributed","attributed","attribute","attributes"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$After "rather than" in a sentence whose main verb is "repeated," the parallel form is the base form: "Rather than attribute ..., the chemists repeated." "Attributes" doesn't fit the construction, and "attributed" and "had attributed" would make the sentence say the chemists did both.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h341$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Unlike the paintings of her contemporaries, which depicted rural landscapes in muted tones, ______ vivid colors drawn from folk art and pre-Columbian sculpture.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["painter Rosa Ilán's canvases featured","there were in painter Rosa Ilán's canvases","painter Rosa Ilán featured","the canvases painter Rosa Ilán made, featuring"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The comparison begins with "the paintings of her contemporaries," so it must be completed with paintings, not a person. "Painter Rosa Ilán's canvases featured" compares paintings with paintings and supplies a main verb. The choice beginning "painter Rosa Ilán featured" compares paintings with a person, the choice beginning "there were in painter…" uses an awkward expletive, and the choice beginning "the canvases painter Rosa…" lacks a main verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h342$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Before the invention of refrigeration, the ice harvested from New England lakes each winter ______ packed in sawdust and shipped as far as Calcutta, where it sold for a handsome profit.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","were","was","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Ice" is a singular noncount noun and the context is past, so "was" is correct. "Lakes" is part of the prepositional phrase and does not control the verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h343$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The dancers in the troupe trained for months to master the ______ a series of leaps so precisely timed that a single misstep would throw off the entire ensemble.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["finales,","finale's,","finale,","finales',"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The context refers to one finale, and no possession is indicated, so the singular "finale" followed by a comma introducing the appositive is correct. The possessive forms "finale's" and "finales'" have nothing to possess, and "finales" is plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h344$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Several of the region's older farmers, whose families have tended the same terraced fields for generations, ______ that the new irrigation system has lowered the water table in nearby wells.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["report","reporting","has reported","reports"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject "several" refers to the farmers and is plural, so "report" is correct. "Reports" and "has reported" are singular, and "reporting" leaves the sentence without a main verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h345$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$In the nineteenth century, the city's harbor was so crowded with ships that sailors claimed one could walk from one side to the other across their decks. Today, only a few ferries and pleasure boats ______ the water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["cross","crossed","crosses","had crossed"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Today" signals the present tense, and the subject is "a few ferries and pleasure boats," which is plural. "Cross" is correct. "Crosses" is singular, and "crossed" and "had crossed" are past forms that conflict with "today."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h346$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Every one of the mathematician's notebooks, now housed at the university archive, ______ annotated in at least three colors of ink, suggesting that she returned to her work repeatedly over many years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","are","is","have been"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "every one," which is singular, and "now housed" points to the present. "Is" agrees. The plural verbs agree only with "notebooks," which sits in an intervening prepositional phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h347$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Climatologist Imani Tate found that the cities with the most tree cover were, on average, five degrees cooler on summer afternoons than ______ the least.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they with","those with","the cities","that with"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The comparison is between cities with the most tree cover and cities with the least. "Those with" refers back to "cities" and repeats "with" for parallelism. "The cities the least" omits the needed preposition, "they with" uses the wrong pronoun form, and "that" is singular.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h348$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The replica Polynesian voyaging canoe <i>Hōkūleʻa</i>'s 1976 trip from Hawaii to Tahiti, navigated without instruments, ______ skeptics who had argued that Pacific islands were settled only by accidental drift.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["refute","have refuted","refuted","refuting"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "trip," which is singular, and the event is in the past, so "refuted" is correct. "Refute" and "have refuted" are plural, and "refuting" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h349$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The town's oldest building is not its church or its city hall but a modest stone granary built around 1640 by the first ______ survived three fires, two floods, and a period in the 1970s when it was used as a bowling alley.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["settlers, having","settlers it has","settlers. It has","settlers, it has"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, so a period correctly separates them. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "having" would make the final part a modifier that seems to describe the settlers rather than the granary.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h350$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Chemist Paulina Reyes designs molecules that change shape when struck by light. In her most recent ______ a strand of polymer curls into a tight coil under blue light and straightens again under green, allowing it to act as a microscopic switch.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["work,","work;","work:","work"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"In her most recent work" is an introductory prepositional phrase, followed by a comma before the main clause. A semicolon or colon would leave the phrase stranded as a fragment, and without punctuation "work a strand" runs together confusingly.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h351$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Medieval Islamic scholars preserved and extended much of ancient Greek mathematics. The algebra of al-Khwarizmi, for ______ problems that Greek geometers had approached only through diagrams.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["instance systematically solved","instance, systematically solved","instance; systematically solved","instance—systematically solved"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"For instance" interrupts the subject "The algebra of al-Khwarizmi" and its verb "solved," so it is set off by a pair of commas. The text supplies the first comma, so a second is required after "instance." Omitting it, or using a semicolon or dash, breaks the pair.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h352$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Some ant colonies function as a single superorganism, with thousands of workers coordinating their behavior through chemical ______ no individual ant directs the colony, its collective decisions are often remarkably efficient.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["signals although","signals; although,","signals, although","signals. Although"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The first sentence ends after "signals." The second begins with the dependent clause "Although no individual ant directs the colony," followed by the main clause. A comma or no punctuation would attach the dependent clause to the first sentence and create a comma splice, and "although," with a comma after it splits the dependent clause incorrectly.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h353$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The report recommended three changes to the city's flood defenses: raising the seawall by one ______ restoring the salt marshes that once absorbed storm surges, which had been filled in for development; and installing pumps beneath the lowest-lying streets.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["meter,","meter;","meter","meter:"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Because the second list item contains an internal comma (before "which had been filled in"), the items must be separated by semicolons, as before "and installing." A semicolon is needed after "meter." A comma would blur the item boundaries, and a colon cannot separate list items.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h354$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The novel's narrator never reveals her name. Readers learn only what she chooses to tell ______ that she grew up near the sea, that she once had a brother, and that she has not spoken to him in twenty years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["them, it is","them;","them, and","them:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A colon introduces the list of facts that elaborates on "only what she chooses to tell them." A semicolon can't introduce a list, "and" with a comma creates an ungrammatical structure, and "it is" creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h355$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Instead of pouring concrete, the engineers stabilized the hillside with a lattice of deep-rooted native ______ which they planted in a pattern modeled on the root networks of the original forest.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["shrubs,","shrubs","shrubs.","shrubs;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The relative clause "which they planted..." is nonrestrictive and must be set off by a comma. A semicolon or period would turn the clause into a fragment, and no punctuation is incorrect before a nonrestrictive "which" clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h356$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Sculptor Ruth Asawa learned to make her looped-wire forms during a 1947 trip to ______ she watched artisans weave wire baskets for carrying eggs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Mexico; where","Mexico, where","Mexico where,","Mexico. Where"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Where she watched artisans weave wire baskets" is a dependent clause describing Mexico, so it's attached with a comma. A semicolon or period would leave it as a fragment, and a comma after "where" splits the clause from its subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h357$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The study's authors are careful to note an important ______ although the participants who slept more scored higher on the memory test, the experiment cannot show that extra sleep caused the improvement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["limitation","limitation, that","limitation,","limitation:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause "The study's authors are careful to note an important limitation" is followed by an explanation of that limitation, so a colon is correct. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "that" with a comma produces an ungrammatical structure.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h358$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$After decades of decline, the population of Atlantic puffins on the Norwegian island of Skarvøy has begun to ______ researchers attribute the turnaround to a ban on commercial fishing for the small sand eels that the puffins feed their chicks.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["recover and","recover, and,","recover, and","recover; and,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses joined by "and" need a comma before the conjunction. The choice without a comma creates a run-on, and placing a comma after "and" separates the conjunction from its clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h359$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Unlike most bats, which navigate by ______ the Egyptian fruit bat produces its sonar clicks with its tongue, a method that researchers once dismissed as crude.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["echolocation calls, made in the larynx","echolocation calls made in the larynx","echolocation calls made in the larynx;","echolocation calls made in the larynx,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The relative clause "which navigate by echolocation calls made in the larynx" opens with a comma after "bats" and must close with one before the main clause. A semicolon creates a fragment, the comma before "made" leaves the clause unclosed, and no punctuation runs the clause into the main subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h360$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Before the Brooklyn Bridge opened in 1883, the only way to cross the East River was by ______ on stormy days, the ferries sometimes stopped running altogether, cutting Brooklyn off from Manhattan.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["ferry; and,","ferry and","ferry, and,","ferry, and"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses joined by "and" need a comma before the conjunction. The introductory phrase "on stormy days" has its own comma, so no comma follows "and." The other choices omit the needed comma or add an extra one after "and."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h361$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The number of honeybee colonies lost each winter in the United States ______ risen sharply since the mid-2000s, prompting researchers to investigate pesticides, parasites, and poor nutrition as possible causes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","has","are","having"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "the number," which is singular, so "has" is correct. "Colonies" and "the United States" sit in intervening phrases. "Have" and "are" are plural, and "having" leaves the sentence without a finite verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h362$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Investigators discovered that the bridge's collapse was caused not by a single catastrophic failure but by dozens of small cracks that ______ for years, unnoticed, in the steel beneath the roadway.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will spread","had spread","spread","have spread"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The cracks' spreading happened over years before the collapse and its investigation, both past events, so the past perfect "had spread" is correct. The simple past "spread" doesn't mark the earlier time as clearly, and the present perfect and future don't fit the past time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h363$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither of the two proposed sites for the new observatory ______ ideal: one has clear skies but suffers from strong winds, while the other is sheltered but often cloudy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","is","are","were"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Neither" as the subject pronoun is singular, and the context is present, so "is" agrees. "Are" and "have been" are plural, and "were" is plural and past tense.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h364$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Today the textile mill is a museum, but in its prime it employed more than two thousand workers, most of whom ______ in boardinghouses owned by the company.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have lived","living","live","lived"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The clause describes the mill's past ("in its prime"), so the past tense "lived" is correct. "Live" and "have lived" don't fit the past time frame, and "living" leaves the relative clause without a finite verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h365$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$In the 1960s, the linguist ______ that all human languages share an underlying grammar, a proposal that has since been challenged by researchers studying languages with radically different structures.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Noam Chomsky, influentially arguing","Noam Chomsky's influential argument","Noam Chomsky influentially argued","Noam Chomsky, who influentially argued"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence needs a subject and finite verb. "Noam Chomsky influentially argued" supplies both. The other choices leave the sentence without a main verb, using a participle, a noun phrase, or a relative clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h366$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The museum's two newest acquisitions, a twelfth-century astrolabe and a hand-drawn map of the Silk Road, ______ to the collection by the descendants of a nineteenth-century diplomat.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was donated","has been donated","is donated","were donated"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "acquisitions," which is plural, and the appositive names two items. "Were donated" agrees in number and fits the past context. The other choices are singular.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h367$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Researchers studying the octopus have found that each of its eight arms ______ its own cluster of neurons, which allows the arm to respond to touch even when it has been severed from the body.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["contain","have contained","contains","are containing"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject "each" is singular, so "contains" is correct. "Arms" belongs to the prepositional phrase "of its eight arms" and doesn't control the verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h368$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Both the original 1925 manuscript and the revised edition published a decade later ______ the same opening line, though the endings differ dramatically.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["share","has shared","shares","is sharing"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A compound subject joined by "both ... and" is plural, so "share" is correct. "Shares," "is sharing," and "has shared" are singular forms.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h369$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Glaciologists drilling into the Antarctic ice sheet have retrieved cores whose layers ______ a continuous record of Earth's atmosphere over the past 800,000 years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has preserved","preserving","preserves","preserve"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$In the relative clause "whose layers preserve...," the subject is "layers," which is plural. "Preserve" agrees. "Preserves" and "has preserved" are singular, and "preserving" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h370$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Among the photographs in the exhibit is a portrait of the photographer's mother, ______ hands, folded in her lap, are the only part of the image in sharp focus.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["whose","their","her","who's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The blank introduces a relative clause describing the mother's hands, so the possessive relative pronoun "whose" is needed. "Who's" means "who is," "her" creates a comma splice, and "their" is plural and also creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h371$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$According to the authors of the study, a diet rich in fiber helps the beneficial bacteria in the human gut, which ferment fiber into compounds that reduce inflammation, ______.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["thrives","thrive","thriving","to thriving"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$After "helps," the object "the beneficial bacteria" takes the base form of the verb: "helps the bacteria thrive." The intervening relative clause doesn't change this. "Thrives" is finite, and "thriving" and "to thriving" are unidiomatic after "helps."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h372$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The three finalists for the architecture prize ______ a library in Dakar built from compressed earth, a ferry terminal in Busan, and a housing cooperative in Zurich.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","were:","were—","were;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"The three finalists ... were" is not a complete independent clause on its own; the list is the complement of the verb "were." A colon, semicolon, or dash would separate the verb from its complement, so no punctuation is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h373$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The poet described her first collection as ______ a way of mapping the neighborhood she grew up in, one street at a time.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["\"a kind of atlas,\"","\"a kind of atlas\":","\"a kind of atlas\",","\"a kind of atlas;\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase "a way of mapping the neighborhood" renames the quoted description, so a comma is needed after the quotation. In American English, the comma goes inside the closing quotation mark. A colon or semicolon would require an independent clause before the following phrase or create a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h374$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$For most of the twentieth century, historians treated the Silk Road as a single ______ in fact, it was a shifting web of routes, and few traders ever traveled more than a small segment of it.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["highway, but,","highway,","highway","highway;"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, joined by the transitional phrase "in fact." A semicolon before the phrase is correct. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "but," with a comma after it misplaces the comma.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h375$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The documentary follows the restoration of a 1930s Art Deco cinema in ______ old seats were reupholstered by hand, and its neon sign, dark since 1987, was rewired piece by piece.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Mumbai, and, its","Mumbai its","Mumbai, its","Mumbai. Its"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses meet here (the second begins "Its old seats were reupholstered"). A period separates them correctly. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the comma after "and" is misplaced.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h376$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Fewer than four hundred North Atlantic right whales survive ______ entanglement in fishing gear and collisions with ships are the leading causes of death among them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["today,","today;","today, and,","today"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, and a semicolon joins them. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and," with a trailing comma is incorrectly punctuated.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h377$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Physician and writer Atul ______ argues that checklists, long used by airline pilots, can dramatically reduce errors in surgery.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Gawande, in his 2009 book <i>The Checklist Manifesto</i>,","Gawande; in his 2009 book <i>The Checklist Manifesto</i>,","Gawande in his 2009 book <i>The Checklist Manifesto</i>,","Gawande, in his 2009 book <i>The Checklist Manifesto</i>"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The phrase "in his 2009 book The Checklist Manifesto" interrupts the subject and verb, so it must be enclosed by a pair of commas. The other choices omit one comma or use a semicolon, which would separate the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h378$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Some deep-sea fish have eyes that point permanently ______ scanning the faint glow from the surface for the silhouettes of prey swimming above them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["upward:","upward;","upward.","upward,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Scanning the faint glow..." is a participial phrase modifying the eyes or fish, so a comma attaches it to the main clause. A semicolon or period leaves it as a fragment, and a colon would wrongly signal an explanation or list.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h379$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Two features distinguish the new vaccine from earlier ______ remains stable at room temperature for months, and it can be administered as a nasal spray rather than an injection.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["versions: and it","versions: it","versions it","versions, it"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second part explains the "two features," so a colon is correct. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and" after a colon is ungrammatical.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h380$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ruins of the city, which flourished between 900 and 1200 ______ were covered by forest for centuries until lidar surveys revealed the full extent of its streets, plazas, and reservoirs.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["CE","CE;","CE—","CE,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The relative clause "which flourished between 900 and 1200 CE" opens with a comma after "city" and must close with one before the verb "were covered." A semicolon separates subject from verb, a dash mismatches the opening comma, and no punctuation leaves the clause unclosed.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h381$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$"The question is not whether the coastline will ______ the planner told the council, "but how much of it we are willing to let go."$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["move\",","move\"","move;\"","move,\""]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$A quotation interrupted by a dialogue tag needs a comma before the closing quotation mark, since the sentence continues after "told the council." In American English, the comma goes inside the quotation marks. The other choices omit the comma, put it outside, or use a semicolon.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h382$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The Hubble Space Telescope's famous Deep Field image, assembled from 342 exposures taken over ten consecutive days in ______ revealed some three thousand galaxies in a patch of sky covering about one twenty-four-millionth of the whole sky.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1995,","1995","1995—","1995;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The participial phrase "assembled from 342 exposures ... in 1995" opens with a comma after "image" and must close with one before the verb "revealed." Omitting it or using a semicolon or dash breaks the matched pair.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h383$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Since the city adopted its bike-share program in 2016, the number of residents who commute by bicycle ______ nearly tripled.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had","having","have","has"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Since 2016" describes something continuing to the present, which calls for the present perfect. The subject "the number" is singular, so "has" is correct. "Have" is plural, "had" is past perfect, and "having" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h384$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The archive holds hundreds of letters exchanged between the two ______ correspondence spans more than forty years and reveals a friendship that survived bitter professional rivalry.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["scientists, whose","scientists, who's","scientist's, whose","scientists', whose"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Scientists" is a plural noun with no possession at this point, and "whose" is the possessive relative pronoun referring to them. "Scientist's" and "scientists'" are possessives with nothing to possess, and "who's" means "who is."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h385$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Walking through the reconstructed Viking longhouse, ______ by how little light entered through the smoke hole in the roof.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["it is often striking to visitors","the dimness often strikes visitors","visitors are often struck","visitors' impression is often one of being struck"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory phrase "Walking through the reconstructed Viking longhouse" must be followed by the people who walk, so "visitors" must come right after the comma. The other choices make "the dimness," "it," or "visitors' impression" the walker.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h386$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The spices that Portuguese traders brought back from the Malabar Coast in the sixteenth century ______ worth more, by weight, than silver in some European markets.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been","is","were","was"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "spices," which is plural, and the context is the sixteenth century, so "were" is correct. The other choices are singular, and "is" and "has been" also clash with the past time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h387$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Researchers compared the songs of humpback whales recorded off Hawaii with ______ recorded off Mexico and found that the two populations shared nearly identical phrases.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["it","that","them","those"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The comparison is between songs and songs. "Those" is a plural pronoun that stands in for "the songs." "That" and "it" are singular, and "them" can't be modified by "recorded off Mexico" in this way.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h388$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The ancient Greek physician Galen, whose writings on anatomy dominated European medicine for more than a thousand years, ______ his conclusions largely on the dissection of monkeys and pigs rather than humans.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["basing","to base","based","have based"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The sentence needs a finite main verb for the subject "Galen" after the long relative clause. "Based" is a past-tense verb that fits. "Basing" and "to base" are nonfinite, and "have based" is plural and present perfect.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h389$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither the drought nor the subsequent wildfires ______ the oak savanna entirely; within a decade, seedlings had reestablished themselves across most of the burned area.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has destroyed","destroys","was destroying","destroyed"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$With "neither ... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "wildfires," which is plural. The past context ("within a decade ... had reestablished") calls for the past tense, so "destroyed" works for both. "Destroys," "has destroyed," and "was destroying" are singular.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h390$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The children's book, first published in 1963, has been translated into more than thirty languages, and ______ illustrations have influenced generations of artists.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["its","their","it's","they're"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The possessive refers to "the children's book," which is singular, so "its" is correct. "It's" is a contraction, "their" is plural, and "they're" is a contraction.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h391$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$To the surprise of the excavation team, the shards of pottery found at the lowest level of the site ______ older than any previously discovered in the region.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","has been","were","is"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "shards," which is plural, and the discovery is in the past, so "were" is correct. "Pottery," "level," and "site" are in intervening phrases.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h392$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$By the end of this decade, engineers predict, the new desalination plant ______ enough fresh water to supply nearly a third of the city's residents.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has been producing","will be producing","had produced","produced"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"By the end of this decade" refers to a future time, so the future progressive "will be producing" fits. "Produced" and "had produced" are past forms, and "has been producing" suggests the plant is already running.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h393$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The bird's ability to recognize individual human faces, which researchers demonstrated by having volunteers wear masks, ______ that crows possess a more sophisticated memory than was once believed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["suggests","have suggested","suggest","suggesting"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is "ability," which is singular, so "suggests" agrees. "Faces," "researchers," "volunteers," and "masks" are all in intervening phrases. "Suggest" and "have suggested" are plural, and "suggesting" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h394$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Mathematician Srinivasa Ramanujan filled his notebooks with thousands of results, many of ______ he stated without proof and which later mathematicians spent decades verifying.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["them","those","these","which"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Many of which" begins a relative clause that attaches to the main clause; "which" is also repeated later ("and which later mathematicians..."), so the structure must be parallel. "Them," "these," and "those" would create a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h395$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The bakery's sourdough starter has been kept alive continuously since ______ a fact the owners advertise on a hand-lettered sign above the counter.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1912.","1912,","1912","1912;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"A fact the owners advertise..." is a noun phrase that sums up the preceding clause (a summative appositive), and it is attached with a comma. A semicolon or period would leave it as a fragment, and no punctuation runs it into the date.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h396$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In 2019, the Event Horizon Telescope collaboration released the first image of a black ______ glowing ring of gas surrounding the dark shadow of the supermassive object at the center of galaxy M87.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["hole, it was a","hole: a","hole. A","hole; a"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The noun phrase "a glowing ring of gas surrounding the dark shadow..." describes the image and has no main verb, so it can't follow a semicolon or period. A colon correctly introduces it. "It was a" creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h397$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Psychologist Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi coined the term "flow" to describe a state of complete absorption in a challenging ______ time seems to disappear, and self-consciousness fades.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["task, in which","task; in which","task. In which","task in which,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"In which time seems to disappear..." is a relative clause modifying "task" (or "state") and is attached with a comma. A semicolon or period leaves it as a fragment, and a comma after "which" separates the relative pronoun from its clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h398$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ______ a carved wooden figure no taller than a pencil, had been misidentified as a child's toy until a curator noticed tiny hieroglyphs on its base.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["artifact,","artifact;","artifact:","artifact"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"A carved wooden figure no taller than a pencil" is a nonrestrictive appositive between the subject "artifact" and its verb "had been misidentified." It closes with a comma, so it must open with one. A colon or semicolon would separate the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h399$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The coastal town has seen its population fall by half since the cannery ______ the closure eliminated nearly eight hundred jobs, many of them held by families who had worked there for three generations.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["closed, in 1998;","closed in 1998,","closed in 1998;","closed in 1998"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses meet at the blank, and a semicolon joins them correctly. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the comma before "in 1998" splits the verb from its time phrase for no reason.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h400$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Like a fingerprint, the pattern of patches on each giraffe's coat is ______ biologists can use photographs to track individual animals across years without ever tagging them.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["unique, so","unique; so,","unique so","unique, so,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses joined by the coordinating conjunction "so" need a comma before it and none after it. The other choices omit the comma or misplace one after "so."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h401$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Volcanologist Hilda Árnadóttir's team placed seismometers on the slopes of ______ within weeks, the instruments detected a swarm of small earthquakes that preceded the peninsula's first eruption in eight hundred years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Fagradalsfjall;","Fagradalsfjall,","Fagradalsfjall","Fagradalsfjall, and,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, and a semicolon joins them correctly. The second begins with the introductory phrase "within weeks." A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "and," with a trailing comma is misplaced.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h402$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The playwright revised the ending of her drama ______ times before its premiere: in the first version, the sisters reconcile; in the second, one leaves for good; and in the third, the play ends before either can decide.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["three:","three;","three","three,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Three times" is a single adverbial phrase, so no punctuation belongs between "three" and "times." Any comma, colon, or semicolon would split the number from the noun it modifies.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h403$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Saffron is the most expensive spice in the world by ______ each flower produces only three stigmas, which must be picked by hand, and roughly 150,000 flowers are needed to yield a single kilogram.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["weight","weight:","weight, and","weight,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second part explains why saffron is so expensive, so a colon correctly introduces it. A comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on. "And" would make a confusing chain of three "and"-linked parts and hide the explanation.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h404$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Writing in the eleventh century, Murasaki ______ a lady-in-waiting at the Japanese imperial court, produced <i>The Tale of Genji</i>, often described as the world's first novel.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Shikibu,","Shikibu","Shikibu:","Shikibu;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The appositive "a lady-in-waiting at the Japanese imperial court" ends with a comma before "produced," so it must begin with a comma after the name. No punctuation leaves the appositive unopened, and a semicolon or colon would split the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h405$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$For years, radio astronomers in Australia puzzled over brief bursts of signal that seemed to come from deep ______ the bursts turned out to be produced by a microwave oven in the observatory's kitchen whenever someone opened its door too early.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["space, eventually,","space, eventually;","space eventually,","space. Eventually,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, so a period correctly separates them, with "Eventually" introducing the second. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and placing a semicolon after "eventually" makes the adverb dangle at the end of the first clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h406$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Each of the three bridges that span the river below the dam ______ designed to withstand a flood twice as large as any yet recorded.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have been","are","were","was"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject "each" is singular, so "was" is correct. "Bridges," "river," and "dam" belong to intervening phrases. The other choices are plural forms.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h407$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Tracing the river's ancient course through satellite imagery, ______ the remains of dozens of settlements that had been buried beneath desert sand for four thousand years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the remains were located by archaeologist Amira Sayed,","there were located by archaeologist Amira Sayed","archaeologist Amira Sayed located","archaeologist Amira Sayed's team's location was of"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The introductory phrase "Tracing the river's ancient course" must modify the person who traced it, so "archaeologist Amira Sayed" must come right after the comma. The other choices make "the remains," a "location," or "there" do the tracing.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h408$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The orchestra played the symphony exactly as the composer ______ it, using the smaller ensemble and period instruments that would have been available in 1790.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["intends","will intend","has intended","had intended"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The composer's intention came before the performance, which is narrated in the past ("played"). The past perfect "had intended" marks this earlier time. The present, present perfect, and future forms don't fit.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h409$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$A study of more than two thousand twins found that the tendency to procrastinate, like many personality traits, ______ partly heritable.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","is","have been","were"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "the tendency," which is singular, and the phrase "like many personality traits" is parenthetical. A general finding is stated in the present, so "is" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h410$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The hikers who reached the summit before noon were rewarded with a clear view of the valley; ______ arrived later saw only clouds.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["those which","those who","they who","them who"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second clause needs a subject referring to hikers. "Those who" is the standard form. "They who" is archaic, "which" is used for things, and "them" is an object pronoun that can't serve as a subject.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h411$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Historian Aiko Matsuda argues that the woodblock prints of the Edo period, often treated as fine art today, ______ originally mass-produced as inexpensive souvenirs and advertisements.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["was","are","is","were"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "prints," which is plural, and "originally" points to the past, so "were" is correct. "Was" is singular, and "are" and "is" are present tense.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h412$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Neither the playwright's diaries nor her letters to her publisher ______ any mention of the manuscript that scholars now consider her masterpiece.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["contain","is containing","contains","has contained"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With "neither ... nor," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "letters," which is plural. "Contain" agrees. The other choices are singular.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h413$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Unlike frogs, whose eggs must be laid in water or moist places, ______ eggs with leathery shells that retain moisture, allowing them to breed far from water.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["reptiles, laying","reptiles' laying of","reptiles lay","the eggs of reptiles have"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The comparison begins with "frogs," so it must be completed with animals, and the sentence needs a main verb. "Reptiles lay" does both. "Reptiles, laying" has no main verb, "the eggs of reptiles" compares frogs with eggs, and "reptiles' laying of" lacks a verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h414$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The scientist's notebooks reveal that she ______ the structure of the molecule weeks before her rivals announced their own discovery.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["had deduced","has deduced","will deduce","deduces"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The deduction happened before another past event, the rivals' announcement, so the past perfect "had deduced" is correct. The present, present perfect, and future forms don't fit the past narrative.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h415$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Along the narrow ridge that separates the two valleys ______ a line of ancient stone markers, each carved with symbols that scholars have yet to decipher.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have run","runs","run","are running"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$This sentence is inverted: the subject, "a line," comes after the verb, and it's singular. "Runs" agrees. "Markers" belongs to the prepositional phrase "of ancient stone markers."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h416$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Engineers at the company have designed a prosthetic hand whose fingers ______ to the wearer's muscle signals within a few milliseconds, allowing for remarkably natural movement.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["responds","responding","respond","has responded"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In the relative clause "whose fingers respond...," the subject is "fingers," which is plural, so "respond" agrees. "Responds" and "has responded" are singular, and "responding" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h417$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Though separated by more than a thousand kilometers, the two populations of wolves ______ nearly identical howling patterns, a finding that surprised the researchers.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["share","sharing","shares","has shared"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is "populations," which is plural, so "share" agrees. "Shares" and "has shared" are singular, and "sharing" leaves the sentence without a main verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h418$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mural covers all four walls of the old train ______ panel depicts a different decade of the town's history, beginning with the arrival of the railroad in 1871.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["station, and, each","station; each","station each","station, each"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, and a semicolon joins them correctly. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the comma after "and" is misplaced.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h419$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Entomologist Farah Haddad has spent a decade studying a single question about monarch ______ do the butterflies that migrate to Mexico navigate by the sun alone, or do they also sense Earth's magnetic field?$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["butterflies,","butterflies:","butterflies","butterflies;"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause is followed by the question it announces, so a colon is correct. A semicolon would wrongly join a statement and a question as parallel clauses, a comma creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h420$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The bridge's designers faced an unusual ______ the river below it freezes solid for five months each year, and the expanding ice can exert enormous pressure on any pillars set in the riverbed.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["challenge, that","challenge","challenge:","challenge,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The second part explains the "unusual challenge," so a colon is correct. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and "that" turns the explanation into an ungrammatical clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h421$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Though the composer wrote more than six hundred ______ her name appears in few textbooks, an omission that musicologist Ines Kowalczyk has spent her career trying to correct.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["works.","works","works;","works,"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Though the composer wrote more than six hundred works" is a dependent clause at the start of the sentence, so a comma separates it from the main clause. A semicolon or period leaves it as a fragment, and no punctuation runs the clauses together.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h422$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The ______ in its native Southeast Asian forests, was introduced to Florida as an ornamental plant and now chokes waterways across the state.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["vine: harmless","vine, harmless","vine harmless","vine; harmless"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Harmless in its native Southeast Asian forests" is a nonrestrictive modifier between the subject "vine" and its verb "was introduced." It ends with a comma, so it must begin with one. The other choices omit the opening comma or split the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h423$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The council voted to preserve the ______ the developer who had planned to replace it with apartments announced that he would appeal the decision.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["theater; however","theater; however,","theater however,","theater, however,"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses are linked by the conjunctive adverb "however." A semicolon goes before it and a comma after it. A comma before "however" creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and omitting the comma after "however" is nonstandard.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h424$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Researchers recorded the calls of fin whales, the second-largest animals on ______ to map the structure of the oceanic crust; the low-frequency sounds penetrate kilometers into the seafloor before reflecting back.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Earth; and used them","Earth, and, used them","Earth, and used them","Earth and used them"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The appositive "the second-largest animals on Earth" opens with a comma after "whales" and must close with one. That closing comma also falls before "and used them," a second verb for the same subject "Researchers." Omitting it leaves the appositive unclosed, a semicolon splits the compound predicate, and a comma after "and" is incorrect.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h425$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Only one of the expedition's original ______ survived the voyage home, and it now sits in a maritime museum in Lisbon.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["maps,","maps:","maps","maps;"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Only one of the expedition's original maps" is the subject, and "survived" is its verb, so no punctuation belongs between them.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h426$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The cook's notebooks, filled with recipes in three languages and annotated over five ______ offer a rare view of how a single household's diet changed as the family moved from Lebanon to Brazil to Canada.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["decades,","decades;","decades","decades—"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The participial phrase "filled with recipes in three languages and annotated over five decades" opens with a comma after "notebooks" and must close with a comma before the verb "offer."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h427$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Seismologists can determine where an earthquake began by comparing the arrival times of two kinds of ______ fast-moving P waves and slower S waves.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["waves, being","waves, these are","waves;","waves:"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The independent clause ends with "two kinds of waves," and a phrase naming them follows, so a colon is correct. A semicolon can't introduce a list, "these are" creates a comma splice, and "being" is ungrammatical here.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h428$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The architect's design calls for a roof covered entirely in native ______ the plants will insulate the building in winter, absorb rainwater in spring, and provide habitat for pollinators in summer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["grasses, once established,","grasses once established,","grasses. Once established,","grasses; once established"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses meet here. A period separates them, and the second opens with the introductory phrase "Once established" followed by a comma. A comma creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the semicolon choice omits the comma needed after the introductory phrase.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h429$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The novel's two narrators, a retired sea captain and his estranged granddaughter, tell the same story from opposite ends of a century, and neither ______ entirely trustworthy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","are","have been","were"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Neither," used as a subject pronoun, is singular, and the novel is discussed in the present ("tell"). "Is" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h430$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$After reviewing footage from more than two hundred camera traps, the biologists concluded that the elusive cat ______ the forest far more often than local surveys had suggested.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["visit","have visited","visited","visiting"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clause needs a finite verb agreeing with the singular "cat" and fitting the past narrative ("concluded"). "Visited" works. "Visit" and "have visited" are plural forms, and "visiting" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h431$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The sudden popularity of the board game, which had sold only a few thousand copies in its first decade, ______ its designer, who learned about it from a newspaper article.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have surprised","were surprising","surprising","surprised"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "popularity," which is singular, and the sentence needs a past-tense finite verb. "Surprised" works. "Surprising" is nonfinite, and "were surprising" and "have surprised" are plural.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h432$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Clinging to the cliff face hundreds of meters above the river, ______ accessible only by a narrow staircase carved into the rock.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["visitors find the monastery","the monastery is","the monastery's only access is","one finds that the monastery is"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$"Clinging to the cliff face" must modify the thing that clings, which is the monastery, so "the monastery" must come right after the comma. The other choices make "visitors," "one," or "access" the thing clinging to the cliff.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h433$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The data from the new satellite, combined with decades of ground measurements, ______ allowed climatologists to estimate the rate of sea ice loss with unprecedented accuracy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have","having","is","has"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$"Data" is treated as plural here, as in "the data ... have," and the phrase "combined with decades of ground measurements" is parenthetical. "Have allowed" is correct. "Has" and "is" are singular, and "having" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h434$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Most of the volunteers who signed up for the bird count had never participated in a scientific study before; ______ had owned a pair of binoculars.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["few","fewest","fewer","few's"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The second clause needs a plural pronoun subject meaning "not many people." "Few" works. "Fewer" requires a comparison, "few's" is a meaningless possessive, and "fewest" requires a superlative context.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h435$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The research vessel, whose crew of thirty scientists and sailors ______ spent four months mapping the seafloor, returned to port with more than a terabyte of data.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["has","had","have","having"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$In the relative clause, the subject is "crew," a singular collective noun, and the mapping came before the return to port. "Had spent" is correct. "Have" is plural, "has" is present perfect and clashes with the past narrative, and "having" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h436$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Unlike the mainland population, ______ grown noticeably smaller over the past century, possibly in response to limited food on the island.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the island has foxes that have","the island's foxes have","the size of the island's foxes has","foxes on the island, which have"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The comparison begins with "the mainland population" (of foxes), so it must be completed with foxes, and the sentence needs a main verb. "The island's foxes have grown" does both. The other choices lack a main verb or compare a population with an island or a size.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h437$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$When the canal was completed in 1914, ships traveling between New York and San Francisco ______ no longer forced to sail around the southern tip of South America.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["were","have been","was","are"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The subject is "ships," which is plural, and the context is 1914, so "were" is correct. "Was" is singular, and "are" and "have been" don't fit the past time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h438$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Though the theory was ridiculed when first proposed, the evidence supporting continental drift ______ overwhelming by the late 1960s.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["becomes","has become","had become","will become"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"By the late 1960s" marks a completed point in the past, so the past perfect "had become" is correct. The present, present perfect, and future forms clash with the time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h439$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Every species of penguin, from the tiny little penguin of Australia to the massive emperor of Antarctica, ______ in the Southern Hemisphere, at least for part of its range.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are found","is found","were found","have been found"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The subject is "every species," which is singular, and the statement is a general present-tense fact. The "from ... to ..." phrase is parenthetical. "Is found" agrees, the plural forms don't, and "were found" also misstates the tense.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h440$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The weavers of the cooperative use only natural ______ indigo for blue, cochineal insects for red, and the bark of local trees for a range of browns and golds.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["dyes:","dyes, such as:","dyes;","dyes, they use"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The independent clause ends with "natural dyes," and a list of examples follows, so a colon is correct. A semicolon can't introduce a list, a colon should not follow "such as," and "they use" creates a comma splice.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h441$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Historian Joseph ______ book on the 1918 influenza pandemic draws heavily on letters written by nurses, argues that the disease's toll was worsened by wartime censorship of the press.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["Mensah, who's","Mensah; whose","Mensah whose","Mensah, whose"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Whose book on the 1918 influenza pandemic draws heavily on letters written by nurses" is a nonrestrictive relative clause, opened with a comma and closed by the comma before "argues." "Whose" is the correct possessive relative pronoun; "who's" means "who is."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h442$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The experiment was simple in ______ volunteers were asked to remember a list of words either while walking on a treadmill or while sitting still.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["design","design:","design,","design, where"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The second part explains the simple design, so a colon is correct. A comma creates a comma splice and no punctuation creates a run-on. "Where" with a comma creates a clause that misuses "where" for something other than a place.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h443$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$In 1977, NASA launched the twin Voyager ______ more than four decades later, both are still transmitting data from beyond the edge of the heliosphere.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["probes, remarkably;","probes, remarkably,","probes; remarkably,","probes remarkably,"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, and a semicolon joins them, with "remarkably" introducing the second clause. A comma before "remarkably" creates a comma splice, no punctuation creates a run-on, and the semicolon after "remarkably" attaches the adverb to the wrong clause.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h444$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The anthology brings together stories by writers from every country in Central ______ have never before appeared in English translation.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["America, many of them","America; many","America many","America, many"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Two independent clauses meet here, so a semicolon is correct. A comma alone (with or without "of them") creates a comma splice, and no punctuation creates a run-on.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h445$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$At high altitudes, the air contains less ______ climbers ascending above 8,000 meters, a region known as the "death zone," usually rely on bottled supplemental oxygen.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["oxygen, so","oxygen; so,","oxygen so","oxygen, so,"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses joined by "so" need a comma before the conjunction and none after it. The other choices omit the required comma or add one after "so."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h446$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The mathematician Emmy Noether proved a theorem linking two seemingly unrelated ______ symmetries in nature and the laws of conservation, such as the conservation of energy.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["ideas, they are","ideas:","ideas;","ideas. Which are"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The independent clause ends with "two seemingly unrelated ideas," and the phrase that follows names them, so a colon is correct. A semicolon needs an independent clause after it, "they are" creates a comma splice, and "Which are" begins a fragment.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h447$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$"Nothing in biology makes sense except in the light of ______ wrote the geneticist Theodosius Dobzhansky in a 1973 essay.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["evolution,\"","evolution\",","evolution\"","evolution.\""]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$When a quoted sentence is followed by a dialogue tag such as "wrote the geneticist...," the quotation ends with a comma inside the closing quotation mark. A period would end the sentence too early, the comma outside the quotation mark is nonstandard in American English, and no punctuation is incorrect.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h448$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The city's first public ______ in 1848 with a few hundred donated books, now holds more than six million volumes.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["library: founded","library founded","library, founded","library; founded"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$"Founded in 1848 with a few hundred donated books" is a nonrestrictive participial phrase. It closes with a comma before "now holds" and must open with one after "library." No punctuation leaves the phrase unopened, and a semicolon or colon separates the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h449$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean ______ they support roughly a quarter of all known marine species.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["floor, yet","floor, yet,","floor; yet,","floor yet"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Two independent clauses joined by the coordinating conjunction "yet" need a comma before it and none after it. The other choices omit the required comma or add an unneeded one after "yet."$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h450$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The sculptor works only with materials salvaged from demolished ______ copper pipes, window frames, roof tiles, and, in one celebrated piece, an entire staircase.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["buildings, for example:","buildings;","buildings:","buildings, these include"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$A colon introduces the list of salvaged materials after an independent clause. A semicolon can't introduce a list, "these include" creates a comma splice, and a colon after "for example" is redundant and nonstandard.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h451$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The herd of elk that wanders through the valley each winter ______ grown so large that wildlife managers are considering relocating some of the animals.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["having","are","has","have"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "herd," a singular collective noun, so "has" is correct. "Elk" and "valley" belong to intervening phrases. "Have" and "are" are plural, and "having" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h452$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The researchers found that people who learn a second language as adults process ______ grammar using different brain regions than native speakers do.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["they're","it's","its","their"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The possessive refers to "a second language," which is singular, so "its" is correct. "It's" is a contraction, and "their" and "they're" are plural or contractions.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h453$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Built without mortar more than five hundred years ago, ______ still stand firm through earthquakes that have toppled modern buildings nearby.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the Inca walls of Cusco","the Inca's skill means that the walls of Cusco","Cusco's earthquakes have not toppled the Inca walls, which","there are Inca walls in Cusco that"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The introductory phrase "Built without mortar" must modify the walls, so "the Inca walls of Cusco" must follow the comma. The other choices place "Cusco's earthquakes," "the Inca's skill," or "there" in that position.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h454$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Of the many inventions credited to the engineer, the one that historians consider most important ______ a simple valve that made steam engines far safer.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["are","have been","is","were"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The subject is "the one," which is singular, and the sentence states a present assessment ("consider"). "Is" agrees. "Inventions" and "historians" are in intervening phrases.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h455$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Before the printing press reached England in 1476, scribes ______ every book by hand, a process that could take months for a single volume.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["will copy","had copied","copy","have copied"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The copying took place before a past event, the arrival of the press in 1476, so the past perfect "had copied" is correct. The other tenses don't fit the time frame.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h456$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The violinist and the pianist, who have performed together for more than twenty years, ______ a new recording of Beethoven's sonatas next spring.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is releasing","will release","releases","has released"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The compound subject "the violinist and the pianist" is plural, and "next spring" points to the future, so "will release" is correct. "Releases," "has released," and "is releasing" are singular.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h457$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Not only the tunnel's walls but also its ceiling ______ decorated with thousands of tiles depicting scenes from the city's history.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["is","were","have been","are"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With "not only ... but also," the verb agrees with the nearer subject, "ceiling," which is singular, and the description is in the present. "Is" is correct.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h458$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$The study tracked how quickly children ______ to read when instruction emphasized phonics compared with when it emphasized whole words.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["learning","having learned","learned","to learn"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The clause "how quickly children ______ to read" needs a finite verb consistent with the past-tense "tracked." "Learned" works. "Learning," "to learn," and "having learned" are nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h459$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Rising from the plain like the back of a sleeping animal, ______ has been sacred to the Anangu people for tens of thousands of years.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["the Anangu people's belief is that Uluru","it is known that Uluru","the sacredness of Uluru","Uluru"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$"Rising from the plain like the back of a sleeping animal" must modify the landform, so "Uluru" must come right after the comma. The other choices make a belief, "it," or "the sacredness" the thing rising from the plain.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h460$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Ocean temperatures off the coast of Peru rose sharply during a strong El Niño, and the anchovy catch, on which much of the region's fishing industry ______, fell by more than half.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["have depended","depend","depends","depending"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$In the relative clause "on which much of the region's fishing industry depends," the subject is "much of the industry," which is singular. "Depends" agrees. "Depend" and "have depended" are plural, and "depending" is nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h461$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Boundaries$q$, 3, $q$The archive's oldest recording, a wax cylinder made in ______ preserves a Lakota song performed by a singer whose name the ethnographer never wrote down.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["1898,","1898:","1898","1898;"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The appositive "a wax cylinder made in 1898" opens with a comma after "recording" and must close with one before the verb "preserves." Omitting the comma leaves the appositive unclosed, and a semicolon or colon would separate the subject from its verb.$q$, null),
  ($q$conv-h462$q$, $q$rw$q$, $q$Standard English Conventions$q$, $q$Form, Structure, and Sense$q$, 3, $q$Of the hundreds of letters that the explorer sent home during the expedition, only a handful ______ survived, most of them water-stained and partly illegible.$q$, $q$Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?$q$, $q$["to have","has","having","have"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The subject is "a handful" of letters, which is treated as plural here ("most of them"), and the sentence needs a finite verb. "Have survived" is correct. "Has" treats the subject as singular despite "most of them," and "having" and "to have" are nonfinite.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-1$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 4x − 9 = 23, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["8","6","3.5","32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4x − 9 = 23 → 4x = 32 → x = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-2$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2(x + 3) = 5x − 6, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4","2","6","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2x + 6 = 5x − 6 → 12 = 3x → x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-3$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line passes through the points (2, 5) and (4, 11). What is the y-intercept of the line?$q$, $q$["−1","1","5","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (11 − 5)/(4 − 2) = 3. Using y = 3x + b with (2, 5): 5 = 3(2) + b → b = −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-4$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 10 and x − y = 2, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["4","6","8","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Subtract the equations: (x + y) − (x − y) = 10 − 2 → 2y = 8 → y = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-5$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 3x − 5 > 10?$q$, $q$["6","5","4","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3x − 5 > 10 → 3x > 15 → x > 5, so the smallest integer satisfying this is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-6$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A gym membership costs $25 to join plus $15 per month. If a member has paid a total of $205, how many months has the membership been active?$q$, $q$["12","10","13","180"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$25 + 15m = 205 → 15m = 180 → m = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-7$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If y = 2x − 1 and 3x + y = 14, what is the value of x + y?$q$, $q$["8","3","5","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute: 3x + (2x − 1) = 14 → 5x = 15 → x = 3, so y = 2(3) − 1 = 5. Thus x + y = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-8$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear, with f(0) = 4 and f(2) = 10. What is the value of f(5)?$q$, $q$["19","14","10","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (10 − 4)/(2 − 0) = 3, so f(x) = 3x + 4. Then f(5) = 3(5) + 4 = 19.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-9$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line given by y = 2x + 3 is parallel to a second line that passes through (0, −1). What is the slope of the second line?$q$, $q$["2","−1","3","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Parallel lines have equal slopes, so the second line also has slope 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-10$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If |x − 4| = 9, what is the sum of all possible values of x?$q$, $q$["8","9","−5","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x − 4 = 9 gives x = 13; x − 4 = −9 gives x = −5. The sum of the two solutions is 13 + (−5) = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-11$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$For what value of k does the system of equations 9x + 9y = 17 and 27x + ky = 56 have no solution?$q$, $q$["31","23","−27","27"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$For no solution, the left sides must be proportional while the right sides are not. Since 27 = 3 · 9, the y-coefficients must match that same scale factor: k = 3 · 9 = 27. (Checking constants: 3 · 17 = 51 ≠ 56, confirming no solution.)$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-12$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A parking garage charges $5 for the first hour and $2 for each additional hour. What is the maximum number of whole hours a driver can park without exceeding $19?$q$, $q$["8","7","9","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5 + 2(h − 1) ≤ 19 → 2(h − 1) ≤ 14 → h − 1 ≤ 7 → h ≤ 8, so the maximum is 8 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-13$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line given by 3x + 2y = 12?$q$, $q$["−3/2","3/2","2/3","−2/3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving for y: 2y = −3x + 12 → y = (−3/2)x + 6, so the slope is −3/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-14$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line 2x − 5y = 20?$q$, $q$["10","−4","4","−10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Set y = 0: 2x = 20 → x = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-15$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x − y = 7 and x + y = 9, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["5","4","9","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: 4x = 16 → x = 4. Then from x + y = 9, y = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-16$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A cell phone plan costs $20 plus $0.10 per minute of calls. If a customer's bill was $35, how many minutes of calls were made?$q$, $q$["150","15","175","350"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$20 + 0.10m = 35 → 0.10m = 15 → m = 150.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-17$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$Company A charges $50 plus $3 per unit produced. Company B charges $20 plus $5 per unit produced. For what number of units is the total cost the same for both companies?$q$, $q$["15","10","30","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$50 + 3x = 20 + 5x → 30 = 2x → x = 15.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-18$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −3 < 2x + 1 ≤ 7?$q$, $q$["3","2","−2","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−4 < 2x ≤ 6 → −2 < x ≤ 3, so the greatest integer value is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-19$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 10x + 7y = 1 is equivalent to y = mx + k, where m and k are constants. What is the value of m?$q$, $q$["−7/10","7/10","10/7","−10/7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Solving for y: 7y = −10x + 1, so y = (−10/7)x + 1/7. Thus m = −10/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-20$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$Sarah is 4 years older than twice Tom's age. If Sarah is 22 years old, how old is Tom?$q$, $q$["9","11","18","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$22 = 2t + 4 → 18 = 2t → t = 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-21$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A water tank had 80 gallons at time t = 0 and 20 gallons at t = 6 minutes, draining at a constant rate. What is the drain rate in gallons per minute?$q$, $q$["10","60","6","600"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The tank lost 60 gallons over 6 minutes: 60/6 = 10 gallons per minute.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-22$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations x + 7y = 15 and 4x + ky = 60 has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−28","26","28","31"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$For infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be a constant multiple of the first. Since 4/1 = 4 and 60/15 = 4, the y-coefficient must follow the same scale factor: k = 4 · 7 = 28.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-23$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y ≤ 3x − 2?$q$, $q$["(2, 3)","(0, 0)","(−1, 2)","(1, 5)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (2, 3): 3 ≤ 3(2) − 2 = 4, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when checked.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-24$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has a slope of −3 and passes through (1, 2). What is the value of y when x = 4?$q$, $q$["−7","−10","7","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$y − 2 = −3(x − 1) → y = −3x + 5. At x = 4: y = −12 + 5 = −7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-25$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$Adult movie tickets cost $12 and child tickets cost $8. A group bought 5 tickets for a total of $52. How many adult tickets were purchased?$q$, $q$["3","2","4","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let a + c = 5 and 12a + 8c = 52. Substituting c = 5 − a: 12a + 8(5 − a) = 52 → 4a = 12 → a = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-26$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 2x + 3y = 28 and 4x + 4y = 44, what is the value of 3x + 3y?$q$, $q$["31","36","33","−33"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Solving the system gives x = 5 and y = 6. So 3x + 3y = 3(5) + 3(6) = 33.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-27$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A total of $6,000 is invested in two accounts, one earning 5% annual interest and the other earning 7% annual interest. If the total interest earned in one year is $330, how much was invested at 5%?$q$, $q$["−4500","4503","4495","4500"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let x be the amount invested at 5%. Then $6,000 − x is invested at 7%. Solving 0.05x.07(6,000 − x) = 330 gives x = $4,500.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-28$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear. If f(5) = −2 and f(7) = −8, what is the value of f(−6) − f(3)?$q$, $q$["28","27","−27","29"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slope = (−8 − −2)/(7 − 5) = −3. Since f(5) = −2, f(x) = −3x + 13. So f(−6) = 31 and f(3) = 4, giving a difference of 27.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-29$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If 2x + 3y ≤ 18 and x = 3, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["4","6","3","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 3: 6 + 3y ≤ 18 → 3y ≤ 12 → y ≤ 4, so the maximum value of y is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-30$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$At a bakery, 3 muffins and 6 croissants cost $408 total, while 4 muffins and 5 croissants cost $406 total. How much more does one muffin cost than one croissant, in dollars?$q$, $q$["−2","2","−1","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let x be the muffin price and y the croissant price. Solving 3x + 6y = 408 and 4x + 5y = 406 gives x = 44 and y = 46, so the muffin costs −2 dollars more.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-31$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 5x − 2y = 20 is graphed in the xy-plane. What is the x-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["4","10","−4","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The x-intercept occurs where y = 0: 5x = 20 → x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-32$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the largest integer value of x that satisfies −4x + 9 > −11?$q$, $q$["4","5","3","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−4x + 9 > −11 → −4x > −20 → x < 5 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number). The largest integer less than 5 is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-33$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Line k has slope 3 and passes through (6, 6). Line k also passes through (−1, y). What is the value of y minus the y-intercept of line k?$q$, $q$["−3","0","3","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using y = 3x + b through (6, 6): 6 = 3(6) + b, so b = −12. At x = −1, y = 3(−1) − 12 = −15. So y − b = −3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-34$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 5x + 7 = 32, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","3","7","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5x + 7 = 32 → 5x = 25 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-35$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation for converting Celsius to Fahrenheit is F = (9/5)C + 32. Which expression gives C in terms of F?$q$, $q$["(5/9)(F − 32)","(9/5)(F − 32)","(5/9)F − 32","(5/9)F + 32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$F − 32 = (9/5)C → C = (5/9)(F − 32).$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-36$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Function f is linear with f(x) = 2x − 7. Function g is linear with g(0) = 34 and a rate of change of 3. For what value of x does g(x) − f(x) = 44?$q$, $q$["5","4","3","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$g(x) = 34 + 3x. So g(x) − f(x) = (3 − 2)x + (34 − −7) = x + 41. Setting this equal to 44 gives x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-37$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2x + y = 15 and y = x − 3, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["6","3","9","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute y = x − 3 into 2x + y = 15: 2x + (x − 3) = 15 → 3x = 18 → x = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-38$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A vendor sells popcorn bags for $4 each after paying a $30 booth fee. What is the minimum number of bags the vendor must sell to make a profit greater than $50?$q$, $q$["21","20","19","30"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4n − 30 > 50 → 4n > 80 → n > 20, so the minimum whole number of bags is 21.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-39$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which of the following ordered pairs is NOT a solution to the inequality 7x − 8y > −9?$q$, $q$["(2, −17)","(−4, −1)","(0, −6)","(3, −7)"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Testing each point in 7x − 8y > −9: only (−4, −1) gives a value that is not greater than −9, so it is not a solution.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-40$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A taxi charges a $3 flat fee plus $2.50 per mile. If a ride costs $15.50, how many miles was the ride?$q$, $q$["5","6","4","12.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3 + 2.5m = 15.5 → 2.5m = 12.5 → m = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-41$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If (2/3)(x − 6) = 10, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["21","9","6","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(2/3)(x − 6) = 10 → x − 6 = 15 → x = 21.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-42$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation A = p(q + 5r) relates the quantities A, p, q, and r. Which equation correctly gives r in terms of A, p, and q?$q$, $q$["r = (A + pq)/(5p)","r = 5p(A − q)","r = (A − pq)/(5p)","r = (A − pq)/5"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Divide both sides by p: A/p = q + 5r. Subtract q: A/p − q = 5r. Divide by 5: r = (A − pq)/(5p), after combining over a common denominator.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-43$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line has a slope of 4 and passes through the point (0, −3). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = 4x − 3","y = 4x + 3","y = −3x + 4","y = 4x"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using slope-intercept form y = mx + b with m = 4 and y-intercept b = −3 (from the point (0, −3)): y = 4x − 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-44$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A store sells pens for $2 and notebooks for $5. Maria bought a total of 8 items for $25. How many notebooks did she buy?$q$, $q$["3","5","2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let p + n = 8 and 2p + 5n = 25. Substituting p = 8 − n: 2(8 − n) + 5n = 25 → 16 + 3n = 25 → n = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-45$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −6(x + 2) ≥ −12 + 4x?$q$, $q$["0","1","−1","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Distribute: −6x − 12 ≥ −12 + 4x. Collecting x-terms: −10x ≥ 0. Dividing by the negative coefficient −10 flips the inequality: x ≤ 0. The greatest integer satisfying this is 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-46$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y > x + 1?$q$, $q$["(2, 5)","(2, 3)","(0, −1)","(3, 2)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (2, 5): 5 > 2 + 1 = 3, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when substituted.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-47$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A tank initially contains 15 gallons of water and is being filled at a rate of 4 gallons per minute. How many minutes will it take for the tank to contain 75 gallons?$q$, $q$["15","20","12","60"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$15 + 4m = 75 → 4m = 60 → m = 15.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-48$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If 4(x + 1) + 4 = −7(x + 7) + 2, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","−4","−3","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Expand both sides: 4x + 4 + 4 = −7x − 49 + 2. Collecting the x-terms and constants gives (11)x = −55, so x = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-49$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the y-intercept of the line given by the equation y = −2x + 7?$q$, $q$["7","−2","−7","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In the form y = mx + b, the y-intercept is b = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-50$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line passes through (−2, 3) and (2, 11). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = 2x + 7","y = 2x + 3","y = 4x + 7","y = 2x − 7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (11 − 3)/(2 − (−2)) = 8/4 = 2. Using point (−2, 3): y − 3 = 2(x + 2) → y = 2x + 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-51$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$For what value of k does the system of equations 6x + 2y = 20 and 24x + ky = 84 have no solution?$q$, $q$["7","8","12","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$For no solution, the left sides must be proportional while the right sides are not. Since 24 = 4 · 6, the y-coefficients must match that same scale factor: k = 4 · 2 = 8. (Checking constants: 4 · 20 = 80 ≠ 84, confirming no solution.)$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-52$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 2x + 3 ≥ 9?$q$, $q$["3","4","2","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2x + 3 ≥ 9 → 2x ≥ 6 → x ≥ 3, so the smallest integer value is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-53$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If x − 2y ≥ 8 and x = 0, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["−4","4","−8","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 0: −2y ≥ 8 → y ≤ −4 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number), so the maximum value of y is −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-54$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A chemist mixes 45 liters of solution by combining a 12% acid solution with a 62% acid solution. If 10 liters of the 62% solution is used, and the resulting mixture is 23.111% acid, how many liters of the 12% solution were used?$q$, $q$["33","−35","37","35"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let x be the liters of the 12% solution. Since 10 liters of 62% solution is also used, 0.12x.62(10) = 0.2311111111111111(x + 10). Solving gives x = 35 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-55$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 7x − 2 = 5x + 12, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["7","14","5","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$7x − 2 = 5x + 12 → 2x = 14 → x = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-56$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x + 4y = 24 and x = 4, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["3","4","6","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 4: 3(4) + 4y = 24 → 12 + 4y = 24 → 4y = 12 → y = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-57$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function h is linear, with h(−3) = −20 and h(7) = 30. What is the x-intercept of the graph of h?$q$, $q$["2","1","−1","3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slope = (h(7) − h(−3))/(7 − −3) = 5. Since h(−3) = −20, h(x) = 5x − 5. Setting h(x) = 0 gives x = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-58$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x = 3y and 2x + y = 21, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["3","9","7","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 3y into 2x + y = 21: 2(3y) + y = 21 → 7y = 21 → y = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-59$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A student needs an average of at least 90 on three tests to earn an A. The student scored 85 and 92 on the first two tests. What is the minimum score needed on the third test to earn an A?$q$, $q$["93","90","88","95"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$(85 + 92 + x)/3 ≥ 90 → 177 + x ≥ 270 → x ≥ 93, so the minimum score needed is 93.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-60$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 14 and x − y = 6, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["10","6","4","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (x + y) + (x − y) = 14 + 6 → 2x = 20 → x = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-61$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 4x + y = 17 and y = 2x − 1, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["3","5","2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute y = 2x − 1 into 4x + y = 17: 4x + (2x − 1) = 17 → 6x = 18 → x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-62$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$Twice a number plus a second number is 20. The second number is 4 less than the first number. What is the first number?$q$, $q$["8","4","12","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let x be the first number and y the second: 2x + y = 20 and y = x − 4. Substituting: 2x + (x − 4) = 20 → 3x = 24 → x = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-63$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations 8x + 9y = 7 and 32x + ky = 28 has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["32","36","38","−36"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$For infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be a constant multiple of the first. Since 32/8 = 4 and 28/7 = 4, the y-coefficient must follow the same scale factor: k = 4 · 9 = 36.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-64$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If y = x + 5 and 2x + y = 20, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["10","5","15","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute y = x + 5 into 2x + y = 20: 2x + (x + 5) = 20 → 3x = 15 → x = 5, so y = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-65$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A group buys 8 tickets to a museum, some at the student price of $5 and some at the adult price of $9, paying $52 total. How many student tickets were purchased?$q$, $q$["5","3","8","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let s + a = 8 and 5s + 9a = 52. Substituting a = 8 − s: 5s + 9(8 − s) = 52 → −4s + 72 = 52 → s = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-66$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 4x + 4y = 4 and x − 4y = 1, what is the value of 2x + 3y?$q$, $q$["3","−1","−2","2"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Solving the system gives x = 1 and y = 0. So 2x + 3y = 2(1) + 3(0) = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-67$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x − 5y = 1 and 2x + 5y = 24, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","2.8","25","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (3x − 5y) + (2x + 5y) = 1 + 24 → 5x = 25 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-68$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line has a slope of −2 and passes through the point (0, 5). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = −2x + 5","y = 2x + 5","y = −2x − 5","y = 5x − 2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using slope-intercept form y = mx + b with m = −2 and y-intercept b = 5: y = −2x + 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-69$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear, with f(1) = 6 and f(3) = 14. What is the value of f(6)?$q$, $q$["26","22","30","18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (14 − 6)/(3 − 1) = 4, so f(x) = 4x + b. Using f(1) = 6: 6 = 4 + b → b = 2. Then f(6) = 4(6) + 2 = 26.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-70$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line passes through the points (1, −2) and (5, 6). What is the slope of the line?$q$, $q$["2","1/2","−2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (6 − (−2))/(5 − 1) = 8/4 = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-71$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear. If f(2) = −16 and f(7) = −21, what is the value of f(3) − f(0)?$q$, $q$["−1","−2","−3","3"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Slope = (−21 − −16)/(7 − 2) = −1. Since f(2) = −16, f(x) = −x − 14. So f(3) = −17 and f(0) = −14, giving a difference of −3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-72$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line given by 4x − y = 8?$q$, $q$["4","−4","8","1/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving for y: −y = −4x + 8 → y = 4x − 8, so the slope is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-73$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function g is linear. If g(0) = −3 and g(4) = 5, what is the value of g(2)?$q$, $q$["1","2","−1","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (5 − (−3))/(4 − 0) = 2, so g(x) = 2x − 3. Then g(2) = 2(2) − 3 = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-74$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Line k has slope −3 and passes through (7, −9). Line k also passes through (2, y). What is the value of y minus the y-intercept of line k?$q$, $q$["6","−6","−4","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Using y = −3x + b through (7, −9): −9 = −3(7) + b, so b = 12. At x = 2, y = −3(2) + 12 = 6. So y − b = −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-75$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The linear function h satisfies h(2) = 9 and h(5) = 18. What is the value of h(0)?$q$, $q$["3","9","6","0"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (18 − 9)/(5 − 2) = 3, so h(x) = 3x + b. Using h(2) = 9: 9 = 6 + b → b = 3. Then h(0) = 3.$q$, null),
