  ($q$alg-105$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If −5(x − 2) + 4 = −3(x − 9) − 3, what is the value of 4x − 3?$q$, $q$["−25","23","−23","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Expand both sides: −5x + 10 + 4 = −3x + 27 − 3. Collecting terms gives (−2)x = 10, so x = −5. Then 4x − 3 = 4(−5) − 3 = −23.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-106$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If −6(x − 5) − 8 = -(x + 9) + 11, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4","2","5","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Expand both sides: −6x + 30 − 8 = −x − 9 + 11. Collecting the x-terms and constants gives (−5)x = −20, so x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-107$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line given by the equation y = 4x − 16?$q$, $q$["4","−16","16","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The x-intercept occurs where y = 0: 0 = 4x − 16 → x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-108$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the y-intercept of the line given by the equation 2x + y = −9?$q$, $q$["−9","9","2","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The y-intercept occurs where x = 0: y = −9.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-109$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If 5x + 2y = 19 and x = 3, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["2","4","8","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 3: 15 + 2y = 19 → 2y = 4 → y = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-110$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 3x − 4y = 24 is graphed in the xy-plane. What is the x-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["8","−6","6","24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The x-intercept occurs where y = 0: 3x = 24 → x = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-111$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2x − 3y = 18 and y = −2, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["6","12","−6","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting y = −2: 2x − 3(−2) = 18 → 2x + 6 = 18 → 2x = 12 → x = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-112$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The formula for the area of a trapezoid is A = (1/2)(b1 + b2)h. Which expression gives h in terms of A, b1, and b2?$q$, $q$["2A/(b1 + b2)","A/(b1 + b2)","2A − b1 − b2","A/(2(b1 + b2))"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A = (1/2)(b1 + b2)h → 2A = (b1 + b2)h → h = 2A/(b1 + b2).$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-113$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 6x − 3y = 18 is graphed in the xy-plane. What is the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["−6","18","6","−18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The y-intercept occurs where x = 0: −3y = 18 → y = −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-114$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 2x + 9y = 14 is equivalent to y = mx + k, where m and k are constants. What is the value of m?$q$, $q$["−2/9","9/2","−9/2","2/9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving for y: 9y = −2x + 14, so y = (−2/9)x + 14/9. Thus m = −2/9.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-115$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation A = p(q + 4r) relates the quantities A, p, q, and r. Which equation correctly gives r in terms of A, p, and q?$q$, $q$["r = (A − pq)/(4p)","r = 4p(A − q)","r = (A + pq)/(4p)","r = (A − pq)/4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Divide both sides by p: A/p = q + 4r. Subtract q: A/p − q = 4r. Divide by 4: r = (A − pq)/(4p), after combining over a common denominator.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-116$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line given by the equation y = −5x + 8?$q$, $q$["−5","8","5","1/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In the form y = mx + b, the slope is m = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-117$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line has a slope of 3 and passes through the point (0, −7). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = 3x − 7","y = 3x + 7","y = −7x + 3","y = 3x"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using y = mx + b with m = 3 and y-intercept b = −7: y = 3x − 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-118$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line passes through the points (0, 2) and (3, 11). What is the slope of the line?$q$, $q$["3","9","1/3","4.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (11 − 2)/(3 − 0) = 9/3 = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-119$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear, with f(0) = −2 and f(3) = 7. What is the value of f(6)?$q$, $q$["16","25","12","19"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (7 − (−2))/3 = 3, so f(x) = 3x − 2. Then f(6) = 3(6) − 2 = 16.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-120$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has a slope of 1/2 and passes through (4, 3). What is the value of y when x = 10?$q$, $q$["6","8","5","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$y − 3 = (1/2)(x − 4) → y = (1/2)x + 1. At x = 10: y = 5 + 1 = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-121$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function g is linear. If g(2) = 10 and g(5) = 19, what is the value of g(0)?$q$, $q$["4","10","1","13"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (19 − 10)/(5 − 2) = 3, so g(x) = 3x + b. Using g(2) = 10: 6 + b = 10 → b = 4. Then g(0) = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-122$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line with slope −2 passes through (3, 4). What is the x-intercept of the line?$q$, $q$["5","10","4","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$y − 4 = −2(x − 3) → y = −2x + 10. Setting y = 0: 0 = −2x + 10 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-123$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Function f is linear with f(x) = 6x + 10. Function g is linear with g(0) = 17 and a rate of change of 9. For what value of x does g(x) − f(x) = 22?$q$, $q$["7","6","−5","5"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$g(x) = 17 + 9x. So g(x) − f(x) = (9 − 6)x + (17 − 10) = 3x + 7. Setting this equal to 22 gives x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-124$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function h is linear, with h(−3) = −5 and h(4) = −40. What is the x-intercept of the graph of h?$q$, $q$["−4","−3","−2","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (h(4) − h(−3))/(4 − −3) = −5. Since h(−3) = −5, h(x) = −5x − 20. Setting h(x) = 0 gives x = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-125$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 4x − 7 > 13?$q$, $q$["6","5","20","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4x − 7 > 13 → 4x > 20 → x > 5, so the smallest integer satisfying this is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-126$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the largest integer value of x that satisfies 2x + 5 < 19?$q$, $q$["6","7","14","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2x + 5 < 19 → 2x < 14 → x < 7, so the largest integer satisfying this is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-127$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest value of x that satisfies 3x + 8 ≥ 2?$q$, $q$["−2","2","−6","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3x + 8 ≥ 2 → 3x ≥ −6 → x ≥ −2, so the smallest value satisfying this is −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-128$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A delivery service charges a $5 fee plus $1.25 per mile. What is the minimum number of whole miles a delivery must travel for the total charge to exceed $20?$q$, $q$["13","12","15","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5 + 1.25m > 20 → 1.25m > 15 → m > 12, so the minimum whole number of miles is 13.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-129$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −2 ≤ 3x − 5 < 10?$q$, $q$["4","5","1","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−2 ≤ 3x − 5 < 10 → 3 ≤ 3x < 15 → 1 ≤ x < 5, so the greatest integer value is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-130$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the smallest value of x that satisfies −5x + 3 ≤ 23?$q$, $q$["−4","4","−20","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−5x + 3 ≤ 23 → −5x ≤ 20 → x ≥ −4 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number), so the smallest value is −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-131$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A caterer charges $150 plus $12 per guest. What is the maximum number of guests for the total cost to stay at or below $450?$q$, $q$["25","24","26","37"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$150 + 12g ≤ 450 → 12g ≤ 300 → g ≤ 25, so the maximum number of guests is 25.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-132$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If x satisfies −2(x − 5) ≥ 7 + x, what is the largest possible value of 2x?$q$, $q$["2","−2","1","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving gives x ≤ 1 (the inequality flips on division by a negative coefficient), so the greatest integer value of x is 1, making the largest value of 2x equal to 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-133$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −2(x − 2) ≥ 31 + x?$q$, $q$["−10","9","−9","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Distribute: −2x + 4 ≥ 31 + x. Collecting x-terms: −3x ≥ 27. Dividing by the negative coefficient −3 flips the inequality: x ≤ −9. The greatest integer satisfying this is −9.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-134$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y ≥ 2x − 3?$q$, $q$["(1, 0)","(2, −3)","(4, 1)","(0, −5)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (1, 0): 0 ≥ 2(1) − 3 = −1, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when substituted.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-135$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y < 4 − x?$q$, $q$["(0, 0)","(3, 2)","(5, 0)","(1, 3)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (0, 0): 0 < 4 − 0 = 4, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when substituted.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-136$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If x − 3y ≤ 6 and x = 0, what is the minimum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["−2","2","−6","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 0: −3y ≤ 6 → y ≥ −2 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number), so the minimum value of y is −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-137$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If 5x + 2y ≤ 20 and x = 2, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["5","10","2","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 2: 10 + 2y ≤ 20 → 2y ≤ 10 → y ≤ 5, so the maximum value of y is 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-138$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If 3x − 4y ≥ −8 and x = 4, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["5","20","3","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 4: 12 − 4y ≥ −8 → −4y ≥ −20 → y ≤ 5 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number), so the maximum value of y is 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-139$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which of the following ordered pairs is NOT a solution to the inequality 3x + 2y ≤ 12?$q$, $q$["(6, 3)","(0, 0)","(2, 1)","(4, 0)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Testing (6, 3): 3(6) + 2(3) = 24, which is not less than or equal to 12, so (6, 3) is not a solution. The other points each satisfy the inequality.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-140$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If y ≥ −2x + 5 and x = −1, what is the minimum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["7","3","−7","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = −1: y ≥ −2(−1) + 5 = 7, so the minimum value of y is 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-141$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A point (x, y) satisfies y ≤ −2x + 17 and y ≥ x − 16. If x = 3, what is the greatest possible integer value of y?$q$, $q$["12","11","−11","10"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Substituting x = 3 into y ≤ −2x + 17 gives y ≤ 11. The greatest integer satisfying this is 11.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-142$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which of the following ordered pairs is NOT a solution to the inequality 2x − 4y > 15?$q$, $q$["(6, −8)","(3, −1)","(−2, −18)","(−3, −18)"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Testing each point in 2x − 4y > 15: only (3, −1) gives a value that is not greater than 15, so it is not a solution.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-143$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 16 and x − y = 4, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["10","6","8","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (x + y) + (x − y) = 16 + 4 → 2x = 20 → x = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-144$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2x + y = 11 and y = x + 2, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["3","9","5","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute y = x + 2 into 2x + y = 11: 2x + (x + 2) = 11 → 3x = 9 → x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-145$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A fruit stand sells apples for $0.75 each and oranges for $1.25 each. A customer bought 10 pieces of fruit for $9.50 total. How many oranges did the customer buy?$q$, $q$["4","6","5","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let a + o = 10 and 0.75a + 1.25o = 9.50. Substituting a = 10 − o: 0.75(10 − o) + 1.25o = 9.50 → 7.5 + 0.5o = 9.5 → o = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-146$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 4x + 3y = 26 and x − y = 3, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["2","5","8","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since x = y + 3, substituting gives 4(y + 3) + 3y = 26 → 7y + 12 = 26 → 7y = 14 → y = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-147$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2x + 5y = 1 and 3x − 5y = 24, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","1","−5","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (2x + 5y) + (3x − 5y) = 1 + 24 → 5x = 25 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-148$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A school fundraiser sold a combination of $3 candles and $7 plants, totaling 40 items and $172 in sales. How many candles were sold?$q$, $q$["27","13","20","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let c + p = 40 and 3c + 7p = 172. Substituting p = 40 − c: 3c + 7(40 − c) = 172 → −4c + 280 = 172 → c = 27.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-149$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$At a bakery, 5 muffins and 4 croissants cost $405 total, while 5 muffins and 2 croissants cost $345 total. How much more does one muffin cost than one croissant, in dollars?$q$, $q$["28","−27","27","23"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let x be the muffin price and y the croissant price. Solving 5x + 4y = 405 and 5x + 2y = 345 gives x = 57 and y = 30, so the muffin costs 27 dollars more.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-150$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$For what value of k does the system of equations 2x + 9y = 15 and 6x + ky = 49 have no solution?$q$, $q$["31","27","−27","25"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$For no solution, the left sides must be proportional while the right sides are not. Since 6 = 3 · 2, the y-coefficients must match that same scale factor: k = 3 · 9 = 27. (Checking constants: 3 · 15 = 45 ≠ 49, confirming no solution.)$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-151$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations 5x + 4y = 20 and 10x + ky = 40 has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["8","5","9","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be a constant multiple of the first. Since 10/5 = 2 and 40/20 = 2, the y-coefficient must follow the same scale factor: k = 2 · 4 = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-152$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A bakery sells muffins for $3 each. If Maya spends $27 total on muffins and a $6 reusable bag, how many muffins did she buy?$q$, $q$["7","9","11","21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3m + 6 = 27 → 3m = 21 → m = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-153$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A water tank initially holds 12 gallons and is filled at a constant rate of 3 gallons per minute. After how many minutes will the tank hold 45 gallons?$q$, $q$["11","15","9","33"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$12 + 3m = 45 → 3m = 33 → m = 11.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-154$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A theater group sold raffle tickets priced at $4 for members and $7 for non-members. They sold 50 tickets total and collected $260. How many member tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["30","20","25","35"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let m + n = 50 and 4m + 7n = 260. Substituting n = 50 − m: 4m + 7(50 − m) = 260 → −3m + 350 = 260 → m = 30.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-155$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$The sum of three consecutive odd integers is 69. What is the smallest of the three integers?$q$, $q$["21","23","19","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let the integers be n, n + 2, and n + 4. Then n + (n + 2) + (n + 4) = 69 → 3n + 6 = 69 → n = 21.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-156$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A concession stand sold a combination of $4 hot dogs and $6 burgers, selling 60 items total for $286. How many hot dogs were sold?$q$, $q$["37","23","30","34"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let h + b = 60 and 4h + 6b = 286. Substituting b = 60 − h: 4h + 6(60 − h) = 286 → −2h + 360 = 286 → h = 37.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-157$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A rectangular garden has a perimeter of 70 feet. The length is 2 feet more than twice the width. What is the length of the garden, in feet?$q$, $q$["24","11","22","26"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Perimeter: 2(l + w) = 70 → l + w = 35. Since l = 2w + 2: (2w + 2) + w = 35 → 3w = 33 → w = 11, so l = 2(11) + 2 = 24.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-158$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$For three consecutive integers, 5 times the smallest equals the sum of the other two, minus some number d. If the smallest integer is 18, what is the value of d?$q$, $q$["51","53","−51","47"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The three consecutive integers are 18, 19, and 20. Their sum of the two larger is 19 + 20 = 39. Since 5 times the smallest is 90, d = 90 − 39 = 51.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-159$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A total of $25,000 is invested in two accounts, one earning 6% annual interest and the other earning 7% annual interest. If the total interest earned in one year is $1730, how much was invested at 6%?$q$, $q$["1999","2000","−2000","2005"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let x be the amount invested at 6%. Then $25,000 − x is invested at 7%. Solving 0.06x.07(25,000 − x) = 1730 gives x = $2,000.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-160$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A chemist mixes 40 liters of solution by combining a 11% acid solution with a 54% acid solution. If 15 liters of the 54% solution is used, and the resulting mixture is 27.125% acid, how many liters of the 11% solution were used?$q$, $q$["27","−25","25","21"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let x be the liters of the 11% solution. Since 15 liters of 54% solution is also used, 0.11x.54(15) = 0.27125(x + 15). Solving gives x = 25 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-161$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 18 and x − y = 4, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["11","7","9","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (x + y) + (x − y) = 18 + 4 → 2x = 22 → x = 11.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-162$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If y = 3x − 2 and x + y = 18, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","13","4","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute y = 3x − 2 into x + y = 18: x + (3x − 2) = 18 → 4x = 20 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-163$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A farmer has 24 animals that are either chickens or goats. Chickens have 2 legs and goats have 4 legs. Together, the animals have 68 legs. How many goats are there?$q$, $q$["10","14","12","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let c + g = 24 and 2c + 4g = 68. Substituting c = 24 − g: 2(24 − g) + 4g = 68 → 48 + 2g = 68 → g = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-164$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 4x + 3y = 33 and x − y = 3, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["6","3","9","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since x = y + 3, substituting gives 4(y + 3) + 3y = 33 → 7y + 12 = 33 → 7y = 21 → y = 3, so x = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-165$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A school play sold student tickets for $6 and adult tickets for $11. A total of 90 tickets were sold for $730. How many adult tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["38","52","45","30"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let a + s = 90 and 11a + 6s = 730. Substituting s = 90 − a: 11a + 6(90 − a) = 730 → 5a + 540 = 730 → a = 38.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-166$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2x − 3y = 4 and 5x + 3y = 31, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","2","7","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (2x − 3y) + (5x + 3y) = 4 + 31 → 7x = 35 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-167$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 4x + y = 3 and 6x + y = 7, what is the value of 2x + 5y?$q$, $q$["−17","21","−21","−24"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Solving the system gives x = 2 and y = −5. So 2x + 5y = 2(2) + 5(−5) = −21.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-168$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$At a bakery, 4 muffins and 3 croissants cost $306 total, while 5 muffins and 3 croissants cost $342 total. How much more does one muffin cost than one croissant, in dollars?$q$, $q$["−18","−14","−20","18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let x be the muffin price and y the croissant price. Solving 4x + 3y = 306 and 5x + 3y = 342 gives x = 36 and y = 54, so the muffin costs −18 dollars more.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-169$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x = 2y and x + y = 24, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["8","16","12","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute x = 2y into x + y = 24: 2y + y = 24 → 3y = 24 → y = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-170$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$For what value of k does the system of equations 3x + 6y = 14 and 9x + ky = 44 have no solution?$q$, $q$["18","15","−18","19"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For no solution, the left sides must be proportional while the right sides are not. Since 9 = 3 · 3, the y-coefficients must match that same scale factor: k = 3 · 6 = 18. (Checking constants: 3 · 14 = 42 ≠ 44, confirming no solution.)$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-171$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x + y = 16 and 2x − y = 9, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["1","5","6","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (3x + y) + (2x − y) = 16 + 9 → 5x = 25 → x = 5, so y = 16 − 3(5) = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-172$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x − y = 9 and x + y = 21, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["6","15","9","21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (x − y) + (x + y) = 9 + 21 → 2x = 30 → x = 15, so y = 21 − 15 = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-173$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line given by y = 7x − 2?$q$, $q$["7","−2","2","1/7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In the form y = mx + b, the slope is m = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-174$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line has a slope of 5 and passes through the point (0, −6). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = 5x − 6","y = 5x + 6","y = −6x + 5","y = 5x"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using slope-intercept form y = mx + b with m = 5 and y-intercept b = −6: y = 5x − 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-175$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line passes through the points (0, −4) and (2, 6). What is the slope of the line?$q$, $q$["5","10","2","1/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (6 − (−4))/(2 − 0) = 10/2 = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-176$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear, with f(0) = 5 and f(3) = 17. What is the value of f(7)?$q$, $q$["33","28","37","21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (17 − 5)/3 = 4, so f(x) = 4x + 5. Then f(7) = 4(7) + 5 = 33.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-177$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has a slope of −4 and passes through (2, 3). What is the value of y when x = 5?$q$, $q$["−9","−17","9","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$y − 3 = −4(x − 2) → y = −4x + 11. At x = 5: y = −20 + 11 = −9.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-178$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line given by 5x + 2y = 16?$q$, $q$["−5/2","5/2","2/5","−2/5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving for y: 2y = −5x + 16 → y = (−5/2)x + 8, so the slope is −5/2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-179$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The linear function g satisfies g(1) = 4 and g(4) = 13. What is the value of g(0)?$q$, $q$["1","4","3","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (13 − 4)/(4 − 1) = 3, so g(x) = 3x + b. Using g(1) = 4: 3 + b = 4 → b = 1. Then g(0) = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-180$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear. If f(4) = −14 and f(10) = −32, what is the value of f(−7) − f(2)?$q$, $q$["−27","28","27","31"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Slope = (−32 − −14)/(10 − 4) = −3. Since f(4) = −14, f(x) = −3x − 2. So f(−7) = 19 and f(2) = −8, giving a difference of 27.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-181$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Line k has slope −4 and passes through (2, −4). Line k also passes through (−1, y). What is the value of y minus the y-intercept of line k?$q$, $q$["−4","4","6","5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Using y = −4x + b through (2, −4): −4 = −4(2) + b, so b = 4. At x = −1, y = −4(−1) + 4 = 8. So y − b = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-182$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Function f is linear with f(x) = −x + 7. Function g is linear with g(0) = 35 and a rate of change of 1. For what value of x does g(x) − f(x) = 42?$q$, $q$["9","8","−7","7"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$g(x) = 35 + x. So g(x) − f(x) = (1 − −1)x + (35 − 7) = 2x + 28. Setting this equal to 42 gives x = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-183$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line passes through the points (3, 7) and (6, 16). What is the slope of the line?$q$, $q$["3","9","1/3","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (16 − 7)/(6 − 3) = 9/3 = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-184$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line given by y = 3x − 9?$q$, $q$["3","−9","9","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Set y = 0: 0 = 3x − 9 → x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-185$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 8x − 5 = 27, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4","32","22","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$8x − 5 = 27 → 8x = 32 → x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-186$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x + 14 = 2x + 20, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["6","34","−6","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3x + 14 = 2x + 20 → x = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-187$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If x/5 + 2 = 9, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["35","7","45","14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x/5 + 2 = 9 → x/5 = 7 → x = 35.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-188$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2(x − 5) = 3x − 7, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["−3","3","−17","17"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2(x − 5) = 3x − 7 → 2x − 10 = 3x − 7 → −10 + 7 = 3x − 2x → x = −3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-189$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 4(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) = 20, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["9","15","20","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4(x + 2) − 3(x − 1) = 20 → 4x + 8 − 3x + 3 = 20 → x + 11 = 20 → x = 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-190$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A moving van rental costs $35 plus $0.45 per mile driven. If the total cost was $107, how many miles were driven?$q$, $q$["160","72","196","35"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$35 + 0.45m = 107 → 0.45m = 72 → m = 160.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-191$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If 4x − 11 = 2(x + 2) − 3, what is the value of 4x − 1?$q$, $q$["23","6","25","−23"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Expand both sides: 4x − 11 = 2x + 4 − 3. Collecting terms gives (2)x = 12, so x = 6. Then 4x − 1 = 4(6) − 1 = 23.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-192$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If 5(x − 5) + 8 = 4(x − 5) + 11, what is the value of 2x + 4?$q$, $q$["−20","8","22","20"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Expand both sides: 5x − 25 + 8 = 4x − 20 + 11. Collecting terms gives (1)x = 8, so x = 8. Then 2x + 4 = 2(8) + 4 = 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-193$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 7 − 3x = −8, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","−5","1/5","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$7 − 3x = −8 → −3x = −15 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-194$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 5x − 3 = 3x + 11, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["7","4","−7","14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5x − 3 = 3x + 11 → 2x = 14 → x = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-195$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 6x − 7 > 29?$q$, $q$["7","6","36","43"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$6x − 7 > 29 → 6x > 36 → x > 6, so the smallest integer satisfying this is 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-196$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the largest integer value of x that satisfies 3x + 10 < 31?$q$, $q$["6","7","21","41"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3x + 10 < 31 → 3x < 21 → x < 7, so the largest integer satisfying this is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-197$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest value of x that satisfies 4x − 9 ≥ −1?$q$, $q$["2","−2","8","−8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4x − 9 ≥ −1 → 4x ≥ 8 → x ≥ 2, so the smallest value satisfying this is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-198$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A food truck charges a $20 setup fee plus $6 per meal for catering. What is the minimum number of whole meals needed for the total charge to exceed $100?$q$, $q$["14","13","16","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$20 + 6m > 100 → 6m > 80 → m > 13.33, so the minimum whole number of meals is 14.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-199$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −7 < 2x − 3 ≤ 9?$q$, $q$["6","5","−2","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−7 < 2x − 3 ≤ 9 → −4 < 2x ≤ 12 → −2 < x ≤ 6, so the greatest integer value is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-200$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the largest integer value of x that satisfies −2x + 5 > −9?$q$, $q$["6","7","−6","14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−2x + 5 > −9 → −2x > −14 → x < 7 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number), so the largest integer value is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-201$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A kayak rental charges $10 for the first hour and $4 for each additional hour. What is the maximum number of whole hours a renter can use the kayak without exceeding $34?$q$, $q$["7","6","8","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$10 + 4(h − 1) ≤ 34 → 4(h − 1) ≤ 24 → h − 1 ≤ 6 → h ≤ 7, so the maximum is 7 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-202$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$How many integer values of x satisfy −10 ≤ x and −3(x + 2) ≥ 10 + x?$q$, $q$["8","7","6","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Simplifying the inequality gives x ≤ −4 (the inequality flips because the x-coefficient after combining is negative). Combined with x ≥ −10, the integers from −10 to −4 work, which is 7 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-203$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If x satisfies −2(x − 5) ≥ 22 + 4x, what is the largest possible value of 5x?$q$, $q$["−10","−11","10","−9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving gives x ≤ −2 (the inequality flips on division by a negative coefficient), so the greatest integer value of x is −2, making the largest value of 5x equal to −10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-204$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line given by the equation y = 6x − 18?$q$, $q$["3","−18","18","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The x-intercept occurs where y = 0: 0 = 6x − 18 → x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-205$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the y-intercept of the line given by the equation 3x + y = −5?$q$, $q$["−5","5","3","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The y-intercept occurs where x = 0: y = −5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-206$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 4x + 5y = 40 is graphed in the xy-plane. What is the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["8","40","5","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The y-intercept occurs where x = 0: 5y = 40 → y = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-207$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x + 2y = 22 and y = 5, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4","6","3","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting y = 5: 3x + 10 = 22 → 3x = 12 → x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-208$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The formula for the lateral surface area of a cylinder is A = 2πrh. Which expression gives h in terms of A, r, and π?$q$, $q$["A/(2πr)","2πrA","A − 2πr","2πr/A"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A = 2πrh → h = A/(2πr).$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-209$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has x-intercept 6 and y-intercept 2. What is the y-coordinate of the point on this line where x = −5?$q$, $q$["3.667","4.667","−3.667","5.667"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The line through (6, 0) and (0, 2) has equation 2x + 6y = 12. Substituting x = −5: 2(−5) + 6y = 12, so y = 3.667.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-210$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 11x + 7y = −4 is equivalent to y = mx + k, where m and k are constants. What is the value of m?$q$, $q$["−7/11","−11/7","7/11","11/7"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Solving for y: 7y = −11x − 4, so y = (−11/7)x − 4/7. Thus m = −11/7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-211$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y ≤ 5 − x?$q$, $q$["(1, 2)","(3, 4)","(0, 6)","(5, 1)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (1, 2): 2 ≤ 5 − 1 = 4, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when substituted.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-212$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + 2y ≤ 10 and x = 2, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["4","8","5","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 2: 2 + 2y ≤ 10 → 2y ≤ 8 → y ≤ 4, so the maximum value of y is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-213$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If 3x − 2y ≥ −4 and x = −2, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["−1","1","−2","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = −2: −6 − 2y ≥ −4 → −2y ≥ 2 → y ≤ −1 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number), so the maximum value of y is −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-214$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which of the following ordered pairs is NOT a solution to the inequality 6x − y > 18?$q$, $q$["(3, 2)","(4, 0)","(5, 1)","(10, 5)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Testing (3, 2): 6(3) − 2 = 16, which is not greater than 18, so (3, 2) is not a solution. The other points each satisfy the inequality.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-215$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If y > 3x − 7 and x = 3, what is the smallest integer value of y that satisfies the inequality?$q$, $q$["3","2","9","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 3: y > 3(3) − 7 = 2, so the smallest integer value of y greater than 2 is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-216$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The point (−1, −4) lies on the boundary line of 4x + 7y ≤ k. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−32","−36","−31","32"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since (−1, −4) is on the boundary line 4x + 7y = k, substituting gives k = 4(−1) + 7(−4) = −32.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-217$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A florist sells single roses for $3.50 each. If Mia spends $46 total on roses and an $11 vase, how many roses did she buy?$q$, $q$["10","13","16","35"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3.5r + 11 = 46 → 3.5r = 35 → r = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-218$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A swimming pool initially holds 300 gallons and is being filled at a rate of 25 gallons per minute. How many minutes will it take for the pool to reach 650 gallons?$q$, $q$["14","26","13","38"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$300 + 25m = 650 → 25m = 350 → m = 14.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-219$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A rectangular patio has a perimeter of 46 feet. The length is 5 feet more than the width. What is the width of the patio, in feet?$q$, $q$["9","14","18","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Perimeter: 2(l + w) = 46 → l + w = 23. Since l = w + 5: (w + 5) + w = 23 → 2w = 18 → w = 9.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-220$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$The sum of three consecutive even integers is 126. What is the largest of the three integers?$q$, $q$["44","42","40","46"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let the integers be n, n + 2, and n + 4. Then n + (n + 2) + (n + 4) = 126 → 3n + 6 = 126 → n = 40, so the largest integer is 40 + 4 = 44.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-221$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A food stand sold a combination of $3 tacos and $5 burritos, selling 70 items total for $278. How many tacos were sold?$q$, $q$["36","34","40","30"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let t + b = 70 and 3t + 5b = 278. Substituting b = 70 − t: 3t + 5(70 − t) = 278 → −2t + 350 = 278 → t = 36.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-222$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$Working alone, machine A fills 6 boxes per hour, and machine B fills 4 boxes per hour. Working together at these rates, how many hours will it take them to fill 80 boxes?$q$, $q$["−8","8","10","6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Together the machines fill 6 + 4 = 10 boxes per hour. To fill 80 boxes takes 80/10 = 8 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-223$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 20 and x − y = 8, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["14","6","12","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (x + y) + (x − y) = 20 + 8 → 2x = 28 → x = 14.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-224$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If y = 4x + 1 and x + y = 16, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["3","13","15","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute y = 4x + 1 into x + y = 16: x + (4x + 1) = 16 → 5x = 15 → x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-225$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2x + y = 13 and y = x − 2, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","3","7","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substitute y = x − 2 into 2x + y = 13: 2x + (x − 2) = 13 → 3x = 15 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-226$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x − y = 11 and x + y = 27, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["8","19","11","27"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (x − y) + (x + y) = 11 + 27 → 2x = 38 → x = 19, so y = 27 − 19 = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-227$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 3x + 2y = 24 and x − y = 3, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["6","3","9","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since x = y + 3, substituting gives 3(y + 3) + 2y = 24 → 5y + 9 = 24 → 5y = 15 → y = 3, so x = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-228$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 4x − y = 10 and 2x + y = 14, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["6","4","2","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (4x − y) + (2x + y) = 10 + 14 → 6x = 24 → x = 4, so y = 14 − 2(4) = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-229$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A music store sells used CDs for $4 and used vinyl records for $9. A customer bought 11 items total for $69. How many vinyl records did the customer buy?$q$, $q$["5","6","4","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let c + v = 11 and 4c + 9v = 69. Substituting c = 11 − v: 4(11 − v) + 9v = 69 → 44 + 5v = 69 → v = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-230$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If 5x + 3y = 28 and 2x − 3y = 7, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","3","4","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (5x + 3y) + (2x − 3y) = 28 + 7 → 7x = 35 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-231$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A bowling alley charges a $4 shoe rental fee plus $5 per game, while a rival alley charges no shoe rental but $7 per game. For how many games is the total cost the same at both alleys?$q$, $q$["2","4","7","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4 + 5g = 7g → 4 = 2g → g = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-232$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations 3x + 7y = 17 and 9x + ky = 51 has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["−21","21","24","17"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$For infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be a constant multiple of the first. Since 9/3 = 3 and 51/17 = 3, the y-coefficient must follow the same scale factor: k = 3 · 7 = 21.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-233$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 6x + 3y = 15 and 5x + 5y = 10, what is the value of 5x + 5y?$q$, $q$["−10","10","12","8"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Solving the system gives x = 3 and y = −1. So 5x + 5y = 5(3) + 5(−1) = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-234$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$At a bakery, 6 muffins and 6 croissants cost $396 total, while 4 muffins and 2 croissants cost $234 total. How much more does one muffin cost than one croissant, in dollars?$q$, $q$["37","33","36","−36"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Let x be the muffin price and y the croissant price. Solving 6x + 6y = 396 and 4x + 2y = 234 gives x = 51 and y = 15, so the muffin costs 36 dollars more.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-235$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line given by y = 6x − 11?$q$, $q$["6","−11","11","1/6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$In the form y = mx + b, the slope is m = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-236$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line has a slope of −6 and passes through the point (0, 2). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = −6x + 2","y = 6x + 2","y = −6x − 2","y = 2x − 6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Using slope-intercept form y = mx + b with m = −6 and y-intercept b = 2: y = −6x + 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-237$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$A line passes through the points (0, 4) and (3, −5). What is the slope of the line?$q$, $q$["−3","3","−9","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (−5 − 4)/(3 − 0) = −9/3 = −3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-238$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line given by y = 8x − 24?$q$, $q$["3","−24","24","8"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Set y = 0: 0 = 8x − 24 → x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-239$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear, with f(0) = −6 and f(4) = 10. What is the value of f(9)?$q$, $q$["30","34","26","22"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (10 − (−6))/4 = 4, so f(x) = 4x − 6. Then f(9) = 4(9) − 6 = 30.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-240$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has a slope of −4 and passes through (3, 6). What is the value of y when x = 7?$q$, $q$["−10","−16","10","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$y − 6 = −4(x − 3) → y = −4x + 18. At x = 7: y = −28 + 18 = −10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-241$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line given by 7x + 3y = 21?$q$, $q$["−7/3","7/3","3/7","−3/7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving for y: 3y = −7x + 21 → y = (−7/3)x + 7, so the slope is −7/3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-242$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The linear function g satisfies g(3) = 8 and g(7) = 20. What is the value of g(0)?$q$, $q$["−1","1","−4","4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (20 − 8)/(7 − 3) = 3, so g(x) = 3x + b. Using g(3) = 8: 9 + b = 8 → b = −1. Then g(0) = −1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-243$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A line passes through (−3, 5) and (1, −7). What is the equation of the line?$q$, $q$["y = −3x − 4","y = −3x + 4","y = 3x − 4","y = −3x − 14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (−7 − 5)/(1 − (−3)) = −12/4 = −3. Using point (−3, 5): y − 5 = −3(x + 3) → y = −3x − 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-244$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function h is linear, with h(−6) = 12 and h(7) = −14. What is the x-intercept of the graph of h?$q$, $q$["1","0","2","3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slope = (h(7) − h(−6))/(7 − −6) = −2. Since h(−6) = 12, h(x) = −2x. Setting h(x) = 0 gives x = 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-245$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The function f is linear. If f(3) = −13 and f(5) = −21, what is the value of f(−9) − f(1)?$q$, $q$["42","40","41","−40"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Slope = (−21 − −13)/(5 − 3) = −4. Since f(3) = −13, f(x) = −4x − 1. So f(−9) = 35 and f(1) = −5, giving a difference of 40.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-246$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Line k has slope 4 and passes through (3, 4). Line k also passes through (−1, y). What is the value of y minus the y-intercept of line k?$q$, $q$["−3","4","−1","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Using y = 4x + b through (3, 4): 4 = 4(3) + b, so b = −8. At x = −1, y = 4(−1) − 8 = −12. So y − b = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-247$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 5x + 8 = 38, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["6","9.2","30","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5x + 8 = 38 → 5x = 30 → x = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-248$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 7x − 10 = 25, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","35","15","−5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$7x − 10 = 25 → 7x = 35 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-249$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If x/6 + 4 = 11, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["42","7","66","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x/6 + 4 = 11 → x/6 = 7 → x = 42.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-250$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3(x − 5) = 2x + 4, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["19","−19","7","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3(x − 5) = 2x + 4 → 3x − 15 = 2x + 4 → x = 19.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-251$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 6(x + 2) − 2(x − 3) = 34, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["4","16","−4","9.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$6(x + 2) − 2(x − 3) = 34 → 6x + 12 − 2x + 6 = 34 → 4x + 18 = 34 → x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-252$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A catering company charges a $75 setup fee plus $18 per guest. If a client's invoice totaled $489, how many guests were served?$q$, $q$["23","22.5","27","414"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$75 + 18g = 489 → 18g = 414 → g = 23.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-253$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 8 − 3(x − 2) = −7, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["7","3","−7","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$8 − 3(x − 2) = −7 → 8 − 3x + 6 = −7 → 14 − 3x = −7 → −3x = −21 → x = 7.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-254$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If 2(x + 3) + 5 = −2(x − 7) + 5, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["−2","2","4","6"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Expand both sides: 2x + 6 + 5 = −2x + 14 + 5. Collecting the x-terms and constants gives (4)x = 8, so x = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-255$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If 4(x − 8) − 4 = 2(x − 3) − 12, what is the value of 2x + 4?$q$, $q$["9","22","24","−22"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Expand both sides: 4x − 32 − 4 = 2x − 6 − 12. Collecting terms gives (2)x = 18, so x = 9. Then 2x + 4 = 2(9) + 4 = 22.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-256$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If |x + 5| = 12, what is the sum of all possible values of x?$q$, $q$["−10","10","17","−7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x + 5 = 12 gives x = 7; x + 5 = −12 gives x = −17. The sum of the two solutions is 7 + (−17) = −10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-257$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest integer value of x that satisfies 7x − 4 > 24?$q$, $q$["5","4","28","24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$7x − 4 > 24 → 7x > 28 → x > 4, so the smallest integer satisfying this is 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-258$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the largest integer value of x that satisfies 4x + 7 < 35?$q$, $q$["6","7","28","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4x + 7 < 35 → 4x < 28 → x < 7, so the largest integer satisfying this is 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-259$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the smallest value of x that satisfies 5x − 9 ≥ 1?$q$, $q$["2","−2","10","−10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5x − 9 ≥ 1 → 5x ≥ 10 → x ≥ 2, so the smallest value satisfying this is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-260$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A photographer charges a $60 session fee plus $15 per edited photo. What is the minimum number of whole edited photos needed for the total cost to exceed $225?$q$, $q$["12","11","15","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$60 + 15n > 225 → 15n > 165 → n > 11, so the minimum whole number of photos is 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-261$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −6 < 4x − 2 ≤ 14?$q$, $q$["4","5","−1","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−6 < 4x − 2 ≤ 14 → −4 < 4x ≤ 16 → −1 < x ≤ 4, so the greatest integer value is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-262$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the largest value of x that satisfies −6x + 5 ≥ −19?$q$, $q$["4","−4","24","−24"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−6x + 5 ≥ −19 → −6x ≥ −24 → x ≤ 4 (the inequality flips when dividing by a negative number), so the largest value satisfying this is 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-263$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A shipping company charges $12 plus $0.80 per pound for a package. What is the maximum whole number of pounds a package can weigh for the shipping cost to stay below $30?$q$, $q$["22","23","18","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$12 + 0.80w < 30 → 0.80w < 18 → w < 22.5, so the maximum whole number of pounds is 22.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-264$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −2(x − 3) ≥ −6 + 4x?$q$, $q$["3","1","2","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Distribute: −2x + 6 ≥ −6 + 4x. Collecting x-terms: −6x ≥ −12. Dividing by the negative coefficient −6 flips the inequality: x ≤ 2. The greatest integer satisfying this is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-265$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$How many integer values of x satisfy 2 ≤ x and −3(x − 6) ≥ −17 + 4x?$q$, $q$["3","5","−4","4"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Simplifying the inequality gives x ≤ 5 (the inequality flips because the x-coefficient after combining is negative). Combined with x ≥ 2, the integers from 2 to 5 work, which is 4 values.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-266$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the x-intercept of the line given by the equation y = 9x − 36?$q$, $q$["4","−36","36","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The x-intercept occurs where y = 0: 0 = 9x − 36 → x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-267$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the y-intercept of the line given by the equation 4x + y = −13?$q$, $q$["−13","13","4","−4"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The y-intercept occurs where x = 0: y = −13.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-268$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 5x + 6y = 30 is graphed in the xy-plane. What is the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["5","30","6","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The y-intercept occurs where x = 0: 6y = 30 → y = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-269$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If 7x − 2y = 26 and x = 4, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["1","2","13","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 4: 7(4) − 2y = 26 → 28 − 2y = 26 → −2y = −2 → y = 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-270$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The total cost of a cab ride is given by C = b + rm, where b is the base fare, r is the rate per mile, and m is the number of miles. Which expression gives m in terms of C, b, and r?$q$, $q$["(C − b)/r","(C + b)/r","C/(br)","(C − r)/b"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$C = b + rm → C − b = rm → m = (C − b)/r.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-271$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation A = p(q + 4r) relates the quantities A, p, q, and r. Which equation correctly gives r in terms of A, p, and q?$q$, $q$["r = (A + pq)/(4p)","r = 4p(A − q)","r = (A − pq)/4","r = (A − pq)/(4p)"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Divide both sides by p: A/p = q + 4r. Subtract q: A/p − q = 4r. Divide by 4: r = (A − pq)/(4p), after combining over a common denominator.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-272$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has x-intercept 10 and y-intercept 8. What is the y-coordinate of the point on this line where x = 2?$q$, $q$["5.4","6.4","−6.4","7.4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The line through (10, 0) and (0, 8) has equation 8x + 10y = 80. Substituting x = 2: 8(2) + 10y = 80, so y = 6.4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-273$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y ≥ 3x − 4?$q$, $q$["(0, 0)","(2, 1)","(3, 2)","(1, −2)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (0, 0): 0 ≥ 3(0) − 4 = −4, which is true. The other points fail the inequality when substituted.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-274$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + 4y ≤ 12 and x = 0, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["3","12","4","48"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 0: 4y ≤ 12 → y ≤ 3, so the maximum value of y is 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-275$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If 6x + 3y ≤ 21 and x = 3, what is the maximum possible value of y?$q$, $q$["1","3","7","−1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 3: 18 + 3y ≤ 21 → 3y ≤ 3 → y ≤ 1, so the maximum value of y is 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-276$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which of the following ordered pairs is NOT a solution to the inequality 5x + 2y ≤ 16?$q$, $q$["(4, 3)","(0, 0)","(2, 1)","(1, 2)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Testing (4, 3): 5(4) + 2(3) = 26, which is not less than or equal to 16, so (4, 3) is not a solution. The other points each satisfy the inequality.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-277$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If y < 4x − 9 and x = 2, what is the largest integer value of y that satisfies the inequality?$q$, $q$["−2","−1","0","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 2: y < 4(2) − 9 = −1, so the largest integer value of y less than −1 is −2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-278$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A point (x, y) satisfies y ≤ −2x + 18 and y ≥ 5x − 21. If x = 3, what is the greatest possible integer value of y?$q$, $q$["−12","12","8","15"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Substituting x = 3 into y ≤ −2x + 18 gives y ≤ 12. The greatest integer satisfying this is 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-279$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$The length of a rectangular mural is 4 feet less than three times its width. If the mural's length is 20 feet, what is its width, in feet?$q$, $q$["8","16","24","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$20 = 3w − 4 → 3w = 24 → w = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-280$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$The sum of two numbers is 56. One number is 6 more than the other. What is the smaller number?$q$, $q$["25","31","28","22"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let the smaller number be x, so the larger is x + 6. Then x + (x + 6) = 56 → 2x = 50 → x = 25.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-281$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A cyclist rides for a total of 3 hours, covering a combined distance of 55 miles, riding at 15 miles per hour for part of the trip and 20 miles per hour for the rest. How many hours did she ride at 20 miles per hour?$q$, $q$["2","1","3","1.5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let t be the hours at 20 mph, so 3 − t hours are at 15 mph. Then 20t + 15(3 − t) = 55 → 5t + 45 = 55 → t = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-282$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A librarian wants the average number of books checked out per day over 5 days to be 42. On the first four days, 38, 45, 40, and 44 books were checked out. How many books must be checked out on the fifth day to reach that average?$q$, $q$["43","42","45","47"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The total needed is 42 × 5 = 210. The first four days total 38 + 45 + 40 + 44 = 167, so the fifth day must be 210 − 167 = 43.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-283$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$For three consecutive integers, 3 times the smallest equals the sum of the other two, minus some number d. If the smallest integer is 17, what is the value of d?$q$, $q$["13","14","19","−14"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$The three consecutive integers are 17, 18, and 19. Their sum of the two larger is 18 + 19 = 37. Since 3 times the smallest is 51, d = 51 − 37 = 14.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-284$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A total of $28,000 is invested in two accounts, one earning 5% annual interest and the other earning 6% annual interest. If the total interest earned in one year is $1660, how much was invested at 5%?$q$, $q$["−2000","2000","2001","1996"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Let x be the amount invested at 5%. Then $28,000 − x is invested at 6%. Solving 0.05x.06(28,000 − x) = 1660 gives x = $2,000.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-285$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A bakery sells muffins for $3 each and cookies for $2 each. Maria bought 5 more cookies than muffins and spent a total of $40. How many muffins did she buy?$q$, $q$["6","5","8","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let m be the number of muffins, so cookies = m + 5. Then 3m + 2(m + 5) = 40 → 5m + 10 = 40 → m = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-286$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$Marcus is 3 times as old as his nephew. In 5 years, Marcus will be 41 years old. How old is his nephew now?$q$, $q$["12","36","9","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Marcus's current age is 41 − 5 = 36. Since Marcus is 3 times his nephew's age, the nephew is 36/3 = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-287$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$A gym charges a one-time enrollment fee of $40 plus $35 per month. If Tasha has spent a total of $390 so far, how many months has she been a member?$q$, $q$["10","9","11","14"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$40 + 35m = 390 → 35m = 350 → m = 10.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-288$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 1, null, $q$The perimeter of a rectangular garden is 84 feet. The length is 6 feet more than the width. What is the width, in feet?$q$, $q$["18","24","21","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Since length = width + 6, (w + 6) + w = 42 → 2w = 36 → w = 18.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-289$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A chemist mixes a solution that is 20% acid with a solution that is 50% acid to create 12 liters of a solution that is 30% acid. How many liters of the 20% solution are used?$q$, $q$["8","4","6","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let x be the liters of the 20% solution: 0.2x + 0.5(12 − x) = 0.3(12) → −0.3x + 6 = 3.6 → x = 8.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-290$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A pipe can fill a pool in 10 hours, and a second pipe can fill the same pool in 15 hours. If both pipes run together, how many hours will it take to fill the pool?$q$, $q$["6","12.5","5","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Combined rate = 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/6 of the pool per hour, so filling the pool takes 6 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-291$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$Two trains leave the same station at the same time traveling in opposite directions. One travels at 55 mph and the other at 65 mph. After how many hours will they be 360 miles apart?$q$, $q$["3","2.5","4","6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The trains separate at a combined rate of 55 + 65 = 120 mph, so 120t = 360 → t = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-292$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A jar contains only dimes and quarters worth a total of $6.75. There are 15 more dimes than quarters. How many quarters are in the jar?$q$, $q$["15","30","12","18"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let q be the number of quarters, so dimes = q + 15. Then 0.10(q + 15) + 0.25q = 6.75 → 0.35q + 1.5 = 6.75 → q = 15.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-293$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A theater sold 200 tickets for a play, some at $18 for adults and the rest at $10 for students, collecting $3,040 in total. How many adult tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["130","70","120","140"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let a be the number of adult tickets, so student tickets = 200 − a. Then 18a + 10(200 − a) = 3,040 → 8a = 1,040 → a = 130.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-294$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A chemist mixes 85 liters of solution by combining a 25% acid solution with a 66% acid solution. If 35 liters of the 66% solution is used, and the resulting mixture is 41.882% acid, how many liters of the 25% solution were used?$q$, $q$["−50","54","48","50"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let x be the liters of the 25% solution. Since 35 liters of 66% solution is also used, 0.25x.66(35) = 0.4188235294117647(x + 35). Solving gives x = 50 liters.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-295$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$Working alone, machine A fills 4 boxes per hour, and machine B fills 6 boxes per hour. Working together at these rates, how many hours will it take them to fill 20 boxes?$q$, $q$["0","4","2","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Together the machines fill 4 + 6 = 10 boxes per hour. To fill 20 boxes takes 20/10 = 2 hours.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-296$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$For three consecutive integers, 3 times the smallest equals the sum of the other two, minus some number d. If the smallest integer is 21, what is the value of d?$q$, $q$["22","17","18","−18"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$The three consecutive integers are 21, 22, and 23. Their sum of the two larger is 22 + 23 = 45. Since 3 times the smallest is 63, d = 63 − 45 = 18.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-297$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$A total of $5,000 is invested in two accounts, one earning 2% annual interest and the other earning 4% annual interest. If the total interest earned in one year is $100, how much was invested at 2%?$q$, $q$["5002","−5000","4999","5000"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Let x be the amount invested at 2%. Then $5,000 − x is invested at 4%. Solving 0.02x.04(5,000 − x) = 100 gives x = $5,000.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-298$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Word problems$q$, 2, null, $q$The sum of three consecutive even integers is 126. What is the largest of the three integers?$q$, $q$["44","42","40","46"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let the integers be n, n + 2, and n + 4: 3n + 6 = 126 → n = 40, so the largest integer is 40 + 4 = 44.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-299$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$Which ordered pair is a solution to the inequality y > −2x + 5?$q$, $q$["(3, 2)","(4, −4)","(2, 1)","(5, −6)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting (3, 2): 2 > −2(3) + 5 = −1, which is true. The other points do not satisfy the inequality.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-300$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If 5x + 2y ≤ 20 and x = 2, what is the maximum integer value of y?$q$, $q$["5","4","6","10"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 2: 10 + 2y ≤ 20 → y ≤ 5, so the maximum integer value is 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-301$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$Which ordered pair is NOT a solution to the inequality y ≥ x − 4?$q$, $q$["(0, −5)","(0, 0)","(4, 0)","(−2, −3)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For (0, −5): −5 ≥ 0 − 4 = −4 is false, so this point is not a solution. The other points each satisfy the inequality.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-302$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The graph of the inequality y < (1/2)x − 3 is shown in the xy-plane. Which point lies in the solution region?$q$, $q$["(−2, −6)","(2, 0)","(0, −2)","(4, −1)"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting (−2, −6): −6 < (1/2)(−2) − 3 = −4, which is true. The other points fail the inequality.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-303$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A store requires that the number of chairs c and tables t it sells satisfy 25t + 8c ≥ 400 to cover costs. If the store sells 10 tables, what is the minimum number of chairs it must sell?$q$, $q$["19","18","20","15"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$With t = 10: 250 + 8c ≥ 400 → 8c ≥ 150 → c ≥ 18.75, so the minimum whole number of chairs is 19.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-304$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$If 3x − 4y < 12, which of the following must be true when x = 0?$q$, $q$["y > −3","y < −3","y > 3","y < 3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = 0: −4y < 12 → y > −3 (dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality).$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-305$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which inequality is graphed by a dashed boundary line with the region above the line y = −x + 6 shaded?$q$, $q$["y > −x + 6","y ≥ −x + 6","y < −x + 6","y ≤ −x + 6"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$A dashed line means points on the line are not included (a strict inequality), and shading above the line corresponds to y > −x + 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-306$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which of the following ordered pairs is NOT a solution to the inequality 5x − 9y > 17?$q$, $q$["(8, −3)","(3, −17)","(5, 2)","(6, −13)"]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$Testing each point in 5x − 9y > 17: only (5, 2) gives a value that is not greater than 17, so it is not a solution.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-307$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The point (6, 1) lies on the boundary line of 8x + 7y ≤ k. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["58","−55","54","55"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Since (6, 1) is on the boundary line 8x + 7y = k, substituting gives k = 8(6) + 7(1) = 55.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-308$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A point (x, y) satisfies y ≤ −2x + 20 and y ≥ 5x − 27. If x = 0, what is the greatest possible integer value of y?$q$, $q$["−20","19","24","20"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Substituting x = 0 into y ≤ −2x + 20 gives y ≤ 20. The greatest integer satisfying this is 20.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-309$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If y ≥ 4x − 7 and x = −1, what is the smallest integer value of y that satisfies the inequality?$q$, $q$["−11","−10","−12","11"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Substituting x = −1: y ≥ 4(−1) − 7 = −11, so the smallest integer value satisfying the inequality is −11.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-310$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A farmer has at most 60 hours per week to spend between tending crops and tending animals. Tending crops takes 3 hours per acre, and tending animals takes 5 hours per animal. If the farmer tends 8 acres of crops, what is the maximum number of animals he can tend?$q$, $q$["7","8","6","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Tending 8 acres takes 3(8) = 24 hours, leaving 60 − 24 = 36 hours. Since 36/5 = 7.2, the farmer can tend at most 7 animals.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-311$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which of the following ordered pairs is NOT a solution to the inequality 9x − 7y > 14?$q$, $q$["(−1, −6)","(−6, −16)","(7, −5)","(−6, −8)"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Testing each point in 9x − 7y > 14: only (−6, −8) gives a value that is not greater than 14, so it is not a solution.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-312$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$The equation 3x + 4y = 24 is graphed in the xy-plane. What is the y-intercept of the graph?$q$, $q$["6","8","24","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Setting x = 0: 4y = 24 → y = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-313$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the slope of the line represented by the equation 6x − 3y = 9?$q$, $q$["2","−2","3","1/2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving for y: −3y = −6x + 9 → y = 2x − 3, so the slope is 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-314$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If 2x − y = 7, what is the value of y when x = 5?$q$, $q$["3","5","17","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2(5) − y = 7 → 10 − y = 7 → y = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-315$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Which equation represents a line that is parallel to 4x + 2y = 10 and passes through the point (0, 3)?$q$, $q$["y = −2x + 3","y = 2x + 3","y = −2x + 5","y = (1/2)x + 3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The given line has slope −2 (from y = −2x + 5). A parallel line through (0, 3) has the same slope: y = −2x + 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-316$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation of a line is 5x + 3y = 30. What is the x-coordinate of the point where this line crosses the x-axis?$q$, $q$["6","10","30","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Setting y = 0: 5x = 30 → x = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-317$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$Line ℓ passes through the points (−2, 7) and (3, −3). Which equation represents line ℓ?$q$, $q$["y = −2x + 3","y = −2x − 3","y = 2x + 3","y = −2x + 7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (−3 − 7)/(3 − (−2)) = −2. Using (3, −3): y − (−3) = −2(x − 3) → y = −2x + 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-318$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If the point (k, 4) lies on the line 3x + 2y = 26, what is the value of k?$q$, $q$["6","8","4","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3k + 2(4) = 26 → 3k = 18 → k = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-319$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation 8x + 2y = 11 is equivalent to y = mx + k, where m and k are constants. What is the value of m?$q$, $q$["−1/4","−4","1/4","4"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Solving for y: 2y = −8x + 11, so y = (−8/2)x + 11/2. Thus m = −4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-320$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$The equation A = p(q + 4r) relates the quantities A, p, q, and r. Which equation correctly gives r in terms of A, p, and q?$q$, $q$["r = 4p(A − q)","r = (A − pq)/(4p)","r = (A − pq)/4","r = (A + pq)/(4p)"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Divide both sides by p: A/p = q + 4r. Subtract q: A/p − q = 4r. Divide by 4: r = (A − pq)/(4p), after combining over a common denominator.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-321$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has x-intercept 2 and y-intercept 4. What is the y-coordinate of the point on this line where x = 5?$q$, $q$["−4","−5","6","−6"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$The line through (2, 0) and (0, 4) has equation 4x + 2y = 8. Substituting x = 5: 4(5) + 2y = 8, so y = −6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-322$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 2, null, $q$A line has a y-intercept of 8 and passes through the point (6, −4). What is the x-intercept of the line?$q$, $q$["4","6","8","2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (−4 − 8)/(6 − 0) = −2, so the equation is y = −2x + 8. Setting y = 0: x = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-323$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in two variables$q$, 1, null, $q$If 7x + 2y = 18 and x = 2, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["2","4","7","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$7(2) + 2y = 18 → 14 + 2y = 18 → y = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-324$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$What is the largest integer value of x that satisfies 2x + 7 < 19?$q$, $q$["5","6","4","12"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$2x + 7 < 19 → 2x < 12 → x < 6, so the largest integer satisfying this is 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-325$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$Solve for x: −3x + 8 ≥ 2.$q$, $q$["x ≤ 2","x ≥ 2","x ≤ −2","x ≥ −2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$−3x + 8 ≥ 2 → −3x ≥ −6 → x ≤ 2 (dividing by a negative number reverses the inequality).$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-326$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$A delivery service charges $4 plus $0.75 per mile. What is the minimum whole number of miles that would make the total charge at least $25?$q$, $q$["28","27","29","21"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$4 + 0.75m ≥ 25 → 0.75m ≥ 21 → m ≥ 28, so the minimum whole number of miles is 28.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-327$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$For what values of x is the inequality 5 − 2x ≤ 3x − 10 true?$q$, $q$["x ≥ 3","x ≤ 3","x ≥ −3","x ≤ −3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$5 − 2x ≤ 3x − 10 → 15 ≤ 5x → x ≥ 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-328$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A student needs an average of at least 85 across 4 tests to earn an A. Her first three scores are 80, 88, and 82. What is the minimum score she needs on the fourth test?$q$, $q$["90","85","92","88"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$The total needed is 85 × 4 = 340. The first three scores total 80 + 88 + 82 = 250, so the fourth score must be at least 340 − 250 = 90.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-329$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the solution set to the compound inequality −1 ≤ 2x + 5 < 9?$q$, $q$["−3 ≤ x < 2","−3 < x ≤ 2","−6 ≤ x < 4","3 ≤ x < −2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Subtract 5 from all parts: −6 ≤ 2x < 4. Dividing by 2: −3 ≤ x < 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-330$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If x satisfies −3(x − 1) ≥ 3 + 2x, what is the largest possible value of 2x?$q$, $q$["0","−1","1","−2"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Solving gives x ≤ 0 (the inequality flips on division by a negative coefficient), so the greatest integer value of x is 0, making the largest value of 2x equal to 0.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-331$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear inequalities in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$What is the greatest integer value of x that satisfies −3(x + 5) ≥ −19 + x?$q$, $q$["0","−1","2","1"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Distribute: −3x − 15 ≥ −19 + x. Collecting x-terms: −4x ≥ −4. Dividing by the negative coefficient −4 flips the inequality: x ≤ 1. The greatest integer satisfying this is 1.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-332$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 1, null, $q$If x + y = 12 and 2x − y = 3, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","7","3","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Adding the equations: (x + y) + (2x − y) = 12 + 3 → 3x = 15 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-333$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$A total of 140 tickets were sold for a concert, some at $25 and some at $40, generating $4,250 in revenue. How many $40 tickets were sold?$q$, $q$["50","90","60","40"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let b be the number of $40 tickets, so $25 tickets = 140 − b. Then 25(140 − b) + 40b = 4,250 → 3,500 + 15b = 4,250 → b = 50.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-334$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$If 2x + y = 11 and x − 3y = −5, what is the value of y?$q$, $q$["3","4","11","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$From 2x + y = 11, y = 11 − 2x. Substituting into x − 3y = −5: x − 3(11 − 2x) = −5 → 7x = 28 → x = 4, so y = 11 − 8 = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-335$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$For what value of k does the system of equations x + y = 1 and 4x + ky = 6 have no solution?$q$, $q$["4","−4","5","0"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For no solution, the left sides must be proportional while the right sides are not. Since 4 = 4 · 1, the y-coefficients must match that same scale factor: k = 4 · 1 = 4. (Checking constants: 4 · 1 = 4 ≠ 6, confirming no solution.)$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-336$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Systems of two linear equations$q$, 2, null, $q$The system of equations 6x + y = 1 and 24x + ky = 4 has infinitely many solutions. What is the value of k?$q$, $q$["4","−4","5","1"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$For infinitely many solutions, the second equation must be a constant multiple of the first. Since 24/6 = 4 and 4/1 = 4, the y-coefficient must follow the same scale factor: k = 4 · 1 = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-337$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 1, null, $q$The function g is linear, with g(1) = 5 and g(3) = 11. What is the slope of g?$q$, $q$["3","6","2","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (11 − 5)/(3 − 1) = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-338$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$A candle is 12 inches tall and burns down at a constant rate of 0.4 inch per hour. After how many hours will the candle be 4.8 inches tall?$q$, $q$["18","16","19.2","20"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$12 − 0.4h = 4.8 → 0.4h = 7.2 → h = 18.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-339$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$The linear function h satisfies h(−2) = 10 and h(4) = −8. What is the value of h(0)?$q$, $q$["4","−8","10","−3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Slope = (−8 − 10)/(4 − (−2)) = −3. Using (4, −8): −8 = −3(4) + b → b = 4, so h(0) = 4.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-340$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear functions$q$, 2, null, $q$Function f is linear with f(x) = 3x − 8. Function g is linear with g(0) = 18 and a rate of change of 4. For what value of x does g(x) − f(x) = 28?$q$, $q$["6","2","−2","3"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$g(x) = 18 + 4x. So g(x) − f(x) = (4 − 3)x + (18 − −8) = x + 26. Setting this equal to 28 gives x = 2.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-341$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 7x + 3 = 4x + 18, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["5","3","15","7"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$7x + 3 = 4x + 18 → 3x = 15 → x = 5.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-342$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If (x/4) + 5 = 11, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["24","6","44","16"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$x/4 + 5 = 11 → x/4 = 6 → x = 24.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-343$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$A rope 75 inches long is cut into two pieces so that one piece is 9 inches longer than twice the other. What is the length, in inches, of the shorter piece?$q$, $q$["22","53","33","25"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Let the shorter piece be x, so the longer piece is 2x + 9. Then x + (2x + 9) = 75 → 3x = 66 → x = 22.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-344$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 0.25(x − 8) = 0.5x − 5, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["12","6","−12","3"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$0.25x − 2 = 0.5x − 5 → 3 = 0.25x → x = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-345$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If (2x − 3)/5 = (x + 4)/3, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["29","9","−29","23"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$Cross-multiplying: 3(2x − 3) = 5(x + 4) → 6x − 9 = 5x + 20 → x = 29.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-346$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If −4(x − 3) + 9 = 2(x + 5) − 7, what is the value of 4x?$q$, $q$["3","−12","10","12"]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$Expand both sides: −4x + 12 + 9 = 2x + 10 − 7. Collecting terms gives (−6)x = −18, so x = 3. Then 4x = 4(3) = 12.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-347$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 2, null, $q$If -(x − 6) − 7 = −6(x − 2) + 2, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["−3","3","7","5"]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$Expand both sides: −x + 6 − 7 = −6x + 12 + 2. Collecting the x-terms and constants gives (5)x = 15, so x = 3.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-348$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 6x + 9 = 45, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["6","9","36","5"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$6x + 9 = 45 → 6x = 36 → x = 6.$q$, null),
  ($q$alg-349$q$, $q$math$q$, $q$Algebra$q$, $q$Linear equations in one variable$q$, 1, null, $q$If 3x − 4 = 2x + 9, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$["13","5","−13","9"]$q$::jsonb, 0, $q$3x − 4 = 2x + 9 → x = 13.$q$, null),
